= Lie theory
{c}
{wiki}

<Lie theory> studies continuous symmetries through <Lie groups>, <Lie algebras>, and their <representation theory>[representations].

= Affine algebraic group
{parent=Lie theory}
{wiki=Linear_algebraic_group}

= Linear algebraic group
{synonym}

An affine algebraic group is an <affine variety> $G$ whose multiplication and inversion are <regular maps>. Equivalently, its <coordinate ring> $k[G]$ is a commutative <Hopf algebra>, with comultiplication induced by multiplication in $G$.

= Algebraic one-parameter subgroup
{parent=Affine algebraic group}
{title2=$\lambda:\mathbb G_m\to G$}

An algebraic one-parameter subgroup is a regular group homomorphism from the multiplicative algebraic group. In $SL_n$, a change of basis makes it $t\mapsto\operatorname{diag}(t^{a_1},\ldots,t^{a_n})$ for integers with sum zero. This is the algebraic multiplicative notion, distinct from an additive real-parameter subgroup of a Lie group.

= Geometrically reductive algebraic group
{parent=Affine algebraic group}

For every finite-dimensional <rational representation> $V$ and every nonzero $v\in V^G$, a geometrically reductive algebraic group admits a homogeneous invariant polynomial $F\in k[V]^G$ of positive degree with $F(v)\ne0$. A linearly reductive group can take degree one. In positive characteristic, higher degree is essential: a power $p^r$ can separate a fixed vector even when an invariant linear functional cannot.

= Determinant separation for positive-characteristic SL2
{parent=Geometrically reductive algebraic group}
{title2=$q=p^r,\quad\det:\operatorname{End}(\operatorname{Sym}^{q-1}k^2)\to k$}

Choose a torus-invariant functional nonzero on a fixed vector, and map the representation to right-torus-invariant matrix coefficients on $SL_2$. These functions lie in a finite filtration by polynomials of bidegree $(n,n)$ in the two matrix columns. For $n=q-1$, all binomial coefficients are nonzero in characteristic $p$, so the invariant tensor given by the determinant to power $n$ induces an invertible map from the symmetric power's dual to itself. The filtration piece becomes its endomorphism space, with the constant function corresponding to the identity. The endomorphism determinant is an invariant degree-$q$ polynomial equal to one there; composing it with the original representation map proves geometric reductivity.

= Linearly reductive algebraic group
{parent=Affine algebraic group}

An <affine algebraic group> is linearly reductive when every finite-dimensional <rational representation> is completely reducible. Equivalently its invariants functor is exact. Over a field of characteristic zero, <reductive algebraic groups> are linearly reductive; in positive characteristic this stronger property fails for many reductive groups. An <algebraic torus> is linearly reductive in every characteristic because its rational representations decompose into character spaces.

= Rational structure on an algebraic group
{parent=Affine algebraic group}

A rational structure over $\mathbb F_q$ is a model over that <finite field> together with its identification after extension to $\overline{\mathbb F}_q$. It determines a <Frobenius endomorphism of an algebraic group>, whose fixed points are the rational points. Conjugating a standard Frobenius by an <algebraic group> automorphism transports the rational structure, even if the automorphism is not itself rational for the original structure.

= Frobenius endomorphism of an algebraic group
{c}
{parent=Rational structure on an algebraic group}
{title2=$F:G\to G$}

= Frobenius endomorphisms of an algebraic group
{c}
{synonym}

For an <algebraic group> defined over $\mathbb F_q$, its Frobenius morphism acts as entrywise $q$th powers in a standard matrix realization. Its differential is zero, and its fixed-point group is finite. A conjugate $F'=\varphi F\varphi^{-1}$ defines an isomorphic rational structure. This group morphism must be distinguished from a field <Frobenius endomorphism> alone and from the convention called geometric Frobenius in Galois theory, which is inverse to the arithmetic generator on field points.

= Lang theorem for algebraic groups
{c}
{parent=Frobenius endomorphism of an algebraic group}

For a connected smooth <algebraic group> and a finite-field Frobenius $F$, the <Lang map> $g\mapsto g^{-1}F(g)$ is surjective. One proof uses the twisted action $x\cdot y=xyF(x)^{-1}$. Since $dF=0$, every orbit map has surjective differential, so each orbit is open; connectedness leaves only one orbit. The same differential calculation makes the Lang map étale, with finite fibres equal to left cosets of $G^F$.

= Lang map
{c}
{parent=Lang theorem for algebraic groups}
{title2=$\mathcal L_F(g)=g^{-1}F(g)$}

For the <Lang map>, $\mathcal L_F(gh)=h^{-1}\mathcal L_F(g)F(h)$. This twisted right-translation identity determines which subgroups act on its inverse images. Fibres are left $G^F$-cosets, by the <Lang theorem for algebraic groups>.

= Affine group
{parent=Affine algebraic group}
{wiki}
{title2=$\operatorname{Aff}(V)=V\rtimes\operatorname{GL}(V)$}

For a finite-dimensional <vector space> $V$, the affine group consists of invertible <affine maps> $x\mapsto Ax+b$, where $A$ belongs to the <general linear group> and $b\in V$. Its product is $(A,b)(A',b')=(AA',b+Ab')$, making it the <semidirect product> of the translation vector space and its general linear group. On the real line the positive-dilation subgroup is the <real affine group> used in the adjacent article.

= Rational representation of an algebraic group
{parent=Affine algebraic group}
{wiki=Rational_representation}
{title2=$\rho:G\to GL(W)$}

= Rational representation
{synonym}

A <rational representation> is a <group homomorphism> whose matrix coefficients are <regular functions> on the <affine algebraic group>. For the <general linear group>, those functions are <polynomials> in matrix entries and the inverse <determinant>. The word rational does not permit poles at points of the group.

= Unstable vector in a rational representation
{parent=Rational representation of an algebraic group}
{title2=$0\in\overline{Gv}$}

A vector is unstable if zero lies in the Zariski closure of its orbit. For complex $SL_n$, this is equivalent to an <algebraic one-parameter subgroup> taking it to zero as $t\to0$. In the subgroup's weight decomposition, exactly the positive-weight components can occur in such a vector.

= Hilbert-Mumford criterion for the affine null cone
{c}
{parent=Unstable vector in a rational representation}

For complex $SL_n$, choose a sequence taking an unstable vector to zero and apply singular value decomposition to each group element. Compactness gives a limiting right-unitary factor. Every nonzero torus-weight component of the transformed vector forces a strict linear inequality on the diagonal exponents. A rational solution of these finitely many strict inequalities scales to integral exponents with sum zero, producing an <algebraic one-parameter subgroup> that takes the vector to zero. The converse follows immediately from the subgroup limit.

= Root-multiplicity criterion for unstable binary forms
{parent=Hilbert-Mumford criterion for the affine null cone}
{title2=$f\text{ unstable}\iff f=0\text{ or a root has multiplicity }>d/2$}

For $\operatorname{diag}(t,t^{-1})$, the monomial $x^{d-i}y^i$ has weight $d-2i$. Positive weights force divisibility by $x^{\lfloor d/2\rfloor+1}$. Conjugating the subgroup replaces $x$ by any nonzero linear factor. Thus a nonzero <binary form> is unstable exactly when a projective root has multiplicity greater than half its degree. Equality gives zero weight and does not imply instability.

= Complete reducibility of rational GL and SL representations
{parent=Rational representation of an algebraic group}

For finite-dimensional <rational representations> of complex $GL_m$ or $SL_m$, average a Hermitian <inner product> over the <compact group> $U(m)$ or $SU(m)$ using normalized <Haar measure>. <Orthogonal complements> become invariant under the compact group and its complexified <Lie algebra>. The latter generates the complex group, giving invariant complements and hence complete reducibility. This conclusion does not hold for arbitrary <affine algebraic groups>, such as the additive group.

= Rational representation of the general linear group
{parent=Rational representation of an algebraic group}
{title2=$\rho_{ij}\in\mathbb C[g_{ab},\det(g)^{-1}]$}

= Rational GL representation
{synonym}

The matrix coefficients of a rational $GL_m$ action belong to its <coordinate ring> $\mathbb C[g_{ab},\det(g)^{-1}]$. Multiplying the action by a sufficiently large <determinant twist> clears all <determinant> denominators and produces a <polynomial representation>. Irreducible rational actions are indexed by decreasing integer highest weights, with negative coordinates allowed.

= Rational Schur module
{parent=Rational representation of the general linear group}
{title2=$D_\lambda=\det^{-k}\otimes D_{\lambda+k\mathbf1}$}

For a dominant integer tuple $\lambda$, choose $k=\max(0,-\lambda_m)$ and define $D_\lambda=\det^{-k}\otimes D_{\lambda+k(1,\ldots,1)}$, using the <polynomial> <Schur module> on the right. The <alternant character formula for the general linear group> shows that a larger admissible shift yields the same irreducible representation.

= Dual of a rational Schur module
{parent=Rational Schur module}
{title2=$D_\lambda^*\cong D_{(-\lambda_m,\ldots,-\lambda_1)}$}

The dual of $D_\lambda$ has dominant label $-w_0\lambda=(-\lambda_m,\ldots,-\lambda_1)$. Substitution $x_i\mapsto x_i^{-1}$ in the <alternant character formula for the general linear group> and reversal of determinant columns gives that <character>. The numerator and denominator reversal signs and common <monomial> factors cancel.

= Highest-weight classification of rational GL representations
{parent=Rational representation of the general linear group}
{title2=$\lambda\in\mathbb Z^m,\quad\lambda_1\ge\cdots\ge\lambda_m$}

The irreducible rational $GL_m$ <modules> are $D_\lambda(V)=(\det V)^{\lambda_m}\otimes D_{\lambda-\lambda_m(1,\ldots,1)}(V)$. The second factor is a <Schur module>. Clearing <determinant> denominators reduces completeness to the <Schur algebra> classification of <polynomial> <modules>. Distinct dominant integer tuples have distinct highest torus weights. Their <characters> are symmetric <Laurent polynomials>; only <polynomial> <modules> have <characters> defined at every singular endomorphism.

= Rational extension from SL to GL
{parent=Rational representation of the general linear group}
{title2=$\rho'(g)|_{W_k}=a^k\rho(a^{-1}g)|_{W_k},\quad a^m=\det g$}

Split a rational $SL_m$ representation by its finite scalar center: on $W_k$, $\rho(\zeta I)=\zeta^kI$ for $\zeta^m=1$. The displayed extension is independent of the scalar-root choice and is a <group homomorphism>. A matrix coefficient of $\rho|_{W_k}$ can be represented on $SL_m$ by a sum of homogeneous <polynomials> $P_d$ with $d\equiv k\pmod m$, by averaging over the scalar center. Its extension is $\sum_d(\det g)^{(k-d)/m}P_d(g)$, a <regular function> on $GL_m$. Every invariant subspace remains invariant under this extension, so irreducibility is preserved in both directions.

= Determinant twist
{parent=Rational representation of the general linear group}
{title2=$g\longmapsto(\det g)^r\rho(g)$}

Tensoring a <rational representation> of the <general linear group> with a <determinant> power shifts every highest-weight coordinate by the same integer $r$. Positive twists can clear <determinant> denominators; negative twists supply <rational representations> that do not extend to singular matrices. Twisting preserves irreducibility and dimension.

= One-dimensional rational characters of the general linear group
{parent=Determinant twist}
{title2=$X^*(GL_m)=\mathbb Z\cdot\det$}

Every one-dimensional rational $GL_m$ representation is $\det^r$ for one integer $r$. Its restriction to the diagonal torus is a <Laurent monomial>. Invariance under conjugation by permutation matrices forces all its exponents to agree, and density of <diagonalizable> invertible matrices gives the result on the whole group. For $GL_1$, the <character> identity $p(zw)=p(z)p(w)$ directly forces a <Laurent polynomial> to be one monomial with coefficient one.

= Polynomial representation of the general linear group
{parent=Rational representation of the general linear group}
{title2=$\rho_{ij}\in\mathbb C[g_{ab}]$}

= Polynomial representation
{synonym}

In a <polynomial representation>, every matrix coefficient is a <polynomial> in the matrix entries. The action extends to the monoid of all matrices. The scalar action splits it into homogeneous <polynomial> degrees; each homogeneous degree-$n$ category is the <module> category of the <Schur algebra> $S(m,n)$. Its irreducibles are the <Schur modules> $D_\lambda(\mathbb C^m)$ with $\lambda\vdash n$ and at most $m$ rows.

= Binary form
{parent=Polynomial representation of the general linear group}
{title2=$f(x,y)=\sum_{i=0}^d\binom di f_i x^{d-i}y^i$}

A binary form is a homogeneous polynomial in two variables. Degree-$d$ binary forms give the symmetric-power representation of the standard two-dimensional representation of $SL_2$, after using its invariant alternating form to identify the standard representation with its dual.

= Multiplicity-free symmetric-algebra model for polynomial GL representations
{parent=Polynomial representation of the general linear group}
{title2=$\operatorname{Sym}(V\oplus\Lambda^2V)=\bigoplus_\lambda D_\lambda(V)$}

The <symmetric algebra> on $V\oplus\Lambda^2V$ contains every irreducible <polynomial representation> of $GL(V)$ exactly once. Its torus <character> is $\prod_i(1-x_i)^{-1}\prod_{i<j}(1-x_ix_j)^{-1}$, which equals $\sum_\lambda s_\lambda(x)$ by the Schur identity. Complete reducibility and linear independence of Schur <characters> identify the summands. This is a formal graded identity: each fixed scalar degree is finite, so no analytic convergence is needed. Multiplicity one refers to irreducible <modules>, not arbitrary reducible representations.

= Schur functor
{c}
{parent=Polynomial representation of the general linear group}
{wiki}
{title2=$D_\lambda:V\longmapsto h_tV^{\otimes n}$}

For a <partition of an integer> $\lambda\vdash n$, applying a <Young symmetrizer> of that shape to the <tensor power> of each <vector space> gives a Schur functor. The construction respects <linear maps> because their <tensor powers> commute with place permutations. For single rows it gives <symmetric powers>, and for single columns <exterior powers>.

= Schur module
{c}
{parent=Schur functor}
{title2=$D_\lambda(V)$}

A Schur <module> is the value of a <Schur functor> on a <vector space>. Over the <complex numbers> it is either zero or an irreducible <polynomial representation> of the <general linear group>. Its <character> is the <Schur polynomial> $s_\lambda$ in the <eigenvalues>, and its highest weight is $\lambda$ padded with zeros.

= Length bound for a Schur module
{parent=Schur module}
{title2=$D_\lambda(\mathbb C^m)\ne0\iff\ell(\lambda)\le m$}

A column of length greater than $m$ antisymmetrizes more than $m$ vectors, giving zero. When there are at most $m$ rows, place the $i$th <basis> vector in every tensor position of row $i$. Row symmetrization multiplies by a nonzero factorial product, and column antisymmetrization is nonzero because the vectors in every column are distinct. This proves the precise nonvanishing criterion for a <Schur module>.

= Schur algebra
{c}
{parent=Polynomial representation of the general linear group}
{wiki}
{title2=$S(m,n)=\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb CS_n}((\mathbb C^m)^{\otimes n})$}

The Schur algebra is the <commutant> of place permutations on a <tensor power>. It is the linear span of the diagonal <general linear group> action, by <Schur–Weyl duality>. <Modules> over $S(m,n)$ correspond to homogeneous degree-$n$ <polynomial representations>. Over the <complex numbers> it is a <semisimple algebra>: decompose the <tensor power> as a <module> for the semisimple <group algebra> $\mathbb CS_n$, and take its endomorphism algebra.

= Invertible tensor powers span the Schur algebra
{parent=Schur algebra}
{title2=$S(m,n)=\operatorname{span}\{g^{\otimes n}:g\in\operatorname{GL}(V)\}$}

The commutant of the <permutation> action on $V^{\otimes n}$ identifies with the <symmetric tensors> in $\operatorname{End}(V)^{\otimes n}$. <Polarization spanning of symmetric tensors> spans it by $a^{\otimes n}$. Interpolating $(a+tI)^{\otimes n}$ at $n+1$ values where $a+tI$ is invertible expresses each such power using invertible ones. Hence the <Schur algebra> is the linear span of the diagonal general linear action.

= Schur–Weyl duality
{c}
{parent=Schur algebra}
{wiki}
{title2=$V^{\otimes n}=\bigoplus_{\lambda\vdash n}S^\lambda\otimes D_\lambda(V)$}

The commuting actions of the <symmetric group> and the <general linear group> on a <tensor power> are mutual <commutants>. The displayed sum ranges over partitions with at most $\dim V$ rows. The <Specht modules> and <Schur modules> are the simple factors for the two actions. In particular a primitive <Young symmetrizer> selects one copy of the matching Schur <module>.

= Tensor cube of the defining unitary representation
{parent=Schur–Weyl duality}
{title2=$V^{\otimes3}=\operatorname{Sym}^3V\oplus\Lambda^3V\oplus V_{(2,1)}^{\oplus2}$}

For the <unitary group> $U(n)$ with $n\geq3$, the third <tensor power> of its <defining representation> decomposes as displayed. The first two summands have <highest weights> $(3,0,\ldots)$ and $(1,1,1,0,\ldots)$ and <dimensions> $\binom{n+2}3$ and $\binom n3$. The mixed summand has <highest weight> $(2,1,0,\ldots)$ and <dimension> $n(n^2-1)/3$. In the three-dimensional <weight space> spanned by <permutations> of $e_1\otimes e_1\otimes e_2$, the vectors killed by all positive <root vectors> have coefficient sum zero, giving multiplicity two. For $n=2$, the exterior cube vanishes and $V_{(2,1)}=\det\otimes\mathbb C^2$.

= Trace of a permuted tensor power
{parent=Schur–Weyl duality}
{title2=$\operatorname{tr}(\sigma\xi^{\otimes n})=\prod_r\operatorname{tr}(\xi^r)^{\alpha_r}$}

If $\sigma$ has $\alpha_r$ cycles of length $r$, contraction of matrix entries around its cycles gives $\operatorname{tr}(\sigma\xi^{\otimes n})=\prod_r\operatorname{tr}(\xi^r)^{\alpha_r}$. The formula holds for every <endomorphism>, not only diagonalizable ones. The <Schur–Weyl duality> decomposition equates it with a sum of products of Specht and Schur <characters>.

= Young-symmetrizer tensor decomposition
{c}
{parent=Schur–Weyl duality}
{title2=$V^{\otimes n}=\bigoplus_{\lambda,t\ \mathrm{standard}}h_tV^{\otimes n}$}

The standard-tableau right-ideal decomposition of $\mathbb CS_n$, tensored over that algebra with $V^{\otimes n}$, gives this direct sum of <general linear group> <modules>. With $e_t=h_t/H_\lambda$, the map $e_t\mathbb CS_n\otimes T\to e_tT$ is an isomorphism. Individual summands generally need not be <symmetric group> <submodules>; instead each is a <Schur module> for the commuting action.

= Lie algebra of an affine algebraic group
{parent=Affine algebraic group}

The Lie algebra of an affine algebraic group $G$ is the tangent space $T_eG$ at its identity. Its bracket is the commutator of the corresponding left-invariant derivations of the coordinate ring.

= Descent of an irreducible SL2 representation to PGL2
{c}
{parent=Affine algebraic group}

The irreducible rational representations of $SL_2(\mathbb C)$ are $\operatorname{Sym}^n(\mathbb C^2)$. Such a representation descends through $SL_2\to PGL_2$ exactly when the central element $-I$ acts trivially, which holds exactly when $n$ is even.

= Faithful representation of an affine algebraic group
{parent=Affine algebraic group}

Every affine algebraic group has a faithful finite-dimensional rational <group representation>. Choose algebra generators of $k[G]$ and place them in a finite-dimensional subspace stable under right translations. An element acting trivially on that subspace has the same coordinate values as the identity and therefore is the identity.

= Left-right regular representation of an affine algebraic group
{parent=Affine algebraic group}

The group $G\times G$ acts on $k[G]$ by
$$
((a,b)f)(x)=f(a^{-1}xb).
$$
For $G=\mathbb G_m$, this gives $k[G]=\bigoplus_{r\in\mathbb Z}k_{(-r,r)}$. For $G=\mathbb G_a$, the action factors through $(a,b)\mapsto b-a$ and the degree filtration of $k[t]$ has trivial one-dimensional successive quotients.

= Semisimple element of an affine algebraic group
{parent=Affine algebraic group}

An element of an affine algebraic group is semisimple when its image in one, equivalently every, faithful finite-dimensional representation is a diagonalizable linear map.

= Unipotent element of an affine algebraic group
{parent=Affine algebraic group}

An element of an affine algebraic group is unipotent when its image in one, equivalently every, faithful finite-dimensional representation has every eigenvalue equal to one.

= Unipotent matrix
{parent=Unipotent element of an affine algebraic group}
{title2=$(U-I)^d=0$}

A square <matrix> $U$ is unipotent when $U-I$ is nilpotent. Over an <algebraically closed field>, this is equivalent to all <eigenvalues> being one. If $j$ belongs to a <nilpotent ideal> of an operator algebra, $I+j$ is a unipotent matrix. This realizes the radical factor in the <Levi decomposition of a quiver automorphism group>.

= Jordan decomposition in an affine algebraic group
{c}
{parent=Affine algebraic group}
{wiki=Jordan–Chevalley_decomposition}

Every element $g$ of an affine algebraic group has unique commuting semisimple and unipotent parts $g_s,g_u$ such that $g=g_sg_u$. The construction agrees with the multiplicative <Jordan normal form>[Jordan decomposition] in every rational representation.

= Derived subgroup of an affine algebraic group
{parent=Affine algebraic group}

The derived subgroup $[G,G]$ is the closed algebraic subgroup generated by the commutators $xyx^{-1}y^{-1}$. If $G$ is connected, images of finite products of commutators are connected and their closures stabilize by dimension, which shows that $[G,G]$ is connected.

= Solvable affine algebraic group
{parent=Derived subgroup of an affine algebraic group}

An affine algebraic group is solvable when its derived series reaches the identity. A connected solvable affine algebraic group can be conjugated into an upper triangular matrix group by the <Lie-Kolchin theorem>.

= Lie-Kolchin theorem
{c}
{parent=Solvable affine algebraic group}
{wiki=Lie–Kolchin_theorem}

Every connected solvable affine algebraic subgroup of $\operatorname{GL}(V)$ preserves a complete flag in $V$, equivalently it is conjugate to a subgroup of the upper triangular matrices.

= Unipotent algebraic group
{parent=Affine algebraic group}
{wiki=Unipotent_group}

A unipotent algebraic group is an affine algebraic group all of whose elements are unipotent. The <Kolchin theorem> embeds it into an upper unitriangular group.

= Unipotent radical
{parent=Unipotent algebraic group}
{title2=$R_u(G)$}

It is the largest connected normal unipotent subgroup of a <linear algebraic group>. A connected <reductive algebraic group> has trivial unipotent radical. The subgroup $1+J(E)$ of a finite-dimensional algebra's unit group is closed, connected and unipotent.

= Kolchin theorem
{c}
{parent=Unipotent algebraic group}

Every unipotent algebraic subgroup of $\operatorname{GL}_n$ is conjugate to a subgroup of the upper unitriangular group. The filtration by vanishing initial superdiagonals then proves that every unipotent algebraic group is a <nilpotent group>.

= Diagonalizable algebraic group
{parent=Affine algebraic group}
{wiki=Diagonalizable_group}

A diagonalizable algebraic group is isomorphic to a closed subgroup of a product of copies of $\mathbb G_m$. Every rational representation of such a group decomposes into weight spaces.

= Reductive algebraic group
{parent=Affine algebraic group}
{wiki=Reductive_group}

A reductive algebraic group is a smooth connected affine algebraic group whose largest connected normal <unipotent algebraic group> is trivial.

= Finite group of Lie type
{parent=Reductive algebraic group}
{title2=$G^F$}

= Finite groups of Lie type
{synonym}

A finite group of Lie type is obtained from rational points of a connected <reductive algebraic group> with a suitable <Frobenius endomorphism of an algebraic group>, often followed by central quotient or passage to a derived subgroup. Its complex representations combine finite-group <character theory> with the geometry of <Borel subgroups>, <maximal algebraic tori>, and the <Weyl group>.

= Harish-Chandra induction
{c}
{parent=Finite group of Lie type}
{title2=$R_{L\subset P}^G$}

For a rational <parabolic subgroup> $P=L\ltimes U$, use <inflation of a group representation> for a complex $L^F$-module across $P^F\to L^F$ and induce it to $G^F$. This is Harish-Chandra induction. Its adjoint, <Harish-Chandra restriction>, takes $U^F$-invariants. These are ordinary finite-group <group representation> functors and should not be confused with general <Deligne-Lusztig induction> from a nonrational Borel.

= Mackey formula for Harish-Chandra induction
{c}
{parent=Harish-Chandra induction}

For rational <parabolic subgroups> $P=LU$ and $Q=MV$, define $\mathcal S(M,L)=\{x:M\cap{}^xL\text{ contains a maximal algebraic torus of }G\}$. Then $\,{}^*R_M^G R_L^G$ is the sum over $M^F\backslash\mathcal S(M,L)^F/L^F$ of $R_{M\cap{}^xL}^{M}\,{}^*R_{M\cap{}^xL}^{{}^xL}\operatorname{ad}x$, with the intersection parabolics understood. It follows from ordinary finite-group <Mackey restriction formula>, taking $V^F$-invariants, and the compatible-Levi intersection lemma for parabolics.

= Harish-Chandra restriction
{c}
{parent=Harish-Chandra induction}
{title2=$\,{}^*R_{L\subset P}^G(V)=V^{U^F}$}

Restriction first views a $G^F$-module as a $P^F$-module, then takes the subspace fixed by the unipotent radical. The resulting $L^F$-module is adjoint to <Harish-Chandra induction> by ordinary <Frobenius reciprocity>. Over characteristic zero, averaging makes invariants exact and identifies them with coinvariants.

= Cuspidal representation of a finite reductive group
{parent=Harish-Chandra restriction}

An irreducible complex $G^F$-module is cuspidal if its <Harish-Chandra restriction> is zero for every proper rational <parabolic subgroup>. By adjunction, it occurs in no <Harish-Chandra induction> from a proper rational <Levi subgroup>. This is a finite-group notion, distinct from a cuspidal automorphic representation.

= Deligne-Lusztig theory
{c}
{parent=Finite group of Lie type}
{wiki=Deligne–Lusztig_theory}

Deligne-Lusztig theory constructs <virtual characters> of <finite groups of Lie type> from compactly supported <étale cohomology> of varieties associated with Frobenius and flags. <Rational maximal tori> and their finite-group <characters> provide induction data, while the geometry explains character orthogonality and cuspidality.

= Deligne-Lusztig induction
{c}
{parent=Deligne-Lusztig theory}
{title2=$R_T^G(\theta)$}

For a <rational maximal torus> $T$, choose a <Borel subgroup> containing it with <unipotent radical> $U$. The alternating compactly supported <étale cohomology> of $\mathcal L_F^{-1}(U)$, in the appropriate $T^F$-isotypic component, defines the <virtual character> of $G^F$ $R_T^G(\theta)$. It is independent of the chosen Borel. When the <Borel subgroup> is rational, this is ordinary induction from its rational points after <inflation of a group representation> from $T^F$.

= Harish-Chandra restriction of a Deligne-Lusztig character
{c}
{parent=Deligne-Lusztig induction}

For a rational <Levi subgroup> $M$ of a rational <parabolic subgroup> and a <rational maximal torus> $T$, the torus case of the induction-restriction formula is
$$
{}^*R_M^G R_T^G(\theta)=\sum_{x\in M^F\backslash\{g\in G^F:{}^gT\subset M\}/T^F}R_{{}^xT}^{M}({}^x\theta).
$$
In particular it vanishes if there is no rational conjugate of $T$ in $M$. If $T\subset M$, transitivity gives $R_T^G=R_M^G R_T^M$. Together with adjunction these identities show that an irreducible character up to sign arising from $T$ is cuspidal exactly when $T$ is elliptic.

= Principal series of a finite reductive group
{parent=Deligne-Lusztig induction}

For a rational <Borel subgroup> $B=TU$, the principal series is $\operatorname{Ind}_{B^F}^{G^F}\widetilde\theta$, with $\widetilde\theta$ inflated from a <linear character> of $T^F$. The <Mackey restriction formula> and rational <Bruhat decomposition> give its self-inner-product as the size of the Weyl stabilizer of $\theta$. It is irreducible precisely when that stabilizer is trivial.

= Irreducibility of the split SL3 principal series
{parent=Principal series of a finite reductive group}

For $N=q-1$, write a diagonal-torus character as $\theta(\operatorname{diag}(a,b,(ab)^{-1}))=\chi(a)^r\chi(b)^s$, with $\chi$ a generator of the character group of $\mathbb F_q^\times$. Its Weyl stabilizer is trivial exactly when $r,s,r-s$ are all nonzero modulo $N$ and, if $3\mid N$, the unordered pair $\{r,s\}$ is not $\{N/3,2N/3\}$. The first conditions exclude transpositions; the exceptional pair excludes cyclic permutation of three characters modulo a common twist.

= Deligne-Lusztig variety
{c}
{parent=Deligne-Lusztig theory}
{title2=$X(w)=\{B':(B',F(B'))\in O(w)\}$}

For a connected <reductive algebraic group> and a <Weyl group> element $w$, this locally closed subset of the <flag variety> consists of flags in relative position $w$ to their Frobenius image. For rational $B$ it is $\mathcal L_F^{-1}(B\dot wB)/B$, using the twisted right action identity of the <Lang map>. Its dimension is the length of $w$.

= Unipotent quotient in a Deligne-Lusztig variety
{parent=Deligne-Lusztig variety}

Choose rational $B=TU$, a representative $\dot w$, and $x$ with $x^{-1}F(x)=\dot w$. Set $T'=xTx^{-1}$ and $U'=F(xUx^{-1})$. Then $T'$ is rational, and
$$
\mathcal L_F^{-1}(U')/[(U'\cap F^{-1}(U'))T'^F]\cong X(w).
$$
The map sends $h$ to $hxB$. Quotienting only by the finite group $T'^F$ leaves fibres isomorphic to the positive-dimensional unipotent intersection when that intersection is nontrivial. For $w=1$ in $\mathrm{SL}_2$, this is an affine-line fibre over each rational flag, so the missing quotient cannot be ignored in a variety isomorphism.

= Borel subgroup
{c}
{parent=Reductive algebraic group}
{wiki}

A Borel subgroup is a maximal closed connected solvable subgroup of an affine algebraic group.

= Parabolic subgroup
{parent=Borel subgroup}
{wiki}

A parabolic subgroup of a connected reductive group is a closed subgroup containing a <Borel subgroup>. Its quotient in the group is projective.

= Levi subgroup
{c}
{parent=Parabolic subgroup}
{wiki=Levi_decomposition}

A Levi subgroup is a reductive complement to the unipotent radical of a parabolic subgroup.

= Flag variety of an algebraic group
{parent=Borel subgroup}
{wiki=Flag_variety}

= Flag variety
{synonym}

For a connected reductive algebraic group $G$ and a <Borel subgroup> $B$, the quotient $G/B$ is its complete flag variety. Quotients $G/P$ by <parabolic subgroups> are partial flag varieties.

= Root datum
{parent=Reductive algebraic group}
{wiki}

A root datum is a quadruple $(X^*,\Phi,X_*,\Phi^\vee)$ of dual lattices, roots, and coroots with the natural perfect pairing and reflection axioms. A reductive group with maximal torus $T$ has $X^*=X^*(T)$ and $X_*=X_*(T)$.

= Bruhat decomposition of a reductive algebraic group
{c}
{parent=Reductive algebraic group}
{wiki=Bruhat_decomposition}

= Bruhat decomposition
{c}
{synonym}

For a connected reductive algebraic group, a <Borel subgroup> $B$, and a maximal torus $T\subseteq B$, the <Weyl group> $W=N_G(T)/T$ indexes the double cosets:
$$
G=\bigsqcup_{w\in W}B\dot wB.
$$

= Relative position of Borel subgroups
{parent=Bruhat decomposition of a reductive algebraic group}

The <relative position of Borel subgroups> $B_1,B_2$ is the <Weyl group> index of their simultaneous conjugacy orbit. After moving the first subgroup to a fixed $B$, the second has the form $gBg^{-1}$ and its index is the double coset $BgB$. This identifies the diagonal action on pairs of flags with the <Bruhat decomposition>.

= Algebraic-group torsor
{parent=Affine algebraic group}

For an algebraic group $H$, a flat $H$-torsor over $Y$ is a faithfully flat morphism $X\to Y$ with a right $H$-action such that $X\times H\to X\times_YX$, $(x,h)\mapsto(x,xh)$, is an isomorphism. A Zariski torsor additionally becomes $U\times H$ on a Zariski open cover of $Y$.

= Lie group
{c}
{parent=Lie theory}
{wiki}

A Lie group is a <group> that is also a <smooth manifold>, with smooth multiplication and inversion. Its tangent space at the identity carries a natural <Lie algebra> structure.

= Semisimple Lie group
{parent=Lie group}

= Semisimple group
{synonym}

A real or complex <Lie group> is semisimple when its <Lie algebra> is semisimple. For a real group, the <Killing form> then defines a nondegenerate <bi-invariant pseudo-Riemannian metric>. Compact semisimple groups have negative-definite <Killing form>; noncompact examples such as $SL(2,\mathbb R)$ have indefinite <Killing form>. A disconnected group can also have a <semisimple Lie algebra>, so connectedness is a separate hypothesis when integrating infinitesimal actions.

= Free step-N nilpotent Lie group
{parent=Lie group}
{title2=$G^N(V)=\exp(\mathfrak g^N(V))$}

Inside the <truncated tensor algebra>, let $\mathfrak g^N(V)$ be the span of iterated <Lie brackets> of elements of $V$ of lengths at most $N$. Its exponential is the connected simply connected free nilpotent <Lie group> of step $N$, with tensor multiplication. The bracket is the commutator in the <tensor algebra>. Its grading defines dilations that multiply degree $k$ by $\lambda^k$.

= Step-2 planar group coordinates
{parent=Free step-N nilpotent Lie group}
{title2=$(v,a)(w,b)=(v+w,a+b+\tfrac12(v_1w_2-v_2w_1))$}

Write a group element as $\exp(v+a[e_1,e_2])$. The step-two Baker-Campbell-Hausdorff formula gives the displayed multiplication and inverse $(-v,-a)$. The scalar $a$ is the antisymmetric second signature coordinate, which equals the signed area for a closed planar path. The <Carnot-Carathéodory distance> is comparable to $|v|+|a|^{1/2}$ near the identity, with the same homogeneous scaling globally.

= Loop group
{parent=Lie group}
{title2=$LG=C^\infty(S^1,G)$}
{wiki}

The loop group of a <Lie group> is the group of smooth maps from the circle to that group, with pointwise multiplication and the smooth-function topology. Rotation of the source circle acts by group automorphisms. For $G=U(1)$, every loop has the form $z^w e^{ih(z)}$, where $w\in\mathbb Z$ is its <winding number> and $h$ is a smooth real periodic function determined up to addition of an integer multiple of $2\pi$.

= Positive energy representation of the loop group of U(1)
{parent=Loop group}
{title2=$R_t\pi(g)R_t^*=\pi(g(e^{it}\,\cdot))$}

Such a continuous <projective unitary representation> admits strongly continuous rotations whose self-adjoint generator is bounded below and whose adjoint action rotates the loops. In the basic fermionic construction, integer one-particle modes are filled below zero. The rotation energy counts nonnegative particle energies and positive hole energies. It has finite-dimensional integer eigenspaces and implements circle rotations. Tensor products give higher nonnegative integer levels.

= Loop group two-cocycle for U(1)
{parent=Positive energy representation of the loop group of U(1)}
{title2=$c((w,h),(v,k))=e^{ivh_0-i\omega(h,k)/2}$}

Write a loop as $z^w e^{ih}$ with $h$ smooth real periodic and $h_0$ its mean. For the level-one representation with modes filled below zero and $\omega(h,k)=(2\pi)^{-1}\int_0^{2\pi}h'k$, choose implementers $U(w,h)=S^w e^{iA(h)}$, where $S$ increases charge by one, $SQS^*=Q-I$, and $[A(h),A(k)]=i\omega(h,k)I$. Their product differs from $U(w+v,h+k)$ by the displayed <two-cocycle>. Its phase is bilinear, which verifies the cocycle identity; integer $v$ makes it independent of the $2\pi$ ambiguity in $h_0$. Rephasing implementers changes it by a <group coboundary>.

= Bi-invariant pseudo-Riemannian metric
{parent=Lie group}

A nondegenerate invariant symmetric <bilinear form> on a <Lie algebra> extends by translation to a metric preserved by both left and right translation. It may have indefinite signature. Its <Levi-Civita connection> is $\nabla_XY=\frac12[X,Y]$ for <left-invariant vector fields>, and its <Ricci tensor> is $-K/4$. Choosing the nondegenerate <Killing form> of a semisimple <Lie algebra> yields a canonical indefinite <Einstein manifold>; compact groups instead admit the positive metric $-K$.

= Killing-form Einstein metric
{c}
{parent=Bi-invariant pseudo-Riemannian metric}
{title2=$g=B,\quad\operatorname{Ric}=-\tfrac14B$}

On a real semisimple <Lie group>, the nondegenerate invariant <Killing form> gives a <bi-invariant pseudo-Riemannian metric>. For left-invariant fields the <Koszul formula> gives $\nabla_XY=[X,Y]/2$ and $R(X,Y)Z=-[[X,Y],Z]/4$. Taking the trace gives $\operatorname{Ric}(Y,Z)=-B(Y,Z)/4$, proving the Einstein property. On a compact <semisimple group>, $-B$ is positive definite and has Einstein constant $1/4$. The indefinite general construction is not a proof of existence of a positive-definite bi-invariant metric on a noncompact group.

= Compact Lie group
{parent=Lie group}
{wiki}

A compact Lie group is a <Lie group> whose underlying manifold is <compact>. Averaging any positive inner product over its <Haar measure> supplies an invariant inner product on each finite-dimensional representation, including the <adjoint representation>.

= Weyl integration formula
{c}
{parent=Compact Lie group}
{wiki}

Let $G$ be a <connected> <compact Lie group>, $T$ a <maximal torus>, and $W$ its <Weyl group>. With normalized <Haar measures> and $D(t)=\prod_{\alpha>0}(1-e^{-\alpha}(t))$, every <continuous> <class function> satisfies $\int_G f(g)\,dg=|W|^{-1}\int_T f(t)|D(t)|^2\,dt$. For a general <continuous function>, replace $f(t)$ by its average $\int_{G/T}f(gtg^{-1})\,d(gT)$. <Conjugation> from $G/T\times T$ covers the regular elements $|W|$ times, and its real <Jacobian determinant> is $|D(t)|^2$.

= Weyl integration formula for U(n)
{c}
{parent=Weyl integration formula}
{title2=$\frac1{n!}\prod_{i<j}|z_i-z_j|^2$}

For the <unitary group>, the diagonal <maximal torus> has coordinates $z_i=e^{i\theta_i}$ and <Weyl group> $S_n$. The <Weyl integration formula> <weights> independent uniform angles by $n!^{-1}\prod_{i<j}|z_i-z_j|^2$. The <Vandermonde determinant> $\det(z_i^{n-j})$ has squared <torus> integral $n!$, by <Fourier orthogonality>. Multiplying an <irreducible> <character> of <highest weight> $\lambda$ by this <determinant> gives an alternating <Laurent polynomial>. Its leading alternant has coefficient one; <character orthogonality for compact groups> forces every other alternant coefficient to vanish. This yields the quotient $\det(z_i^{\lambda_j+n-j})/\det(z_i^{n-j})$.

= Translation-dilation group of the plane
{parent=Lie group}
{title2=$\mathbb R^2\rtimes\mathbb R_{>0}$}

This <Lie group> acts on the plane by $v\mapsto x+\rho v$ with $\rho>0$. Its multiplication is $(\rho,x)(\rho',x')=(\rho\rho',x+\rho x')$, and its inverse is $(\rho^{-1},-\rho^{-1}x)$. It is a <semidirect product> in which positive dilation acts on the translation subgroup. A dilation generator $D$ and translation generators $T_1,T_2$ have <Lie brackets> $[D,T_a]=T_a$ and $[T_1,T_2]=0$.

= Left-invariant coframe of the translation-dilation group
{parent=Translation-dilation group of the plane}
{title2=$\lambda^0=d\rho/\rho,\quad\lambda^a=dx^a/\rho$}

The <Maurer-Cartan form> of the <translation-dilation group of the plane> has these coefficients in its dilation–translation basis, with $a=1,2$. Left multiplication rescales each coordinate differential and $\rho$ equally, proving they are <left-invariant differential forms>. Their dual <left-invariant vector fields> are $\rho\partial_\rho$ and $\rho\partial_{x^a}$. The <Maurer-Cartan equation> is $d\lambda^0=0$, $d\lambda^a=-\lambda^0\wedge\lambda^a$. The tensor $\sum_j\lambda^j\otimes\lambda^j$ is a positive definite <left-invariant metric>.

= Lie group homomorphism
{c}
{parent=Lie group}
{title2=$F:G\to H,\quad F(ab)=F(a)F(b)$}

A smooth homomorphism of <Lie groups> has differential $D_eF$ at the identity. Differentiating $F\circ L_g=L_{F(g)}\circ F$ makes the corresponding <left-invariant vector fields> $F$-related. Their commutator action on pulled-back functions shows that the <differential of a Lie group homomorphism preserves Lie brackets>. The map need not be injective or surjective.

= Right-invariant Riemannian metric
{parent=Lie group}
{title2=$R_g^*h=h$}

A <Riemannian metric> on a <Lie group> is right-invariant when every right translation is an <isometry>. It is determined by a positive-definite <inner product> at the identity, extended with a coframe of <right-invariant differential forms>. Its translation-generated <Killing vector fields> are left-invariant, rather than the right-invariant vector fields dual to that coframe.

= Right-invariant differential form
{parent=Lie group}

A <differential form> $\alpha$ on a <Lie group> is right-invariant if $R_g^*\alpha=\alpha$ for every right translation $R_g(p)=pg$. A right <Maurer-Cartan form> is represented in a matrix group by $dg\,g^{-1}$, whereas the left version is $g^{-1}dg$. Its components supply a right-invariant coframe.

= Maximal torus
{parent=Lie group}
{title2=$T$}

A maximal torus is a connected compact abelian <Lie subgroup> maximal among such subgroups. Restricting a finite-dimensional <unitary representation> to it splits into one-dimensional weight characters. For <special unitary group> $\mathrm{SU}(2)$ it is the diagonal circle $\operatorname{diag}(z,z^{-1})$.

= Character lattice of a torus
{parent=Maximal torus}
{title2=$X^*(T)=\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathrm{cts}}(T,U(1))$}

For a <compact> <torus> $T\cong U(1)^r$, its <continuous> one-dimensional <characters> form a <free abelian group> of rank $r$. They are precisely $z\mapsto z_1^{m_1}\cdots z_r^{m_r}$ with integer exponents. This follows from the classification of <representations of the circle group>, applied to each factor. These <characters> are the integral <group> <weights> occurring upon restricting a <group representation> to a <maximal torus>; global integrality must be distinguished from integrality against coroots for a <simply connected> semisimple covering <group>.

= Identity component of a Lie group
{parent=Lie group}
{title2=$G^\circ$}

The <connected component> of the identity in a <Lie group> is an open <normal subgroup>. Connected coordinate neighbourhoods prove openness, and conjugation preserves the component of the identity. Every open identity neighbourhood generates it: the generated <subgroup> is open, its other <cosets> are open, and connectedness forces it to be the whole component.

= Group manifold
{parent=Lie group}

The <group manifold> is the underlying <smooth manifold> of a <Lie group>, with smooth multiplication and inversion. Its global topology need not be determined by its <Lie algebra>: the <SU(2) group> and <SO(3) group> have isomorphic <Lie algebras> but different <fundamental groups>.

= Lie subgroup
{c}
{parent=Lie group}
{wiki}

A Lie subgroup is a subgroup carrying a <Lie group> structure such that its inclusion is an injective <immersion> and a <group homomorphism>. Its <Lie algebra> includes in that of the ambient <Lie group>.

= Closed-subgroup theorem
{parent=Lie subgroup}
{wiki}

= Cartan closed-subgroup theorem
{c}
{synonym}

Every closed <subgroup> of a finite-dimensional <Lie group> is an <embedded submanifold> and a <Lie subgroup>. Closedness alone forces the compatible smooth structure; smoothness need not be assumed in advance. The proof constructs the <Lie algebra of a closed subgroup> from <one-parameter subgroups>, then uses <local product coordinates for a closed subgroup>.

= Local product coordinates for a closed subgroup
{parent=Closed-subgroup theorem}
{title2=$F(U,V)=\exp U\exp V,\quad\mathfrak g=\mathfrak m\oplus\mathfrak h$}

Choose a <vector subspace> $\mathfrak m$ complementary to the <Lie algebra of a closed subgroup> $H$. The differential of $F$ at $(0,0)$ is $(U,V)\mapsto U+V$, so the <inverse function theorem> makes $F$ a local <diffeomorphism>. Sufficiently small nonzero $U\in\mathfrak m$ cannot satisfy $\exp U\in H$: otherwise <limit directions of a closed subgroup> give a nonzero element of $\mathfrak m\cap\mathfrak h$. Consequently $H$ is locally exactly the coordinate slice $U=0$, proving that it is an <embedded submanifold>.

= Lie algebra of a closed subgroup
{c}
{parent=Closed-subgroup theorem}
{title2=$\mathfrak h=\{X\in\mathfrak g:\exp(tX)\in H\ \forall t\in\mathbb R\}$}

For a closed <subgroup> $H$ of a <Lie group>, this set is a real <Lie subalgebra>. Scalar closure is immediate; the <Lie product formula in a Lie group> and closedness of $H$ give addition. It is a closed <vector subspace>, also directly by continuity of the <exponential map of a Lie group>. Conjugating its <one-parameter subgroups> by elements of $H$ preserves it. Differentiating the <adjoint representation of a Lie group> along $\exp(sX)$ gives $[X,Y]\in\mathfrak h$ for $X,Y\in\mathfrak h$. After the <closed-subgroup theorem> establishes smoothness, $\mathfrak h$ is exactly the tangent space $T_eH$.

= Limit directions of a closed subgroup
{parent=Closed-subgroup theorem}
{title2=$X_j\to0,\quad X_j/\|X_j\|\to X,\quad\exp X_j\in H\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\exp(tX)\in H$}

Let $H$ be a closed <subgroup> of a <Lie group> $G$, and let nonzero vectors $X_j$ in the ambient <Lie algebra> $\mathfrak g$ tend to zero with $\exp X_j\in H$ and $X_j/\|X_j\|\to X$. For any real $t$, choose <integers> $m_j$ with $m_j\|X_j\|\to t$. Then $(\exp X_j)^{m_j}=\exp(m_jX_j)\to\exp(tX)$. Closedness gives $\exp(tX)\in H$. <Compactness> of the unit sphere supplies a limit direction from every sequence approaching the identity through nonidentity subgroup elements.

= Lie algebra of a normal Lie subgroup
{parent=Lie subgroup}
{c}
{title2=$H\triangleleft G\Longrightarrow[\mathfrak g,\mathfrak h]\subseteq\mathfrak h$}

Conjugation by every element of $G$ preserves a normal <Lie subgroup> $H$. Its differential therefore preserves $\mathfrak h=T_eH$. Differentiate that invariance along $\exp(tX)$ to obtain $\operatorname{ad}(X)\mathfrak h\subseteq\mathfrak h$, proving that $\mathfrak h$ is an <ideal of a Lie algebra>. Closed subgroups have a canonical Lie-subgroup structure; an arbitrary abstract subgroup need not.

= Lie group action
{c}
{parent=Lie group}

A Lie group action is a smooth <group action> of a Lie group on a smooth manifold. Differentiating the action gives an infinitesimal action of its <Lie algebra> by vector fields.

= Proper Lie group action
{parent=Lie group action}

= Proper group action
{synonym}

A Lie group action on $M$ is proper if $(g,x)\mapsto(gx,x)$ from $G\times M$ to $M\times M$ is a proper map. A free proper smooth action has a smooth quotient manifold, with the quotient map a submersion and principal bundle. Proper actions with stabilizers can have orbit-type strata and singular quotients. These qualifications are necessary before treating an orbit space as a smooth phase space.

= Torus action
{parent=Lie group action}

A smooth action of the compact abelian <Lie group> $\mathbb R^n/(2\pi\mathbb Z)^n$. Commuting <vector fields> with a common periodic normalization give such an action. In <action-angle variables>, the torus acts by translation of the angles while leaving the actions fixed.

= Special linear congruence action on symmetric matrices
{parent=Lie group action}
{title2=$A\cdot B=ABA^T,\quad\det A=\det B=1$}

The <special linear group> acts on determinant-one real <symmetric matrices> by <matrix congruence>. Symmetry and determinant are preserved, and signatures are preserved by <Sylvester's law of inertia>. The determinant-one set is a <regular level set> of dimension $n(n+1)/2-1$, with tangent directions $C=C^T$ satisfying $\operatorname{tr}(B^{-1}C)=0$. Its infinitesimal left-action fields are $XB+BX^T$; converting them to a <Lie algebra representation> requires the <infinitesimal left-action sign convention>.

= Coadjoint representation
{parent=Lie group action}
{title2=$\operatorname{Ad}^*$}
{wiki}

= Coadjoint action
{synonym}

The coadjoint action of a <Lie group> on the <dual space> of its <Lie algebra> is $(\operatorname{Ad}_g^*\eta)(X)=\eta(\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}X)$. The inverse in this formula makes it a left <group action>.

= Coadjoint orbit
{parent=Coadjoint representation}
{wiki}

A <coadjoint orbit> is the orbit of $\ell\in\mathfrak g^*$ under $\operatorname{Ad}^*_g\ell$, defined by $\langle\operatorname{Ad}^*_g\ell,\xi\rangle=\langle\ell,\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}\xi\rangle$. For a connected Lie group these orbits are the <symplectic leaves> of its Lie-Poisson manifold. Their tangent vectors are infinitesimal coadjoint actions, and their natural two-form is the <Kirillov–Kostant–Souriau symplectic form>.

= Kirillov–Kostant–Souriau symplectic form
{c}
{parent=Coadjoint orbit}
{title2=$\omega_\ell(\operatorname{ad}^*_\xi\ell,\operatorname{ad}^*_\eta\ell)=\langle\ell,[\xi,\eta]\rangle$}

With $\langle\operatorname{ad}^*_\xi\ell,\eta\rangle=-\langle\ell,[\xi,\eta]\rangle$, the displayed form on a <coadjoint orbit> is well-defined: changing a generator by an element of the stabilizer changes no pairing. It is nondegenerate on orbit tangents, and its closedness follows from the Jacobi identity. This sign agrees with the positive Lie-Poisson bracket when Hamiltonian vector fields act by $X_f(g)=\{g,f\}$. A negative Lie-Poisson convention reverses the orbit form as well.

= Fundamental vector field
{parent=Lie group action}
{title2=$X_M$}
{wiki}

For a <Lie group action> and $X$ in its <Lie algebra>, the fundamental <vector field> is $X_M(p)=\left.\frac{d}{dt}\right|_{t=0}\exp(tX)\cdot p$.

= Infinitesimal left-action sign convention
{parent=Fundamental vector field}
{title2=$[X^\#,Y^\#]=-[X,Y]^\#,\quad v_X=-X^\#$}

For a smooth left <Lie group action>, define the <fundamental vector field> by $X^\#(p)=\left.\frac d{ds}\right|_0\exp(sX)\cdot p$. With the <Lie bracket of vector fields> $[U,V]f=U(Vf)-V(Uf)$, this map is an anti-homomorphism. The map $X\mapsto v_X=-X^\#$ is a <Lie algebra representation>. A right action with the positive exponential has the opposite homomorphism convention, so specifying action side and bracket convention prevents a sign ambiguity.

= Matrix Lie group
{c}
{parent=Lie group}
{wiki}

A matrix Lie group is a subgroup of a <general linear group> that is also a <Lie group> for its matrix topology. Near the identity, the <matrix exponential> and <matrix logarithm> give inverse coordinate charts whenever they are restricted to sufficiently small neighborhoods in the group and its tangent space.

= Lie algebra of a quadratic-form stabilizer
{c}
{parent=Matrix Lie group}
{title2=$\mathfrak g_Q=\{X:X^TA+AX=0\}$}

For a real <quadratic form> $Q(v)=v^TAv$ with $A$ a <symmetric matrix>, its stabilizer is $G_Q=\{g\in\operatorname{GL}_n(\mathbb R):g^TAg=A\}$. Its <Lie algebra> consists exactly of $X$ satisfying $X^TA+AX=0$. Differentiation proves necessity; the <matrix exponential> proves sufficiency because $e^{tX^T}Ae^{tX}=A$. The result holds even when the <quadratic form> is degenerate.

= Exponential map of a matrix Lie group
{title2=$\exp:\mathfrak g\to G$}
{parent=Matrix Lie group}

For a <matrix Lie group> $G$ with Lie algebra $\mathfrak g$, its exponential map is the restriction of the <matrix exponential>, $X\mapsto e^X$. It maps $\mathfrak g$ into $G$ and is a local diffeomorphism at zero, but it need not be surjective globally.

= Exponential-surjectivity obstruction from distinct negative eigenvalues
{parent=Exponential map of a matrix Lie group}

A real matrix with at least one simple negative <eigenvalue> cannot be a <matrix exponential> of a real matrix: a logarithm would commute with it and preserve each one-dimensional real negative-eigenvalue space, where exponentiation can only produce a positive <eigenvalue>. In particular $\operatorname{diag}(-2,-1/2)$ belongs to the connected <special linear group> $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb R)$ but has no real logarithm.

= Logarithmic chart of a matrix Lie group
{parent=Matrix Lie group}

For a matrix Lie group $G$ with Lie algebra $\mathfrak g=T_I G$, choose neighborhoods on which $\exp:\mathfrak g\to G$ and $\log:G\to\mathfrak g$ are inverse. Left translation gives a chart near $g\in G$ by
$$
h\longmapsto\log(g^{-1}h).
$$
The <Baker--Campbell--Hausdorff formula> expresses multiplication smoothly in these coordinates.

= Simple Lie group
{c}
{parent=Lie group}
{wiki}

A connected Lie group is simple when its <Lie algebra> is nonabelian and simple. Some conventions additionally quotient out or exclude a discrete center; the infinitesimal gauge fields depend only on the Lie algebra.

= Homogeneous space
{parent=Lie group}
{wiki}

A homogeneous space is a space on which a group acts transitively. After choosing a point, a transitive Lie-group action identifies the space with a quotient $G/H$ by the point stabilizer.

= Left and right translation on a Lie group
{title2=$L_g,R_g$}
{parent=Lie group}

= Left translations on a Lie group
{synonym}

= Right translations on a Lie group
{synonym}

= Right translation on a Lie group
{synonym}

= Left translation on a Lie group
{synonym}
{c}

= Left translation
{synonym}

For a <Lie group> $G$, left and right translation are the <diffeomorphisms> $L_g(h)=gh$ and $R_g(h)=hg$. They commute: $L_gR_h=R_hL_g$.

= Flat translation connections on a Lie group
{parent=Left and right translation on a Lie group}
{title2=$\nabla^-_XY=0,\quad\nabla^+_XY=[X,Y]$}

Declare a global left-invariant, respectively right-invariant, frame parallel to obtain two flat <affine connections> on a <Lie group>. For <left-invariant vector fields>, their derivatives are as displayed and their <torsion tensors> are $-[X,Y]$ and $+[X,Y]$. The tensors vanish on Abelian groups; two genuinely torsionful connections are not asserted there.

= Canonical torsion-free connection on a Lie group
{parent=Flat translation connections on a Lie group}
{title2=$\nabla^0_XY=\frac12[X,Y]$}

The average of the two <flat translation connections on a Lie group> is a <torsion-free connection>. For <left-invariant vector fields>, the <Jacobi identity> gives $R(X,Y)Z=-\frac14[[X,Y],Z]$ with $R(X,Y)=[\nabla_X,\nabla_Y]-\nabla_{[X,Y]}$. Contracting gives $\operatorname{Ric}(X,Y)=-\frac14K(X,Y)$ for the <Killing form> $K$. It is flat exactly when all double Lie brackets vanish, including the case of a two-step nilpotent group. For a semisimple <Lie algebra>, $K$ is nondegenerate and supplies a bi-invariant indefinite metric whose <Levi-Civita connection> is $\nabla^0$.

= Right-invariant vector field
{parent=Left and right translation on a Lie group}
{title2=$r_\xi(g)=(dR_g)_e\xi$}

The right-invariant <vector field> associated with a <Lie algebra> element $\xi$ is obtained by right translation of its value at the identity. Its flow is $g\mapsto\exp(t\xi)g$, hence consists of <left translations on a Lie group>. Right invariance follows because left and right multiplication commute.

= Right-invariant vector fields realize the opposite Lie algebra
{parent=Right-invariant vector field}
{title2=$[r_\xi,r_\eta]=-r_{[\xi,\eta]}$}

For a <matrix Lie group>, the <right-invariant vector field> is $r_\xi(g)=\xi g$. Differentiating these ambient matrix-valued functions in the definition of the <Lie bracket of vector fields> gives $[r_\xi,r_\eta](g)=(\eta\xi-\xi\eta)g$. Thus $\xi\mapsto r_\xi$ is an antihomomorphism, and $\xi\mapsto-r_\xi$ is a homomorphism. In contrast, $\xi\mapsto l_\xi$ for <left-invariant vector fields> is a homomorphism.

= Left-invariant vector field
{title2=$l_\xi$}
{parent=Left and right translation on a Lie group}
{wiki=Invariant_vector_field}

For $\xi$ in the <Lie algebra> of $G$, the left-invariant vector field is $l_\xi(g)=(dL_g)_e\xi$. Its global <local flow>[flow] is right translation $\Phi^t=R_{\exp(t\xi)}$.

= Pauli-coordinate left-invariant vector fields on SU(2)
{parent=Left-invariant vector field}
{c}
{title2=$T_j=i(u_0\delta_{ij}+u_k\epsilon_{jki})\partial_{u_i}$}

On a hemisphere of <SU(2) as the three-sphere>, write $A=u_0I+i u_i\sigma_i$, with real coefficients satisfying $u_0^2+|\mathbf u|^2=1$. Right multiplication by $I+i v_j\sigma_j$ gives $du_i=(u_0\delta_{ij}+u_k\epsilon_{jki})v_j$. This right multiplication flow defines left-invariant tangent fields. With the displayed factor $i$, they obey $T_jA=-A\sigma_j$, since $\partial_{u_i}u_0=-u_i/u_0$. Applying the <commutator> to $A$ and using the <Pauli matrix commutator identity> gives $[T_i,T_j]=-2i\epsilon_{ijk}T_k$. The entries of $A$ contain every coordinate, so this determines the vector-field identity, not just its action in one isolated representation.

= Parallelization of a Lie group by left translations
{parent=Left-invariant vector field}
{title2=$X_a(g)=(dL_g)_eT_a$}

A basis $T_a$ of the <Lie algebra> of a <Lie group> gives a global <frame of a vector bundle> of its <tangent bundle> by $X_a(g)=(dL_g)_eT_a$. Left translation is a <diffeomorphism>, so the <left-invariant vector fields> are independent everywhere. Every <Lie group> is therefore a <parallelizable manifold>.

= Differentiating left-invariant matrix fields
{parent=Left-invariant vector field}
{title2=$[X_{B_1},X_{B_2}](Q)=Q[B_1,B_2]$}

On a <matrix Lie group>, the <left-invariant vector field> associated to a <tangent vector> $B$ at the identity is $X_B(Q)=QB$. Its ambient derivative is $DX_B(Q)[H]=HB$. Therefore the <Lie bracket of vector fields> is $[X_{B_1},X_{B_2}](Q)=Q(B_1B_2-B_2B_1)$, and the induced <Lie algebra> bracket is the <commutator>. This fixes the sign for left invariance; right-invariant fields induce the opposite sign when evaluated with the same matrix identification.

= Completeness of left-invariant vector fields
{parent=Left-invariant vector field}

Every smooth <left-invariant vector field> on a <Lie group> is a <complete vector field>. Translate a local <integral curve of a vector field> through the identity to every other point. The same positive local existence interval works at every initial point, so no maximal <integral curve of a vector field> can have a finite endpoint. Uniqueness then makes the curve through the identity a <one-parameter subgroup>.

= Left-invariant differential form
{parent=Left and right translation on a Lie group}
{wiki=Invariant_differential_form}

A differential form $\alpha$ on a <Lie group> is left-invariant when $L_g^*\alpha=\alpha$ for every $g$. It is determined by its value at the identity.

= Invariant volume form on a Lie group
{parent=Left-invariant differential form}
{title2=$\omega_g=(L_{g^{-1}})^*\omega_e$}

Choose a nonzero element of $\Lambda^nT_e^*G$ for an $n$-dimensional <Lie group> and transport it by inverse left translations. This gives a smooth nowhere-zero <left-invariant differential form> of top degree, hence an <orientation>. The identity $L_{(hg)^{-1}}L_h=L_{g^{-1}}$ proves invariance. The space of invariant top forms is one-dimensional, since evaluation at the identity identifies it with $\Lambda^nT_e^*G$.

= Bi-invariant differential form
{parent=Left-invariant differential form}

A differential form on a <Lie group> is bi-invariant when it is invariant under both left and right translations.

= Closed left-invariant 1-form
{parent=Bi-invariant differential form}

On a connected <Lie group>, a <left-invariant differential form>[left-invariant] <differential 1-form> is <closed differential form>[closed] exactly when it is <bi-invariant differential form>[bi-invariant]. Connectedness is essential: on $O(2)$ every left-invariant 1-form is closed because its Lie algebra is abelian, while reflection conjugation negates every nonzero one.

= One-parameter subgroup
{parent=Lie group}
{wiki}

= One-parameter group
{synonym}

A one-parameter subgroup of a Lie group $G$ is a smooth homomorphism $\gamma:(\mathbb R,+)\to G$. Every such subgroup has the form $\gamma(t)=\exp(tX)$ for a unique $X$ in the Lie algebra of $G$.

= Classification of nontrivial one-parameter subgroups
{parent=One-parameter subgroup}

For $X\ne0$, the image of $t\mapsto e^{tX}$, with its intrinsic immersed <Lie subgroup> topology, is isomorphic to $(\mathbb R,+)$ or the <circle group>. The kernel is respectively zero or $\tau\mathbb Z$ for a least positive period $\tau$. The case $X=0$ is the trivial group. An irrational winding in a two-dimensional torus need not be an embedded or closed subgroup, so its intrinsic topology must be distinguished from the subspace topology.

= Compact one-parameter subgroups of SL2R
{title2=$X^2=(a^2+bc)I_2$}
{parent=Classification of nontrivial one-parameter subgroups}

For $X=\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\c&-a\end{pmatrix}$, the image of its <matrix exponential> is compact precisely when $a^2+bc<0$ or $X=0$. If $a^2+bc=-\omega^2<0$, then $e^{tX}=\cos(\omega t)I_2+\sin(\omega t)X/\omega$ and its image is conjugate to the <circle group>. A nonzero nilpotent generator or a generator with real nonzero eigenvalues produces an unbounded image.

= Circle group
{title2=$S^1$}
{parent=Lie group}
{wiki}

= U(1)
{c}
{synonym}

The circle group is the unit complex numbers under multiplication, equivalently planar rotations or the quotient $\mathbb R/(2\pi\mathbb Z)$.

= Circle metric
{parent=Circle group}
{title2=$d(x,y)=\|x-y\|_{\mathbb R/\mathbb Z}$}

The quotient metric of circumference one uses the shorter distance around the circle: $\|x\|=\min_{m\in\mathbb Z}|\widetilde x-m|$. It also measures separation across the identified endpoints. This is the metric used by <circular spacing> in the <analytic large sieve inequality>.

= Lie-group representation
{parent=Lie group}

A Lie-group representation is a smooth <group representation> $D:G\to GL(V)$.

= Derived representation
{parent=Lie-group representation}

The derived representation of a smooth Lie-group representation is $dD(X)=\left.\frac d{dt}\right|_{0}D(\exp(tX))$. It is a <Lie-algebra representation> because $dD([X,Y])=[dD(X),dD(Y)]$.

= Adjoint representation of a Lie group
{title2=$\operatorname{Ad}$}
{c}
{parent=Lie group}

= Adjoint action
{synonym}

The adjoint representation of a Lie group is its action on its Lie algebra by differentiating conjugation. For a matrix Lie group, $\operatorname{Ad}_gX=gXg^{-1}$.

= Normal subgroups of a compact group with irreducible adjoint action
{parent=Adjoint representation of a Lie group}

Let $G$ be <connected> and <compact> with nonzero <irreducible> real <adjoint representation>. Its <Lie algebra> has no proper invariant <Lie algebra ideal>. If it is <abelian>, irreducibility forces <dimension> one, so $G$ is the <circle group>. Otherwise its centre has zero-dimensional <Lie algebra> and is finite. A proper closed <normal subgroup> likewise has zero-dimensional <Lie algebra>, hence is finite; connectedness of $G$ makes <conjugation> on that finite subgroup trivial, so the subgroup is central. A <Lie group homomorphism> onto a nontrivial <connected> target with <surjective> differential consequently has finite central <group kernel>. The qualifications “proper” and “nontrivial” are essential.

= Inverse conjugation and adjoint antirepresentations
{parent=Adjoint representation of a Lie group}
{title2=$F(gh)=F(h)F(g),\quad F(g)=\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}$}

With <adjoint representation> convention $\operatorname{ad}_X(Y)=[X,Y]$, the <matrix exponential> identity is $e^{-X}Ye^X=e^{-\operatorname{ad}_X}Y$. Inverse conjugation reverses product order, so it is a right action or antirepresentation, whereas $\operatorname{Ad}_g(Y)=gYg^{-1}$ is an ordinary left <group representation>. The first-order terms $Y\mp[X,Y]$ detect a sign mismatch immediately. Negating every adjoint generator without reversing the Lie bracket does not give a new Lie-algebra homomorphism.

= Adjoint double cover from SU(2) to SO(3)
{c}
{title2=$SU(2)\to SO(3)$}
{parent=Adjoint representation of a Lie group}

Conjugation by $SU(2)$ on its three-dimensional real Lie algebra of traceless skew-Hermitian matrices preserves $-\operatorname{tr}(AB)$ and defines a surjection
$$
SU(2)\longrightarrow SO(3)
$$
with kernel $\{\pm I_2\}$. It is the universal double covering of $SO(3)$.

= Descent of an SU(2) representation to SO(3)
{parent=Adjoint double cover from SU(2) to SO(3)}

A <SU(2) representation> descends to the <SO(3) group> exactly when $-I$ acts trivially. In $\operatorname{Sym}^n(\mathbb C^2)$ it acts by $(-1)^n$, so descent holds precisely for even $n$, or integer spin $j=n/2$. This is the <inflation of a group representation> criterion for the kernel $\{\pm I\}$. Half-integer-spin representations instead remain representations of the covering group.

= Local group law
{parent=Lie group}

A local group law is the coordinate expression $z=F(y,x)$ of multiplication near the identity of a <Lie group>. The identity, inverse, and associativity equations for $F$ encode the group structure locally.

= Maurer-Cartan form
{c}
{parent=Lie group}
{wiki=Maurer–Cartan_form}

For a matrix Lie group, the left-invariant Maurer-Cartan form is $\rho=g^{-1}dg$. It identifies each tangent space with the Lie algebra and obeys $d\rho+\rho\wedge\rho=0$.

= Maurer-Cartan equation
{c}
{parent=Maurer-Cartan form}

The Maurer-Cartan equation is $d\rho+\rho\wedge\rho=0$ for the left-invariant form $\rho=g^{-1}dg$.

= Maurer-Cartan equation in a Lie-algebra basis
{c}
{parent=Maurer-Cartan equation}

If $\rho=\sigma^\alpha T_\alpha$ and $[T_\alpha,T_\beta]=c^\gamma{}_{\alpha\beta}T_\gamma$, then
$$
d\sigma^\gamma=-\frac12c^\gamma{}_{\alpha\beta}
\sigma^\alpha\wedge\sigma^\beta.
$$

= Symmetric-part ambiguity in Maurer-Cartan coefficients
{parent=Maurer-Cartan equation in a Lie-algebra basis}
{title2=$f^\gamma{}_{[\alpha\beta]}=-\tfrac12c^\gamma{}_{\alpha\beta}$}

When $d\sigma^\gamma=\sum_{\alpha,\beta}f^\gamma{}_{\alpha\beta}\sigma^\alpha\wedge\sigma^\beta$ sums over ordered pairs, the <Maurer-Cartan equation> determines only $f^\gamma{}_{[\alpha\beta]}=-c^\gamma{}_{\alpha\beta}/2$. Symmetric additions vanish in the <exterior product>. The usual coefficients are the unique antisymmetric representatives; summing instead over $\alpha<\beta$ removes the factor one half.

= Orientation-preserving affine group of the real line
{parent=Lie group}
{title2=$\operatorname{Aff}^+(\mathbb R)$}

= Real affine group
{synonym}

The <real affine group> used here is the orientation-preserving subgroup of the <affine group> on the real line. It acts by $x\mapsto e^ax+b$ and has multiplication $(a,b)(a',b')=(a+a',b+e^ab')$. The positive dilation distinguishes this connected component from the full group, which also contains negative dilations.

= Invariant frames of the real affine group
{parent=Orientation-preserving affine group of the real line}
{title2=$D_L=\partial_\alpha,\ T_L=e^\alpha\partial_\beta,\ D_R=\partial_\alpha+\beta\partial_\beta,\ T_R=\partial_\beta$}

In exponential scale coordinates, the <real affine group> has matrices $\begin{pmatrix}e^\alpha&\beta\\0&1\end{pmatrix}$. The left and right <Maurer-Cartan forms> are respectively $D\,d\alpha+T\,e^{-\alpha}d\beta$ and $D\,d\alpha+T(d\beta-\beta\,d\alpha)$. Their dual <vector fields> obey $[D_L,T_L]=T_L$ and $[D_R,T_R]=-T_R$, displaying the opposite bracket sign for <right-invariant vector fields>.

= Maurer-Cartan coframe of the real affine group
{c}
{parent=Orientation-preserving affine group of the real line}
{title2=$\sigma^D=da/a,\quad\sigma^T=db/a$}

In the faithful matrices $g=\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\0&1\end{pmatrix}$ with $a>0$, the <Maurer-Cartan form> has entries $da/a$ and $db/a$. Thus the <left-invariant differential forms> $\sigma^D=da/a$, $\sigma^T=db/a$ obey $d\sigma^D=0$ and $d\sigma^T=-\sigma^D\wedge\sigma^T$. The <Lie algebra> generators satisfy $[D,T]=T$.

= Left-invariant metric
{parent=Lie group}
{wiki}

A left-invariant Riemannian metric is preserved by every left translation. Its Killing fields generated by left translations are right-invariant vector fields.

= Bi-invariant Riemannian metric
{parent=Left-invariant metric}

A <Riemannian metric> on a <Lie group> is bi-invariant if both left and right translations are isometries. Its identity inner product is invariant under the <adjoint representation of a Lie group>; infinitesimally,
$$
\langle[X,Y],Z\rangle+\langle Y,[X,Z]\rangle=0.
$$
This skew-adjointness is the key additional property beyond left invariance.

= Ricci curvature of a bi-invariant Riemannian metric
{c}
{parent=Bi-invariant Riemannian metric}
{title2=$\operatorname{Ric}(X,X)=\tfrac14\sum_i\|[X,e_i]\|^2$}

For a <bi-invariant Riemannian metric> on a <Lie group>, the induced inner product on its <Lie algebra> makes every adjoint operator skew-adjoint. The <Levi-Civita connection of a bi-invariant metric> gives $R(X,Y)Z=-\tfrac14[[X,Y],Z]$. Thus $K(X,Y)=\tfrac14\|[X,Y]\|^2$ for orthonormal $X,Y$, and
$$
\operatorname{Ric}(X,X)=\frac14\sum_i\|[X,e_i]\|^2.
$$
The nullspace of this quadratic form is exactly the <center of a Lie algebra>. A zero center therefore gives positive <Ricci curvature>, uniformly bounded below by a positive constant times the metric through left invariance and compactness of the unit sphere in the <Lie algebra>.

= Geodesics of a bi-invariant metric are one-parameter subgroups
{parent=Bi-invariant Riemannian metric}
{title2=$\gamma(t)=\exp_G(t\xi)$}

Integral curves through the identity of <left-invariant vector fields> are <one-parameter subgroups>, by flow uniqueness and left translation. They extend for all time because a fixed local existence interval translates to every point. Under a <bi-invariant Riemannian metric> they are <geodesics> by the <Levi-Civita connection of a bi-invariant metric>. Geodesic uniqueness proves that these are all geodesics through the identity.

= Levi-Civita connection of a bi-invariant metric
{c}
{parent=Bi-invariant Riemannian metric}
{title2=$\nabla_XY=\tfrac12[X,Y]$}

For <left-invariant vector fields>, the <Koszul formula> and the adjoint invariance of a <bi-invariant Riemannian metric> give the displayed <Levi-Civita connection>. In particular $\nabla_XX=0$.

= Geodesic-vector criterion for a left-invariant metric
{title2=$\langle\xi,[\xi,\eta]\rangle=0$}
{parent=Left-invariant metric}

For a <left-invariant metric> on a <Lie group>, the one-parameter subgroup $\gamma_\xi(t)=\exp(t\xi)$ is a <geodesic> exactly when
$$
\langle\xi,[\xi,\eta]\rangle=0
$$
for every $\eta$ in the <Lie algebra>. The <Koszul formula> gives $\langle\nabla_\xi\xi,\eta\rangle=-\langle\xi,[\xi,\eta]\rangle$.

= Exponential map of a Lie group
{title2=$\exp:\mathfrak g\to G$}
{c}
{parent=Lie group}

For a matrix Lie group, the exponential map is the matrix exponential $\exp X=\sum_{n\geq0}X^n/n!$ and sends one-parameter additive subgroups of the Lie algebra to one-parameter subgroups of the group.

= Lie product formula in a Lie group
{c}
{parent=Exponential map of a Lie group}
{title2=$\exp(t(X+Y))=\lim_{m\to\infty}(\exp(tX/m)\exp(tY/m))^m$}

The <Lie product formula> extends from <matrices> to any finite-dimensional <Lie group>. In a <local exponential chart>, smooth multiplication and its differential give $\log(\exp(sX)\exp(sY))=s(X+Y)+O(s^2)$. Taking $s=t/m$ and using the <one-parameter subgroup> identity shows that the $m$th power is $\exp(t(X+Y)+O(m^{-1}))$, proving the displayed <limit>. This proof needs only the first-order expansion, rather than convergence of the full <Baker--Campbell--Hausdorff formula>.

= Naturality of the Lie group exponential
{parent=Exponential map of a Lie group}
{title2=$F(\exp_Gv)=\exp_H(D_eF\,v)$}

For a <Lie group homomorphism> $F$, the image of the integral curve through the identity of a <left-invariant vector field> is the integral curve of the field induced by its differential. Uniqueness of <integral curves of a vector field> identifies the curves for all times. Evaluating at time one gives the formula. Completeness of invariant fields follows from left translation and repetition of a local integral curve, independently of a matrix-series formula.

= Local exponential chart
{parent=Exponential map of a Lie group}
{title2=$\log:\exp(U)\longrightarrow U\subseteq\mathfrak g$}

Since the differential of the <exponential map of a Lie group> at zero is the identity, the <inverse function theorem> makes it a <diffeomorphism> on a sufficiently small neighborhood of zero. Its local inverse is a logarithm near the group identity. It need not extend to a global logarithm, even for the real <general linear group>.

= Cayley transform
{disambiguate=Lie theory}
{title2=$C(X)=(I+X)(I-X)^{-1}$}
{c}
{parent=Lie group}
{wiki}

The Cayley transform is a rational local parametrization of a matrix Lie group from its Lie algebra wherever $I-X$ is invertible. Unlike the exponential map, its image can meet nonidentity components.

= Lie algebra
{c}
{parent=Lie theory}
{wiki}

A Lie algebra over a <field> is a <vector space> $\mathfrak g$ with a bilinear operation $[x,y]$, called the <Lie bracket>, that is alternating and satisfies the <Jacobi identity>.

= Lie algebra cohomology
{c}
{parent=Lie algebra}
{title2=$H^k(\mathfrak g,V)$}

The Chevalley-Eilenberg complex consists of alternating maps from $\mathfrak g^k$ to a <Lie algebra representation> $V$, with differential built from the bracket and module action. For trivial real coefficients, a two-cocycle is an alternating bilinear form $\kappa$ satisfying $\kappa([X,Y],Z)+\kappa([Y,Z],X)+\kappa([Z,X],Y)=0$. Coboundaries have the form $b([X,Y])$, up to the differential's sign convention. Their quotient is $H^2(\mathfrak g,\mathbb R)$ and classifies <central extensions of a Lie algebra>.

= Compact Lie algebra
{parent=Lie algebra}

A real Lie algebra is compact when it is the Lie algebra of some <compact Lie group>. Equivalently, it has a positive-definite <invariant bilinear form on a Lie algebra>. It is a direct sum of an abelian center and a compact <semisimple Lie algebra>. For its semisimple part, the <compactness criterion from the Killing form> is negative-definiteness of the <Killing form>. This definition concerns the algebra; a global group with that algebra can still have a noncompact central covering direction.

= Powerful Lie algebra over p-adic integers
{parent=Lie algebra}
{title2=$[L,L]\subseteq p^\epsilon L$}

= Powerful Lie lattice
{synonym}

A <Lie algebra> over the <p-adic integers> whose underlying <module> is free of finite rank and whose <Lie bracket> satisfies $[L,L]\subseteq p^\epsilon L$, where $\epsilon=1$ for odd $p$ and $\epsilon=2$ for $p=2$. Finite freeness is part of this convention: merely requiring a small <Lie bracket> on a torsion module would not correspond to <uniform pro-p groups>.

= Lie algebra of a uniform pro-p group
{c}
{parent=Powerful Lie algebra over p-adic integers}
{title2=$L_G$}

= Intrinsic Lie algebra of a uniform pro-p group
{synonym}

On the set underlying a <uniform pro-p group>, define scalar multiplication by $\lambda x=x^\lambda$, and define addition and the <Lie bracket> by
$$
x+_Ly=\lim_{n\to\infty}(x^{p^n}y^{p^n})^{p^{-n}},\qquad [x,y]_L=\lim_{n\to\infty}[x^{p^n},y^{p^n}]^{p^{-2n}}.
$$
Roots are the unique roots in the appropriate power layers. The limits give a <powerful Lie lattice>. In a suitable complete normed <group algebra>, the <p-adic logarithm> identifies these operations with ordinary addition and the associative-algebra commutator.

= BCH convergence on a powerful Lie lattice
{c}
{parent=Powerful Lie algebra over p-adic integers}
{title2=$x*y=\operatorname{BCH}(x,y)$}

The <Baker--Campbell--Hausdorff formula> converges integrally on a <powerful Lie lattice>. A degree-$m$ Lie term has bracket divisibility $p^{\epsilon(m-1)}$; the standard integral coefficient bound loses at most $\lfloor(m-1)/(p-1)\rfloor$ powers of $p$. The remaining valuation tends to infinity. Associativity follows from the formal exponential identity, the identity is zero, the inverse is $-x$, and $x^{*n}=nx$. The resulting <pro-p group> is a <uniform pro-p group>, with power subgroups $p^nL$.

= Cross-product Lie algebra
{parent=Lie algebra}
{title2=$(\mathbb R^3,\times)$}

The <vector space> $\mathbb R^3$ with the <cross product> as <Lie bracket>. The vector triple-product identity proves the <Jacobi identity>. The map $x\mapsto-(i/2)x\cdot\sigma$ is a <Lie algebra isomorphism> to the compact real <SU(2) Lie algebra>, using the <Pauli matrix commutator identity>.

= Euclidean motion Lie algebra in two dimensions
{parent=Lie algebra}
{c}
{title2=$\mathfrak{iso}(2)$}

The real <Lie algebra> $\mathfrak{iso}(2)$ is a semidirect product of planar translations with infinitesimal rotations. One orientation convention is $[J,E_1]=-E_2$, $[J,E_2]=E_1$, $[E_1,E_2]=0$. Its translation space is a two-dimensional abelian <ideal of a Lie algebra>.

= Killing form of the planar Euclidean motion Lie algebra
{parent=Euclidean motion Lie algebra in two dimensions}
{c}
{title2=$[\kappa]=\operatorname{diag}(-2,0,0)$}

In the ordered basis $(J,E_1,E_2)$ of the <Euclidean motion Lie algebra in two dimensions>, the <Killing form> has matrix $\operatorname{diag}(-2,0,0)$. Its radical is the translation ideal, and the algebra is solvable rather than semisimple.

= Graded Lie algebra
{parent=Lie algebra}

A <graded Lie algebra> has a <graded Lie bracket> preserving total degree, with $[u,v]=-(-1)^{|u||v|}[v,u]$ and $[u,[v,w]]=[[u,v],w]+(-1)^{|u||v|}[v,[u,w]]$ for homogeneous vectors in the indicated degrees. A <Gerstenhaber algebra> uses this sign rule after shifting degrees by one.

= Graded Lie bracket
{parent=Graded Lie algebra}
{title2=$[-,-]$}

The bracket on a <graded Lie algebra> is a bilinear operation satisfying graded antisymmetry and the graded <Jacobi identity>. For homogeneous elements of degrees $r,s$, $[u,v]=-(-1)^{rs}[v,u]$. A <Gerstenhaber bracket> uses this convention on the shifted degrees $r=|u|-1$ and $s=|v|-1$.

= Central extension of a Lie algebra
{parent=Lie algebra}
{title2=$0\to\mathfrak z\to\widehat{\mathfrak g}\to\mathfrak g\to0$}

= Lie algebra central extension
{c}
{synonym}

= Central extensions of a Lie algebra
{synonym}

A central extension is a <Lie algebra> surjection whose <kernel> commutes with every element of the larger algebra. The <Virasoro central extension> adds a central generator to the <Witt algebra>.

= Witt algebra
{c}
{parent=Lie algebra}
{title2=$[\ell_m,\ell_n]=(m-n)\ell_{m+n}$}
{wiki}

The Witt algebra is the <Lie algebra> of vector fields on a circle with Laurent-polynomial coefficients. Its nontrivial <central extension of a Lie algebra> is the <Virasoro algebra>.

= Centralizer of an element of a Lie algebra
{parent=Lie algebra}
{title2=$Z_{\mathfrak g}(x)$}

= Lie algebra centralizer
{c}
{synonym}

For an element $x$ of a <Lie algebra> $\mathfrak g$, its centralizer is the <Lie subalgebra> $Z_{\mathfrak g}(x)=\{y\in\mathfrak g:[y,x]=0\}$, equivalently the <kernel> of $\operatorname{ad}x$ in the <adjoint representation>.

= Centralizer lower bound for a root vector
{parent=Centralizer of an element of a Lie algebra}

In a complex <semisimple Lie algebra> whose <rank of a semisimple Lie algebra> is $\ell$, a <root vector> $x$ satisfies $\dim Z_{\mathfrak g}(x)\geq3\ell-2$. To see this, select $\ell-1$ roots whose images form a basis of the real root span modulo $\mathbb R\alpha$, where $x\in\mathfrak g_\alpha$. Take the highest endpoints of the <root strings> through both signs of each selected root. Their $2(\ell-1)$ distinct <root spaces> commute with $x$. Together with $\ker\alpha\subset\mathfrak t$ and $\mathbb Cx$, these give $2(\ell-1)+(\ell-1)+1$ independent vectors in the <Lie algebra centralizer>.

= Lie algebra generator
{c}
{parent=Lie algebra}

A Lie algebra generator is an element of a chosen spanning or generating set for a Lie algebra. In a matrix representation, an infinitesimal group transformation has the form $1+i\alpha_at^a+O(\alpha^2)$ in terms of generator matrices $t^a$.

= Lie algebra of a matrix Lie group
{c}
{parent=Lie algebra}

For a <matrix Lie group> $G$, its tangent space $\mathfrak g=T_I G$ is closed under the matrix <commutator>. Indeed, for $X,Y\in\mathfrak g$, the group commutator
$$
e^{tX}e^{sY}e^{-tX}e^{-sY}
$$
lies in $G$, and the mixed derivative at $(0,0)$ of its matrix logarithm is $XY-YX$. Thus $[X,Y]=XY-YX$ belongs to $\mathfrak g$.

= General linear Lie algebra
{parent=Lie algebra of a matrix Lie group}
{title2=$\mathfrak{gl}(V)=\operatorname{End}(V)$}

The <endomorphisms> of a <vector space> form a <Lie algebra> under the <commutator> $[A,B]=AB-BA$. It is the <Lie algebra> of the <general linear group>; the <Jacobi identity> follows by expanding associative products.

= Unitary Lie algebra
{parent=Lie algebra of a matrix Lie group}
{title2=$\mathfrak u(N)$}

= Lie algebra of the unitary group
{synonym}
{c}

The real <Lie algebra> of the <unitary group> is $\mathfrak u(N)=\{X:X^\dagger=-X\}$, of real dimension $N^2$. Differentiating unitarity gives necessity; exponentiating any <skew-Hermitian matrix> gives a curve in the group, proving sufficiency.

= Special unitary Lie algebra
{parent=Unitary Lie algebra}
{title2=$\mathfrak{su}(n)$}

The real <Lie algebra> of the <special unitary group> consists of traceless <skew-Hermitian matrices>. Differentiating unitarity and determinant one gives the two constraints; exponentiating any such matrix proves their sufficiency. The <commutator> is again skew-Hermitian and traceless. Its real dimension is $n^2-1$, and its complexification is $\mathfrak{sl}_n(\mathbb C)$.

= Trace normalization of su(2)
{parent=Special unitary Lie algebra}
{title2=$e_a=-i\sigma_a/\sqrt2,\quad[e_a,e_b]=\sqrt2\varepsilon_{abc}e_c$}

With the defining two-dimensional <matrix trace> and <inner product> $\langle X,Y\rangle=-\operatorname{tr}(XY)$, the displayed <Pauli matrix> basis is orthonormal. The <Pauli matrix multiplication law> gives the indicated <structure constants of a Lie algebra>. With the alternative form $-2\operatorname{tr}(XY)$, the orthonormal basis is $-i\sigma_a/2$ and the structure constant is one. The bracket and inner-product normalizations must be kept consistent in projected gauge-field formulas.

= SU(3) Lie algebra
{parent=Unitary Lie algebra}
{c}
{title2=$\mathfrak{su}(3)$}

The compact real <Lie algebra> $\mathfrak{su}(3)$ consists of traceless anti-Hermitian $3\times3$ matrices. Physicists often specify Hermitian generators $t_a$ instead, so the genuine <Lie algebra> elements are $it_a$. The compact real algebra and its complexification $\mathfrak{sl}_3(\mathbb C)$ should be distinguished.

= Root SU(2) subalgebras of SU(3)
{parent=SU(3) Lie algebra}

Each <root> $\alpha$ of the <SU(3) Lie algebra> defines an <SU(2) Lie algebra> generated by $J_3=\alpha\cdot H$ and $J_\pm=\sqrt2 E_{\pm\alpha}$ in the root-length-one convention. They obey $[J_3,J_\pm]=\pm J_\pm$ and $[J_+,J_-]=2J_3$. These subalgebras explain <root> strings, <Weyl group> reflections and the integer <Dynkin labels> of <highest weights>.

// Target: lie-theory.bigb

= Matrix-unit basis of the unitary Lie algebra
{parent=Unitary Lie algebra}
{title2=$iE_{ii},\ E_{ij}-E_{ji},\ i(E_{ij}+E_{ji})$}

A real basis of the <unitary Lie algebra> is $iE_{ii}$, $E_{ij}-E_{ji}$ and $i(E_{ij}+E_{ji})$ for $i<j$, where $E_{ij}$ are <matrix units>. These separately span the imaginary diagonal and real and imaginary upper off-diagonal entries.

= Lie algebra isomorphism
{parent=Lie algebra}

A Lie algebra isomorphism is a bijective <linear map> $\varphi:\mathfrak g\to\mathfrak h$ satisfying $\varphi([x,y])=[\varphi(x),\varphi(y)]$. Its inverse automatically preserves the <Lie bracket>.

= Direct sum of Lie algebras
{parent=Lie algebra}

The direct sum $\mathfrak g\oplus\mathfrak h$ has componentwise bracket
$$
[(x,y),(x',y')]=([x,x'],[y,y']).
$$
The two summands are commuting <ideal of a Lie algebra>[ideals].

= Lie subalgebra
{parent=Lie algebra}

A Lie subalgebra is a <vector subspace> closed under the <Lie bracket>.

= Normalizer of a Lie subalgebra
{title2=$N_L(H)$}
{parent=Lie subalgebra}

The normalizer of a <Lie subalgebra> $H\subseteq L$ is
$$
N_L(H)=\{x\in L:[x,H]\subseteq H\}.
$$
It is the largest Lie subalgebra of $L$ in which $H$ is an <ideal of a Lie algebra>.

= Derivation of a Lie algebra
{parent=Lie algebra}
{title2=$D[x,y]=[Dx,y]+[x,Dy]$}

= Derivations of a Lie algebra
{synonym}

A derivation of a <Lie algebra> is an <algebraic derivation>, namely a <linear map> $D:L\to L$ satisfying
$$
D[x,y]=[Dx,y]+[x,Dy].
$$
Every adjoint map $\operatorname{ad}z:x\mapsto[z,x]$ is a derivation.

= Inner derivation of a Lie algebra
{parent=Derivation of a Lie algebra}
{title2=$\operatorname{ad}\mathfrak g\subseteq\operatorname{Der}(\mathfrak g)$}

= Inner derivations of a Lie algebra
{synonym}

An inner <derivation of a Lie algebra> is $\operatorname{ad}_x:y\mapsto[x,y]$. The <Jacobi identity> makes this a <derivation of a Lie algebra>. For every <derivation of a Lie algebra> $D$, $[D,\operatorname{ad}_x]=\operatorname{ad}_{Dx}$, so the inner derivations form an <ideal of a Lie algebra> in the <derivation Lie algebra>. The <kernel> of $x\mapsto\operatorname{ad}_x$ is the <center of a Lie algebra>.

= Derivation Lie algebra
{parent=Derivation of a Lie algebra}
{title2=$\operatorname{Der}(\mathfrak g)$}

The <derivations of a Lie algebra> form a <Lie subalgebra> of $\operatorname{End}(\mathfrak g)$ with the <commutator> bracket. The identity $[D,\operatorname{ad}x]=\operatorname{ad}(Dx)$ makes the inner derivations an <ideal of a Lie algebra>. For a complex <semisimple Lie algebra>, the <Weyl complete reducibility theorem> and the vanishing <center of a Lie algebra> imply $\operatorname{Der}(\mathfrak g)=\operatorname{ad}\mathfrak g$.

= Outer derivation of a nilpotent Lie algebra
{parent=Derivation of a Lie algebra}

Every nonzero finite-dimensional nilpotent Lie algebra over a field of characteristic zero has a derivation outside its adjoint image. This remains true for characteristically nilpotent Lie algebras: all their derivations may be nilpotent, but they cannot all be inner.

= Generalized-eigenspace bracket lemma
{parent=Derivation of a Lie algebra}

If $D$ is a <derivation of a Lie algebra>, then its <generalized eigenspace>[generalized eigenspaces] satisfy
$$
[L_\lambda,L_\mu]\subseteq L_{\lambda+\mu}.
$$
Apply a sufficiently large power of $D-(\lambda+\mu)I$ and use the <binomial theorem> together with the derivation rule.

= Invariant bilinear form on a Lie algebra
{parent=Lie algebra}

A bilinear form $B$ on a Lie algebra is invariant when
$$
B([x,y],z)=B(x,[y,z]).
$$
For a symmetric form this says that every adjoint map is skew-adjoint with respect to $B$.

= Invariance of a bilinear form on a Lie algebra
{parent=Invariant bilinear form on a Lie algebra}
{title2=$B([x,y],z)=B(x,[y,z])$}

For a symmetric <bilinear form> on a <Lie algebra>, invariance is the displayed identity. It equivalently says that each <adjoint representation> endomorphism is skew-adjoint with respect to $B$. The <Killing form> has this property by cyclic invariance of the <trace>.

= Positive invariant metric on a Lie algebra
{parent=Invariant bilinear form on a Lie algebra}

A positive invariant metric is a positive-definite real symmetric <invariant bilinear form on a Lie algebra>. Each adjoint map is skew-adjoint. Invariance gives $[\mathfrak g,\mathfrak g]^\perp=\mathfrak z(\mathfrak g)$, so the algebra splits orthogonally into its Abelian center and its derived algebra. The latter has zero center, and $\kappa(X,X)=\operatorname{tr}(\operatorname{ad}_X^2)=-\|\operatorname{ad}_X\|^2$ is strictly negative for nonzero $X$. Thus it is compact semisimple; the whole algebra is compact reductive. Conversely, a compact semisimple summand has metric $-\kappa$ and the center can carry any positive metric. The <Killing form> alone vanishes on the center.

= Lie superalgebra
{parent=Lie algebra}
{wiki}

A Lie superalgebra is a Z2-graded vector space with a graded-antisymmetric bracket satisfying the graded Jacobi identity. The bracket of two odd elements is symmetric and is conventionally written as an anticommutator.

= Lie superalgebra representation
{parent=Lie superalgebra}

= Superalgebra representation
{synonym}

A Lie superalgebra representation acts on a parity-graded vector space by linear operators preserving the graded brackets. Odd generators reverse parity, and their brackets are represented by <anticommutators>.

= Graded Jacobi identity
{parent=Lie superalgebra}

The <Jacobi identity> of a <Lie superalgebra> includes parity signs. In particular, for odd $c$ and even $\phi$ it gives $[c,[c,\phi]]=\tfrac12[[c,c],\phi]$.

= Lie bracket
{title2=$[x,y]$}
{c}
{parent=Lie algebra}
{wiki}

The Lie bracket is the product in a <Lie algebra>. For matrix Lie algebras it is the <commutator> $[X,Y]=XY-YX$.

= Antisymmetry of a Lie bracket
{parent=Lie bracket}
{title2=$[x,y]=-[y,x]$}

The relation $[x,y]=-[y,x]$ for a bilinear <Lie bracket>. In characteristic zero it implies $[x,x]=0$. In characteristic two, the alternating identity must be required separately when defining a <Lie algebra>.

= Lie bracket from local group commutators
{parent=Lie bracket}
{c}
{title2=$[X,Y]=\partial_s\partial_t\log(e^{sX}e^{tY}e^{-sX}e^{-tY})|_{s=t=0}$}

In a <local exponential chart>, the mixed second differential of the <group commutator> defines a <bilinear map> on the <tangent space> at the identity. Inversion after swapping the two group elements proves antisymmetry. Differentiating conjugation gives $[X,Y]=\operatorname{ad}(X)Y$; applying <naturality of the Lie bracket> to the <adjoint representation of a Lie group> then proves the <Jacobi identity>. For a matrix group this recovers $XY-YX$ by expanding through the mixed term.

= Commutator
{title2=$[A,B]$}
{parent=Lie bracket}
{wiki=Commutator}

= Matrix commutator
{synonym}

The commutator of two elements of an associative algebra is $[A,B]=AB-BA$. Matrix Lie algebras use the commutator as their <Lie bracket>.

= Commutator expansion for exponential conjugation
{parent=Commutator}
{title2=$e^ABe^{-A}=\sum_{n\ge0}\operatorname{ad}_A^n(B)/n!$}

For bounded <linear operators>, or formally in an associative algebra,
$$
e^{\lambda A}Be^{-\lambda A}=\sum_{n\ge0}\frac{\lambda^n}{n!}\operatorname{ad}_A^n(B),\qquad \operatorname{ad}_A(B)=[A,B].
$$
Differentiate the two exponentials using the product rule: the result is $e^{\lambda A}[A,B]e^{-\lambda A}$. Induction gives the $n$th derivative $e^{\lambda A}\operatorname{ad}_A^n(B)e^{-\lambda A}$, and the <Taylor series> at zero proves the formula. For bounded operators convergence follows from $\|\operatorname{ad}_A^n(B)\|\le(2\|A\|)^n\|B\|$. For unbounded quantum operators, use a common invariant domain and justify the series there, or use the finite-dimensional system of commutators when their span closes.

= Trace of a matrix commutator
{parent=Commutator}
{title2=$\operatorname{tr}[A,B]=0$}

The <matrix trace> of $[A,B]=AB-BA$ is zero, since $\operatorname{tr}(AB)=\sum_{i,j}A_{ij}B_{ji}=\operatorname{tr}(BA)$. This obstructs a matrix with nonzero trace from being a single <commutator>.

= Commutator derivation identity
{parent=Commutator}
{title2=$[A,BC]=[A,B]C+B[A,C]$}

Expand both sides using the definition of a <commutator>: the middle terms $BAC$ cancel, leaving $ABC-BCA$. Thus commutation with a fixed operator is a <derivation of an algebra>. In particular, $[A,[B,C]]=[[A,B],C]+[B,[A,C]]$, which gives a short way to derive a <Lie algebra> representation from generator actions on an underlying algebra.

= Jacobi identity
{c}
{parent=Lie bracket}
{wiki}

The Jacobi identity is
$$
[x,[y,z]]+[y,[z,x]]+[z,[x,y]]=0.
$$

= Abelian Lie algebra
{parent=Lie algebra}
{wiki}

A Lie algebra is abelian when every <Lie bracket> vanishes.

= Semidirect product of a Lie algebra and a module
{title2=$\mathfrak g\ltimes V$}
{parent=Lie algebra}

For a <Lie algebra representation> of $\mathfrak g$ on $V$, the semidirect product $\mathfrak g\ltimes V$ is the vector space $\mathfrak g\oplus V$ with bracket
$$
[(x,v),(y,w)]=([x,y],xw-yv).
$$
The subspace $V$ is an abelian <ideal of a Lie algebra>.

= Killing form of a semidirect product with a module
{parent=Semidirect product of a Lie algebra and a module}

For a finite-dimensional representation $\rho:\mathfrak g\to\mathfrak{gl}(V)$,
$$
K_{\mathfrak g\ltimes V}((x,v),(y,w))
=K_{\mathfrak g}(x,y)+\operatorname{tr}(\rho(x)\rho(y)).
$$
In particular, $V$ lies in the radical of the Killing form.

= Ideal of a Lie algebra
{parent=Lie algebra}

= Lie algebra ideal
{c}
{synonym}

= Ideals of a Lie algebra
{synonym}

A vector subspace $I\subseteq\mathfrak g$ is an ideal when $[\mathfrak g,I]\subseteq I$. It is therefore the kernel of a Lie-algebra quotient map.

= Quotient Lie algebra
{parent=Ideal of a Lie algebra}
{title2=$\mathfrak g/I$}

For a <Lie algebra ideal> $I$, the quotient <vector space> $\mathfrak g/I$ has <Lie bracket> $[x+I,y+I]=[x,y]+I$. The ideal condition makes this independent of representatives. Its <Lie algebra ideals> correspond to the ideals of $\mathfrak g$ containing $I$, by inverse image under the quotient <Lie algebra homomorphism>.

= Derived series of a Lie algebra
{parent=Lie algebra}

The derived series is $\mathfrak g^{(0)}=\mathfrak g$ and $\mathfrak g^{(n+1)}=[\mathfrak g^{(n)},\mathfrak g^{(n)}]$.

= Derived algebra
{parent=Derived series of a Lie algebra}
{title2=$\mathcal D(\mathfrak g)=[\mathfrak g,\mathfrak g]$}

The derived algebra is the linear span of all <Lie brackets> $[x,y]$ in a <Lie algebra>. The <Jacobi identity> makes it an <ideal of a Lie algebra>. Its quotient is the largest <abelian Lie algebra> quotient of $\mathfrak g$.

= Derived algebra nilpotence criterion
{parent=Derived algebra}

A finite-dimensional complex <Lie algebra> is a <solvable Lie algebra> exactly when its <derived algebra> is a <nilpotent Lie algebra>. The forward direction follows from the <Lie theorem> in the <adjoint representation> and lifting nilpotence through its central kernel. The reverse direction follows because the <derived series of a Lie algebra> after its first term is the <derived series of a Lie algebra> of the <derived algebra>.

= Solvable Lie algebra
{parent=Derived series of a Lie algebra}
{wiki}

A Lie algebra is solvable when its derived series eventually becomes zero.

= Affine Lie algebra of the line
{parent=Solvable Lie algebra}
{title2=$[h,e]=e$}

The two-dimensional <Lie algebra> of infinitesimal dilations and translations of a line has a <basis> $h,e$ with $[h,e]=e$. Its <derived algebra> is the abelian line spanned by $e$, but every nontrivial term of its <lower central series of a Lie algebra> is that same line. It is solvable and not nilpotent.

= Solvable radical
{parent=Solvable Lie algebra}
{wiki=Radical_of_a_Lie_algebra}

The solvable radical of a finite-dimensional <Lie algebra> is its largest solvable <ideal of a Lie algebra>. A Lie algebra is <semisimple Lie algebra>[semisimple] exactly when its solvable radical is zero.

= Lie theorem
{c}
{parent=Solvable Lie algebra}
{wiki=Lie's_theorem}

Every finite-dimensional complex representation of a solvable Lie algebra has a common eigenvector. Equivalently, an irreducible finite-dimensional complex representation is one-dimensional; iterating gives simultaneous upper triangularization.

= Infinite-dimensional simple module for the two-dimensional affine Lie algebra
{parent=Lie theorem}
{title2=$x=t,\quad yf(t)=f(t-1)$}

The <solvable Lie algebra> $\mathbb Cx\oplus\mathbb Cy$, with $[x,y]=y$, has an infinite-dimensional <irreducible Lie algebra representation> on $\mathbb C[t]$: $xf(t)=tf(t)$ and $yf(t)=f(t-1)$. Indeed $[x,y]f=yf$. An <invariant subspace> is an ideal $p(t)\mathbb C[t]$ because it is invariant under multiplication by $t$. Shift invariance gives $p(t)\mid p(t-1)$, hence $p(t-1)=p(t)$ and $p$ is constant. This shows why the <Lie theorem> needs finite-dimensional representations, even when the algebra itself is finite-dimensional.

= Simultaneous triangularization of a Lie algebra representation
{parent=Lie theorem}
{title2=$0=V_0\subset V_1\subset\cdots\subset V_n=V$}

A <Lie algebra representation> is simultaneously upper triangular precisely when it preserves a <complete flag>, with $\dim V_j=j$. A <basis> adapted to the invariant flag makes every representing matrix upper triangular. For a complex finite-dimensional representation of a <solvable Lie algebra>, the <Lie theorem> provides a common <eigenvector>; iteration on the <quotient representations> constructs the flag.

= Failure of Lie theorem in positive characteristic
{parent=Lie theorem}

In a <field> of <characteristic> $p$, take a <basis> $e_0,\ldots,e_{p-1}$, and let $xe_j=e_{j-1}$ with indices modulo $p$, while $ye_j=je_j$. Then $[x,y]=x$, so $kx+ky$ is a <solvable Lie algebra>, but the distinct <eigenvalues> of $y$ force any common <eigenvector> to be a coordinate vector, and $x$ cyclically permutes those vectors. There is consequently no common <eigenvector>, even over an <algebraically closed field>.

= Cartan solvability criterion
{c}
{parent=Solvable Lie algebra}
{wiki=Cartan's_criterion}

A complex Lie subalgebra $\mathfrak h\subseteq\mathfrak{gl}(V)$ is solvable if
$$
\operatorname{tr}(xy)=0
$$
for every $x\in[\mathfrak h,\mathfrak h]$ and $y\in\mathfrak h$. For an abstract Lie algebra, this is equivalent to $\kappa(\mathfrak h,[\mathfrak h,\mathfrak h])=0$.

= Conjugate-spectrum proof of Cartan solvability
{parent=Cartan solvability criterion}
{c}
{title2=$\operatorname{tr}(xy)=\sum_\lambda\dim(V_\lambda)|\lambda|^2$}

For $x$ in the <derived algebra> of a complex matrix <Lie algebra> $L$, define $y$ by multiplication by $\overline\lambda$ on each <generalized eigenspace> $V_\lambda$ of $x$. <Polynomial interpolation> and <adjoint compatibility of additive Jordan decomposition> make $\operatorname{ad}y$ a <polynomial> in $\operatorname{ad}x$ with zero constant term, so $[y,L]\subseteq[L,L]$. Trace orthogonality of $[L,L]$ and $L$ forces the displayed sum to vanish. Thus $x$ is a <nilpotent endomorphism>, and the <Engel theorem> implies solvability. The auxiliary $y$ and the Jordan components are not required to lie in $L$.

= Center of a Lie algebra
{title2=$Z(\mathfrak g)$}
{parent=Lie algebra}
{wiki=Center_(group_theory)#Lie_algebras}

The center is $Z(\mathfrak g)=\{z:[z,x]=0\text{ for every }x\in\mathfrak g\}$.

= Central ideal
{parent=Center of a Lie algebra}

A central ideal is a <linear subspace> of the <center of a Lie algebra>. It is a <Lie algebra ideal> because its <Lie bracket> with every element is zero. Passing to the <quotient Lie algebra> removes this central subspace. For $\mathfrak{sl}_p$ in <characteristic> $p$, the identity matrix has zero <trace> and spans a central ideal.

= Lower central series of a Lie algebra
{title2=$(\gamma_i(\mathfrak g))$}
{parent=Lie algebra}

The lower central series is defined by $\gamma_1(\mathfrak g)=\mathfrak g$ and $\gamma_{i+1}(\mathfrak g)=[\mathfrak g,\gamma_i(\mathfrak g)]$.

= Nilpotent Lie algebra
{parent=Lower central series of a Lie algebra}
{wiki}

A <Lie algebra> is nilpotent when its <lower central series of a Lie algebra>[lower central series] eventually becomes zero.

= Killing form of a nilpotent Lie algebra vanishes
{c}
{parent=Nilpotent Lie algebra}
{title2=$\kappa\equiv0$}

Every <adjoint representation> operator raises the <lower central series of a Lie algebra> filtration. In a basis adapted to that finite filtration, all adjoint operators are strictly triangular. Their products have zero trace, so the <Killing form> vanishes. Consequently its <radical of the Killing form> is the whole algebra. For strictly upper triangular matrices the filtration can be seen directly by the gap between the column and row indices.

// Target: algebra.bigb

= Upper central series of a Lie algebra
{parent=Nilpotent Lie algebra}
{title2=$Z_0=0,\quad Z_{i+1}/Z_i=Z(\mathfrak g/Z_i)$}

Each term is the preimage of the <center of a Lie algebra> after quotienting by the preceding term. Equivalently, $Z_{i+1}=\{x:[\mathfrak g,x]\subseteq Z_i\}$. A <Lie algebra> is nilpotent exactly when this series reaches the whole algebra after finitely many steps. For the <Heisenberg Lie algebra>, the series is $0\subset\mathbb Cc\subset\mathfrak g$.

= Two-dimensional subalgebra criterion for Lie algebra nilpotence
{parent=Nilpotent Lie algebra}

Over an <algebraically closed field>, a finite-dimensional <Lie algebra> is nilpotent exactly when every two-dimensional <Lie subalgebra> is abelian. A nonnilpotent <adjoint representation> matrix has a nonzero <eigenvalue> $\lambda$ with <eigenvector> $y$, so $[x,y]=\lambda y$ generates a nonabelian two-dimensional subalgebra. Conversely a subalgebra of a <nilpotent Lie algebra> is nilpotent, while a nonabelian two-dimensional algebra has a basis $u,v$ with $[u,v]=v$ and a nonnilpotent adjoint matrix. Algebraic closure is what guarantees the required eigenvector.

= Normalizer condition for a nilpotent Lie algebra
{parent=Nilpotent Lie algebra}

Every proper <Lie subalgebra> $H$ of a finite-dimensional <nilpotent Lie algebra> is properly contained in its <normalizer of a Lie subalgebra>[normalizer]. Choose the first term of the lower central series contained in $H$; the preceding term contains an element outside $H$ that normalizes it.

= Engel theorem
{c}
{parent=Nilpotent Lie algebra}
{wiki=Engel's_theorem}

A finite-dimensional Lie algebra is nilpotent if and only if every adjoint map $\operatorname{ad}_x$ is nilpotent. A Lie algebra of nilpotent endomorphisms can be simultaneously represented by strictly upper triangular matrices.

= Engel lemma
{c}
{parent=Engel theorem}

A finite-dimensional <Lie algebra representation> in which every representing endomorphism is <nilpotent linear map>[nilpotent] has a nonzero vector annihilated by the entire Lie algebra. Applying this to the <adjoint representation> is the key step in <Engel theorem>.

= Engel normalizer lemma
{parent=Engel lemma}
{c}
{title2=$M\subsetneq N_L(M)$}

If $L$ is a finite-dimensional <Lie algebra> of <nilpotent endomorphisms>, every proper <Lie subalgebra> $M$ is strictly contained in its <normalizer of a Lie subalgebra>. The <adjoint representation> of $M$ on $L/M$ consists of nilpotent maps by <nilpotence of commutation by a nilpotent endomorphism>. In the inductive proof of <Engel theorem>, the lower-dimensional <Engel lemma> gives a nonzero class $y+M$ with $[M,y]\subseteq M$. A maximal proper $M$ is consequently an <ideal of a Lie algebra> of codimension one.

= Lie algebra homomorphism
{c}
{parent=Lie algebra}

A Lie algebra homomorphism is a <linear map> $f$ satisfying $f([x,y])=[f(x),f(y)]$.

= Differential of a Lie group homomorphism preserves Lie brackets
{parent=Lie algebra homomorphism}
{title2=$df_e([X,Y])=[df_eX,df_eY]$}

= Naturality of the Lie bracket
{synonym}

For a smooth homomorphism of <Lie groups>, naturality gives $f(\exp X)=\exp(df_eX)$. Therefore $\log(f(g))=df_e(\log g)$ locally. Apply this to the <group commutator> and differentiate its two curve parameters to obtain the displayed identity for the <Lie bracket from local group commutators>. In particular the differential of a group representation is a <Lie algebra representation>.

= Lie algebra representation
{c}
{parent=Lie algebra homomorphism}
{wiki}

A representation of $\mathfrak g$ on a <vector space> $V$ is a <Lie algebra homomorphism> $\rho:\mathfrak g\to\mathfrak{gl}(V)$.

= Defining representation of a matrix Lie algebra
{parent=Lie algebra representation}
{title2=$X\cdot v=Xv$}

= Defining representation
{synonym}

For a <Lie subalgebra> $\mathfrak g\subseteq\mathfrak{gl}(V)$, its defining representation is its given action on the <vector space> $V$. The <Lie bracket> identity $[X,Y]v=X(Yv)-Y(Xv)$ verifies the representation property. For the <special linear Lie algebra>, diagonal trace-zero matrices have coordinate <weights of a representation> $L_i$ on the standard basis; for a <special orthogonal Lie algebra>, the same action preserves the defining symmetric <bilinear form>.

= Hermitian quantum generator convention
{c}
{parent=Lie algebra representation}
{title2=$H_X=i\rho(X)$}

For a unitary derived representation $\rho$, its generators are anti-Hermitian. Define Hermitian observables $H_X=i\rho(X)$; then their ordinary operator <commutator> is $[H_X,H_Y]=iH_{[X,Y]}$. Thus real <Lie algebra> structure constants and Hermitian-operator commutation relations differ by an $i$. Products in a <universal enveloping algebra> require one conversion factor for every generator. In particular, the abstract bilinear Pauli-Lubanski element has representation image minus the pseudovector formed from the Hermitian physical generators.

= Lie algebra representation homomorphism
{parent=Lie algebra representation}
{c}
{title2=$T(xv)=xT(v)$}

A linear map between <Lie algebra representations> is a representation homomorphism when it commutes with the action of every element. Its <kernel> and <image of a linear map> are invariant subspaces. This is a map of modules, distinct from a <Lie algebra homomorphism> between the acting algebras themselves.

= Exterior-power Lie algebra representation
{parent=Lie algebra representation}
{title2=$x\cdot(v_1\wedge\cdots\wedge v_r)=\sum_i v_1\wedge\cdots\wedge xv_i\wedge\cdots\wedge v_r$}

A <Lie algebra representation> on $V$ induces one on each <exterior power> by acting on every factor and summing. The <tensor product of Lie algebra representations> preserves the defining alternating relations, so this descends to the quotient in every <characteristic of a field>. In <characteristic two> define the <exterior algebra> by the relations $v\wedge v=0$, rather than by dividing a tensor antisymmetrizer by $2$.

= Nilpotent Lie algebras need not act nilpotently
{parent=Lie algebra representation}

The <abelian Lie algebra> $kI\subseteq\operatorname{End}(V)$, for $V\ne0$, is a <nilpotent Lie algebra> but contains the nonnilpotent <identity map>. Thus nilpotence of the abstract <Lie algebra> does not imply that a particular <Lie algebra representation> consists of <nilpotent endomorphisms>. <Engel theorem> requires nilpotence of every represented endomorphism to obtain strict upper triangularity.

= Tensor product of Lie algebra representations
{parent=Lie algebra representation}
{title2=$V\otimes W$}

The <tensor product> of <Lie algebra representations> has action $x(v\otimes w)=xv\otimes w+v\otimes xw$. The <Lie algebra representation> identity follows because operators on the two different tensor factors commute. This action descends to <exterior powers> and <symmetric powers>.

= SU(3) triplet-octet decomposition
{parent=Tensor product of Lie algebra representations}
{c}
{title2=$\mathbf3\otimes\mathbf8=\mathbf{15}\oplus\overline{\mathbf6}\oplus\mathbf3$}

The summands have <Dynkin labels> $(2,1)$, $(0,2)$ and $(1,0)$. Convolving the triplet and octet <weight diagrams> and applying <highest-weight character subtraction> removes these three characters in that order. Their dimensions $15+6+3=24$ match the product.

// Target: quantum-field-theory.bigb

= sl2 tensor product of cubic and quadratic symmetric powers
{parent=Tensor product of Lie algebra representations}
{title2=$S^3V\otimes S^2V\cong\Gamma_5\oplus\Gamma_3\oplus\Gamma_1$}

For the defining two-dimensional <sl2 Lie algebra> module with coordinates $x,y$, put $u_{ij}=x^{3-i}y^i\otimes x^{2-j}y^j$. Its three <highest-weight vectors> are $u_{00}$, $u_{10}-u_{01}$ and $u_{20}-2u_{11}+u_{02}$, of weights five, three and one. Lowering the last once gives $u_{30}-2u_{21}+u_{12}$, an explicit second vector of its two-dimensional irreducible summand.

= Action map of a Lie algebra representation
{parent=Tensor product of Lie algebra representations}
{title2=$a:\mathfrak g\otimes V\to V,\quad x\otimes v\mapsto xv$}

Give $\mathfrak g$ its <adjoint representation>. The representation identity gives $a(y\cdot(x\otimes v))=[y,x]v+xyv=y(xv)$, so the action map is a <Lie algebra representation homomorphism>. For a nontrivial simple module it is surjective. If the acting algebra is semisimple and the module is finite dimensional, <Weyl complete reducibility theorem> splits the surjection and makes $V$ a direct summand.

= Action summand can fail for an infinite-dimensional simple module
{parent=Action map of a Lie algebra representation}

Take the <Verma module> for the <sl2 Lie algebra> of highest weight $-2$, with basis $v_m$ and $fv_m=v_{m+1}$, $hv_m=(-2-2m)v_m$, $ev_m=-m(m+1)v_{m-1}$. It is simple. In $\mathfrak{sl}_2\otimes V$, the highest vectors of weight $-2$ are multiples of $w=e\otimes v_1-h\otimes v_0=f(e\otimes v_0)$. Every homomorphism to $V$ kills $e\otimes v_0$, whose weight is zero, and hence kills $w$. Every embedded copy of $V$ would have its highest vector proportional to $w$, so no projection onto such a copy can exist. Thus semisimplicity of the Lie algebra alone does not provide an action summand in infinite dimension.

= Tensor product of the standard representation with its exterior square
{parent=Tensor product of Lie algebra representations}
{title2=$V\otimes\Lambda^2V\cong\mathbb S_{(2,1)}V\oplus\Lambda^3V$}

For $V=\mathbb C^n$, $n\ge3$, the <tensor product of Lie algebra representations> for the <special linear Lie algebra> splits as displayed. The <exterior product> map $v\otimes(u\wedge w)\mapsto v\wedge u\wedge w$ has an equivariant right inverse obtained by dividing its three-term alternating insertion by three. Its <kernel> is the <Schur functor> of shape $(2,1)$, with <highest weight> $\omega_1+\omega_2$ and <dimension> $n(n^2-1)/3$. The other summand has <highest weight> $\omega_3$ for $n\ge4$ and is trivial for $n=3$.

The <weight space> of $2\varepsilon_i+\varepsilon_j$ has <dimension> one when $i\ne j$. The <weight space> of $\varepsilon_i+\varepsilon_j+\varepsilon_k$ has <dimension> three for distinct indices. Restricting these functionals to the traceless diagonal <Cartan subalgebra> creates no additional coincidences; for $n=3$ the latter weight is zero.

= sl3 highest-weight tensor rule
{parent=Tensor product of Lie algebra representations}
{title2=$\Gamma_{a,b}\otimes\Gamma_{1,0}\cong\Gamma_{a+1,b}\oplus\Gamma_{a-1,b+1}\oplus\Gamma_{a,b-1}$}

For the complex <special linear Lie algebra> $\mathfrak{sl}_3$, tensoring an irreducible <highest-weight representation> by the defining representation gives the displayed sum, omitting terms with negative <Dynkin labels>. It follows by multiplying its <Weyl character formula> by $x_1+x_2+x_3$. In particular $\Gamma_{2,1}\otimes\Gamma_{1,0}=\Gamma_{3,1}\oplus\Gamma_{1,2}\oplus\Gamma_{2,0}$, with dimensions $24+15+6=45$.

= Integration of a Lie-algebra representation
{parent=Lie algebra representation}

For a finite-dimensional real <Lie algebra>, a finite-dimensional <Lie-algebra representation> integrates uniquely to a smooth representation of the connected <simply connected> <Lie group> with that <Lie algebra>. For another connected group with the same <Lie algebra>, integration is possible exactly when the representation of its universal cover is trivial on the covering kernel. This separates local Lie-algebra classification from global representation restrictions.

= Lie-invariant bilinear form
{c}
{parent=Lie algebra representation}
{title2=$B(xv,w)+B(v,xw)=0$}

A <bilinear form> on a <Lie algebra representation> is invariant when it satisfies the displayed identity. Equivalently, $T_B(v)=B(v,-)$ is an <intertwining operator> from the representation to its <dual Lie algebra representation>. For a finite-dimensional <irreducible representation> over an <algebraically closed field>, any nonzero such form is <nondegenerate>, and the <Schur lemma> makes all invariant forms proportional. In <characteristic> different from two, transposing twice then proves that the form is a <symmetric bilinear form> or an <alternating bilinear form>.

= Dual Lie algebra representation
{parent=Lie algebra representation}
{title2=$\rho^*(x)=-\rho(x)^T$}

The dual of a <Lie algebra representation> on $V$ acts on its <dual space> by $(x\phi)(v)=-\phi(xv)$. The minus sign makes this a <Lie algebra homomorphism>: taking transposes reverses the order in a <commutator>. It is the infinitesimal version of the <dual representation> of a <group>.

= Highest weight of a dual representation
{parent=Dual Lie algebra representation}
{title2=$-w_0\lambda$}

For a finite-dimensional irreducible representation $L(\lambda)$ of a complex <semisimple Lie algebra>, its <dual Lie algebra representation> has <highest weight> $-w_0\lambda$, where $w_0$ is the <longest Weyl-group element>. Indeed duality negates all <weights>, and $w_0\lambda$ is the lowest weight of the original module. In particular $w_0=-I$ makes every such irreducible module self-dual.

= Trivial Lie algebra representation
{parent=Lie algebra representation}

The trivial representation sends every element of the <Lie algebra> to the zero linear map. For a complex <semisimple Lie algebra>, the irreducible representation of <highest weight> zero is the one-dimensional trivial representation.

= Hom representation
{title2=$\operatorname{Hom}(V,W)$}
{c}
{parent=Lie algebra representation}

For <Lie algebra representations> $V$ and $W$, the Hom representation on $\operatorname{Hom}(V,W)$ is
$$
(x\cdot f)(v)=x\cdot f(v)-f(x\cdot v).
$$
Its invariant vectors are exactly the intertwining maps.

= Automorphism of a Lie algebra
{parent=Lie algebra representation}

= Lie algebra automorphism
{c}
{synonym}

An automorphism of a Lie algebra is an invertible linear map $f:\mathfrak g\to\mathfrak g$ satisfying $f([X,Y])=[f(X),f(Y)]$.

= Branching rule
{parent=Lie algebra representation}
{wiki}

A branching rule decomposes a representation into irreducible representations after restriction from a Lie group or Lie algebra to a subgroup or subalgebra.

= Adjoint branching to root sl2 subalgebras in rank two
{title2=$\mathfrak g\downarrow_{\mathfrak{sl}_2(\alpha)}$}
{parent=Branching rule}

A <root string> of length $\Lambda+1$ gives an irreducible <sl2 Lie algebra> module $R(\Lambda)$ when restricting the <adjoint representation> to the <sl2 subalgebra associated with a root>. Include also the root's own $R(2)$ and one commuting Cartan direction $R(0)$. For $A_2$ either simple root gives $R(2)\oplus2R(1)\oplus R(0)$. For $B_2$, the long root gives $R(2)\oplus2R(1)\oplus3R(0)$ and the short root gives $3R(2)\oplus R(0)$. For $G_2$, the long root gives $R(2)\oplus4R(1)\oplus3R(0)$ and the short root gives $R(2)\oplus2R(3)\oplus3R(0)$.

= Structure constant of a Lie algebra
{title2=$f_{ab}{}^c$}
{parent=Lie algebra representation}

= Structure constants of a Lie algebra
{synonym}

= Lie algebra structure constant
{synonym}
{c}

For a basis $(T_a)$ of a Lie algebra, its structure constants are defined by $[T_a,T_b]=f_{ab}{}^cT_c$.

= Antisymmetry of Killing-lowered structure constants
{parent=Structure constant of a Lie algebra}
{title2=$c_{abc}=\kappa([t_a,t_b],t_c)$}

Lower the output index of the <structure constants of a Lie algebra> with its <Killing form>. The <Lie bracket> makes the result antisymmetric in the first two indices, while invariance gives $\kappa([t_a,t_b],t_c)=\kappa(t_a,[t_b,t_c])$, making it antisymmetric in the last two indices. These two adjacent transpositions imply total antisymmetry. Nondegeneracy of the <Killing form> is unnecessary.

// Target: lie-theory.bigb

= Faithful Lie algebra representation
{c}
{parent=Lie algebra representation}

A <Lie algebra representation> is faithful when its representing homomorphism is <injective function>[injective].

= Ado theorem
{parent=Faithful Lie algebra representation}
{c}
{wiki=Ado's_theorem}

= Ado's theorem
{synonym}
{c}

Every finite-dimensional <Lie algebra> over a <field> of <characteristic zero> has a finite-dimensional faithful <Lie algebra representation>. Thus it embeds as a Lie subalgebra of a <matrix algebra>, using the <matrix commutator> as bracket. Faithfulness is crucial for detecting central elements: the <adjoint representation> alone kills the <center of a Lie algebra>.

= Irreducible Lie algebra representation
{c}
{parent=Lie algebra representation}

A nonzero <Lie algebra representation> is irreducible when it has no proper nonzero invariant <vector subspace>[subspace].

= Adjoint representation of a Lie algebra
{title2=$\operatorname{ad}$}
{c}
{parent=Lie algebra representation}

= Adjoint representation
{c}
{synonym}

The adjoint representation is $\operatorname{ad}_x(y)=[x,y]$. For a semisimple <Lie algebra>, its nonzero weights are the roots and its zero-weight space is the <Cartan subalgebra>.

= Adjoint representation of SU(3)
{parent=Adjoint representation of a Lie algebra}
{title2=$\mathbf8=R(1,1)$}

= SU(3) adjoint representation
{c}
{synonym}

The complex <adjoint representation> of <SU(3)> is the action by conjugation on traceless $3\times3$ matrices. With $H_1=\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,0)$ and $H_2=\operatorname{diag}(0,1,-1)$, its <highest-weight vector> is $E_{13}$ of <Dynkin labels> $(1,1)$. The six off-diagonal matrix units have weights $(2,-1),(-2,1),(-1,2),(1,-2),(1,1),(-1,-1)$, and the two-dimensional <Cartan subalgebra> has weight zero. In coordinates $(h_1,(h_1+2h_2)/\sqrt3)$, the <weight diagram> is a regular hexagon together with a central point of <weight multiplicity> two. This representation describes both the <gluon> colour multiplet and the approximate flavour <baryon octet>, under different physical <SU(3)> symmetries.

= Killing form
{title2=$\kappa(X,Y)$}
{c}
{parent=Adjoint representation of a Lie algebra}
{wiki}

The Killing form is
$$
\kappa(X,Y)=\operatorname{Tr}(\operatorname{ad}_X\operatorname{ad}_Y).
$$
It is symmetric and invariant: $\kappa([X,Y],Z)=\kappa(X,[Y,Z])$.

= Killing form of the general linear Lie algebra
{c}
{parent=Killing form}
{title2=$\kappa(X,Y)=2n\operatorname{tr}(XY)-2\operatorname{tr}X\operatorname{tr}Y$}

For the <general linear Lie algebra> of all $n\times n$ matrices over a <field> of <characteristic zero>, write $\operatorname{ad}_X=L_X-R_X$ on the matrix space. The traces of $L_XL_Y$ and $R_XR_Y$ are $n\operatorname{tr}(XY)$, whereas both mixed traces are $\operatorname{tr}X\operatorname{tr}Y$. Expanding gives the displayed <Killing form>. Its radical is the scalar matrices because $\kappa(X,Y)=0$ for every $Y$ forces $nX-\operatorname{tr}(X)I=0$. Thus the full general linear algebra is not semisimple, even though its traceless summand is semisimple for $n\ge2$.

// Target: lie-theory.bigb

= Killing-form invariance under a Lie-group adjoint action
{c}
{parent=Killing form}
{title2=$\kappa(\operatorname{Ad}_gX,\operatorname{Ad}_gY)=\kappa(X,Y)$}

Every Lie algebra automorphism $S$ obeys $\operatorname{ad}_{SX}=S\operatorname{ad}_XS^{-1}$. The <cyclic property of the trace> therefore makes the <Killing form> invariant under $S$. For a matrix <Lie group>, $S=\operatorname{Ad}_g$ is conjugation by $g$, proving the displayed identity even when the group is disconnected. This directly proves <gauge invariance> of Killing-form contractions of adjoint fields.

= Killing form for cyclic three-generator brackets
{c}
{parent=Killing form}
{title2=$B=\operatorname{diag}(-2ac,-2ab,-2bc)$}

For $[e_1,e_2]=ae_3$, $[e_2,e_3]=be_1$, and $[e_3,e_1]=ce_2$, the <adjoint representation> <matrices> give the displayed <Killing form>. Cross traces vanish. Nonzero $a,b,c$ make it nondegenerate and hence the algebra semisimple. If the three constants have the same sign it is negative-definite; mixed signs give signature $(2,1)$. In particular $(a,b,c)=(2,2,2)$ is the compact $\mathfrak{su}(2)$ case, whereas $(2,2,-2)$ is the split real traceless two-by-two algebra. This gives a direct <compactness criterion from the Killing form> in a small explicit example.

= Orthogonal ideal splitting for a nondegenerate Killing form
{parent=Killing form}
{title2=$\mathfrak g=I\oplus I^\perp$}

For an <ideal of a Lie algebra> $I$ and nondegenerate <Killing form>, invariance makes $I^\perp$ an ideal. An element of $I\cap I^\perp$ commutes with $I$, so the intersection is an abelian ideal. <Abelian ideals lie in the radical of the Killing form>, forcing the intersection to vanish. Thus $\mathfrak g=I\oplus I^\perp$ as commuting ideals. Iterating minimal nonzero ideals gives the direct-sum formulation of a <semisimple Lie algebra>.

= Radical of the Killing form
{parent=Killing form}
{title2=$\operatorname{rad}B$}

The <radical of a bilinear form> of the <Killing form> is the <ideal of a Lie algebra> $\{x:B(x,y)=0\text{ for all }y\}$. It is zero for a complex <semisimple Lie algebra>. Every element of the <center of a Lie algebra> lies in this radical.

= Compactness criterion from the Killing form
{parent=Killing form}

A finite-dimensional real <Lie algebra> has negative-definite <Killing form> exactly when it is compact semisimple. In the compact direction, average an <inner product> over a compact group; all adjoint maps are skew, so $\kappa(X,X)=-\|\operatorname{ad}_X\|^2$, and semisimplicity removes the center. Conversely, negative-definiteness gives the positive invariant metric $-\kappa$, embeds the faithful adjoint algebra in an orthogonal algebra, and nondegeneracy gives semisimplicity. This is an algebraic compactness criterion, not a claim that every global group with an Abelian compact-type algebra is compact.

= Abelian ideals lie in the radical of the Killing form
{parent=Killing form}

If $\mathfrak a$ is an abelian <ideal of a Lie algebra>, then $\operatorname{ad}_x\operatorname{ad}_y$ maps $\mathfrak g$ into $\mathfrak a$ and vanishes on $\mathfrak a$ whenever $x\in\mathfrak a$. Its trace is zero, so $\kappa(\mathfrak a,\mathfrak g)=0$. Every nonzero solvable ideal has a last nonzero term in its <derived series of a Lie algebra>, which is such an abelian ideal. Consequently a nondegenerate <Killing form> forces the <solvable radical> to vanish.

= Solvability of the radical of the Killing form
{c}
{parent=Killing form}

The radical $\mathfrak g^\perp=\{x:\kappa(x,\mathfrak g)=0\}$ of the Killing form is a solvable ideal. Invariance makes it an ideal, and the <Cartan solvability criterion> applied to its adjoint image proves solvability.

= Cartan criterion for semisimplicity
{c}
{parent=Killing form}

A finite-dimensional complex <Lie algebra> is <semisimple Lie algebra>[semisimple] if and only if its <Killing form> is <nondegenerate bilinear form>[nondegenerate].

= Killing radical is a solvable ideal
{c}
{parent=Cartan criterion for semisimplicity}
{title2=$\operatorname{rad}B_L\subseteq\operatorname{rad}L$}

For a finite-dimensional complex <Lie algebra> $L$, the radical $R$ of its <Killing form> is a <Lie algebra ideal> by invariance. For $x,y\in R$, their adjoint actions vanish on $L/R$, so $B_R(x,y)=B_L(x,y)=0$. The <Cartan solvability criterion> makes the image $\operatorname{ad}_R(R)$ solvable; its kernel is the abelian center, so $R$ is solvable. A <semisimple Lie algebra> has no nonzero solvable ideal, forcing its Killing radical to vanish.

= Uniqueness of an invariant bilinear form on a simple Lie algebra
{parent=Killing form}

Every invariant bilinear form on a finite-dimensional complex simple Lie algebra is a scalar multiple of its Killing form. A nondegenerate invariant form identifies the algebra with its dual; comparing this identification with the Killing form gives an endomorphism of the irreducible adjoint representation, so <Schur lemma> makes it scalar.

= Real-simple exception to uniqueness of the Killing form
{parent=Uniqueness of an invariant bilinear form on a simple Lie algebra}

The usual uniqueness theorem assumes a complex simple <Lie algebra>, or a real form with simple complexification. For a complex simple algebra regarded as real, both $\operatorname{Re}\kappa_{\mathbb C}$ and $\operatorname{Im}\kappa_{\mathbb C}$ define a real symmetric <invariant bilinear form on a Lie algebra>, and they are linearly independent. The real <Killing form> is $2\operatorname{Re}\kappa_{\mathbb C}$. For $\mathfrak{sl}_2(\mathbb C)$ and $H=\operatorname{diag}(1,-1)$, $\kappa_{\mathbb C}(H,H)=8$ and $\kappa_{\mathbb C}(H,iH)=8i$, proving the independence. This real algebra is simple: its real complexification is two complex simple ideals exchanged by conjugation, and a real ideal would complexify to a conjugation-stable sum of these ideals, hence either zero or the whole algebra.

= Killing form of the special linear Lie algebra
{title2=$\kappa(X,Y)=2n\operatorname{tr}(XY)$}
{c}
{parent=Killing form}

For $\mathfrak{sl}_n(\mathbb C)$,
$$
\kappa(X,Y)=2n\operatorname{tr}(XY).
$$

= Adjoint irreducibility of a simple Lie algebra
{parent=Adjoint representation of a Lie algebra}

Every invariant subspace of the <adjoint representation of a Lie algebra> is an ideal. Hence the adjoint representation of a <simple Lie algebra> is irreducible.

= Trace form of a Lie algebra representation
{title2=$(\cdot,\cdot)_V$}
{c}
{parent=Lie algebra representation}

For a finite-dimensional <Lie algebra representation> $\rho:\mathfrak g\to\mathfrak{gl}(V)$, its trace form is the symmetric bilinear form
$$
(x,y)_V=\operatorname{tr}(\rho(x)\rho(y)).
$$
It is invariant: $([x,y],z)_V=(x,[y,z])_V$.

= Cubic trace tensor of a Lie algebra representation
{parent=Trace form of a Lie algebra representation}
{title2=$B(X,Y,Z)=\operatorname{Tr}(d(X)d(Y)d(Z))$}

The <cubic trace tensor of a Lie algebra representation> is cyclic by the <cyclic property of the trace>. It need not be fully antisymmetric. Its antisymmetric part in the first two arguments is fixed by the <trace form of a Lie algebra representation>:
$$
B(X,Y,Z)-B(Y,X,Z)=H([X,Y],Z).
$$

// Target: lie-theory.bigb

= Killing-normalized contraction of a cubic trace tensor
{parent=Cubic trace tensor of a Lie algebra representation}
{c}
{title2=$c_a{}^{mn}B_{mnb}=-\frac12H_{ab}$}

Lowering and raising <structure constants> with the <Killing form> gives $c_a{}^{mn}c_{mn}{}^r=-\delta_a{}^r$. Since $c_a{}^{mn}$ is antisymmetric in $m,n$, only the <commutator> part of the <cubic trace tensor of a Lie algebra representation> contributes, proving $c_a{}^{mn}B_{mnb}=-H_{ab}/2$. In an adapted compact basis with $\kappa_{ab}=-\delta_{ab}$ and $H_{ab}=-\mu\delta_{ab}$, the resulting sign is positive: $+\mu\delta_{ab}/2$. Defining the lowered structure constants with a positive Euclidean metric instead would reverse this sign.

// Target: lie-theory.bigb

= Invariance identity for a cubic trace tensor
{parent=Cubic trace tensor of a Lie algebra representation}

The <trace> of a <commutator> gives
$$
B([W,X],Y,Z)+B(X,[W,Y],Z)+B(X,Y,[W,Z])=0.
$$
In a basis, cyclicity converts this to $c_{da}{}^\ell B_{bc\ell}+c_{db}{}^\ell B_{ca\ell}+c_{dc}{}^\ell B_{ab\ell}=0$.

// Target: lie-theory.bigb

= Positive trace index for a compact simple Lie algebra
{parent=Trace form of a Lie algebra representation}
{title2=$\operatorname{Tr}(d(X)d(Y))=\mu\kappa(X,Y),\quad\mu>0$}

For a nontrivial finite-dimensional <anti-Hermitian> <Lie algebra representation> of a <compact Lie algebra> that is simple, its <trace form of a Lie algebra representation> is a positive multiple of the <Killing form>. Define $T$ by $H(X,Y)=\kappa(TX,Y)$. Invariance makes $T$ commute with the <adjoint representation of a Lie algebra>. Symmetry makes $T$ self-adjoint for $-\kappa$, so its <eigenspaces> are ideals. Simplicity implies $T=\mu I$. The kernel of the representation is an ideal and hence vanishes; therefore $H(X,X)=-\operatorname{Tr}(d(X)^\dagger d(X))<0$ for nonzero $X$, proving $\mu>0$.

// Target: lie-theory.bigb

= Trace trilinear form of a Lie algebra representation
{title2=$B(X,Y,Z)$}
{parent=Lie algebra representation}

For a finite-dimensional representation $d$, the trilinear form $B(X,Y,Z)=\operatorname{Tr}(d(X)d(Y)d(Z))$ is invariant under simultaneous adjoint action. This follows by writing the sum of its three infinitesimal variations as the trace of a commutator.

= Heisenberg Lie algebra
{c}
{parent=Lie algebra}

The three-dimensional Heisenberg Lie algebra has a basis $a,b,c$ with $[a,b]=c$ and $c$ central. It is a two-step <nilpotent Lie algebra>.

= Heisenberg group
{c}
{parent=Heisenberg Lie algebra}
{wiki}

= Real Heisenberg group
{synonym}

The real Heisenberg group consists of upper-unitriangular $3\times3$ real matrices. In coordinates $(q,r,s)$ its multiplication is $(q,r,s)(q',r',s')=(q+q',r+r',s+s'+qr')$.

= Right-invariant coframe of the real Heisenberg group
{parent=Heisenberg group}
{title2=$dx,dy,dz-y\,dx$}

For the <Heisenberg group> law $(x,y,z)(a,b,c)=(x+a,y+b,z+c+xb)$, the right <Maurer-Cartan form> has coefficients $dx,dy,dz-y\,dx$. They satisfy $d\sigma^1=d\sigma^2=0$ and $d\sigma^3=\sigma^1\wedge\sigma^2$. A general <right-invariant Riemannian metric> is a constant positive-definite symmetric matrix in this coframe.

= Killing frame for a right-invariant Heisenberg metric
{parent=Right-invariant coframe of the real Heisenberg group}
{title2=$\partial_x,\partial_y+x\partial_z,\partial_z$}

Every <right-invariant Riemannian metric> on the <Heisenberg group> has <Killing vector fields> $\partial_x$, $\partial_y+x\partial_z$ and $\partial_z$. Their <Lie brackets of vector fields> reproduce $[X,Y]=Z$ and the central relations. Their flows are right translations. The dual frame of the right-invariant coframe is different and has the opposite Lie-bracket sign.

= Modular Heisenberg group
{parent=Heisenberg group}
{title2=$H_p=UT_3(\mathbb F_p)$}

The modular Heisenberg group consists of upper-unitriangular $3\times3$ matrices over the <finite field> $\mathbb F_p$. In coordinates $M(x,y,z)$, multiplication is $M(x,y,z)M(x\prime,y\prime,z\prime)=M(x+x\prime,y+y\prime+xz\prime,z+z\prime)$, and its order is $p^3$. Its <center of a group> is $\{M(0,y,0)\}\cong C_p$; the <quotient group> by the centre is $C_p\times C_p$, represented by the $x,z$ coordinates. The elementary generators $a=M(1,0,0)$, $b=M(0,0,1)$, $c=M(0,1,0)$ satisfy $a^p=b^p=c^p=e$, $aba^{-1}b^{-1}=c$, and $c$ central. For $p=2$, $(ab)^2=c$, so the whole group does not have exponent two.

= Left-invariant frame of the real Heisenberg group
{parent=Heisenberg group}

For multiplication $(x,y,z)(a,b,c)=(x+a,y+b,z+c+xb)$, the left-invariant frame is
$$
E_1=\partial_x,\qquad E_2=\partial_y+x\partial_z,\qquad E_3=\partial_z.
$$
Its <Lie brackets of vector fields> satisfy $[E_1,E_2]=E_3$ and the other basis brackets vanish.

= Heisenberg horizontal distribution
{c}
{parent=Left-invariant frame of the real Heisenberg group}
{title2=$D=\operatorname{span}(E_1,E_2)$}

The horizontal <smooth distribution> on the <real Heisenberg group> is spanned by $E_1=\partial_x$ and $E_2=\partial_y+x\partial_z$. It is not an <involutive distribution> because their bracket is $\partial_z$, but their brackets span the full tangent space. A horizontal curve satisfies $\dot x=\alpha$, $\dot y=\beta$, and $\dot z=x\beta$.

= Smooth horizontal reachability in the real Heisenberg group
{parent=Heisenberg horizontal distribution}

Every point $(P_x,P_y,P_z)$ can be reached from the identity by a smooth curve tangent to the <Heisenberg horizontal distribution>. Put $a=2(P_z-P_xP_y/2+P_x)$ and choose
$$
\alpha(t)=P_x+a\cos(2\pi t),\qquad
\beta(t)=P_y+2\pi\sin(2\pi t).
$$
Their integrals give $x(1)=P_x$, $y(1)=P_y$, and $z(1)=\int_0^1x(t)\beta(t)dt=P_xP_y/2-P_x+a/2=P_z$. The third coordinate measures the effect of the noncommuting horizontal directions; integrability would instead confine horizontal curves to a two-dimensional leaf.

= Integer Heisenberg group
{c}
{title2=$H(\mathbb Z)$}
{parent=Heisenberg group}

The integer Heisenberg group consists of upper-unitriangular three-by-three integer matrices. In coordinates $(x,y,z)$ its multiplication is
$$
(x,y,z)(x',y',z')=(x+x',y+y',z+z'+xy').
$$
It is the semidirect product $\mathbb Z^2\rtimes_A\mathbb Z$ for
$$
A=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\1&1\end{pmatrix},
$$
and its <abelianization> is $\mathbb Z^2$.

= Commutator identity in the integer Heisenberg group
{parent=Integer Heisenberg group}
{title2=$[a^r,b^s]=c^{rs}$}

Write the <integer Heisenberg group> with central generator $c=[a,b]$, so $ab=cba$. Moving $a$ successively past powers of $b$ gives
$$
a^r b^s a^{-r}b^{-s}=c^{rs}\qquad(r,s\in\mathbb Z).
$$
For positive exponents this follows by two inductions; inverses give the remaining signs. The representation $a=I+E_{12}$, $b=I+E_{23}$, $c=I+E_{13}$ checks the identity and shows $c$ has infinite order.

= Schrödinger representation of the Heisenberg group
{c}
{parent=Heisenberg group}

On $L^2(\mathbb R)$, translations and multiplication by phases give the Schrödinger representation of the Heisenberg group. The central coordinate acts by an overall phase.

= Polynomial representation of the Heisenberg Lie algebra
{c}
{parent=Heisenberg Lie algebra}

On $\mathbb C[x]$, the operators $d/dx$, multiplication by $x$, and the identity satisfy $[d/dx,x]=1$. Sending $a,b,c$ to these operators gives a faithful irreducible infinite-dimensional representation of the <Heisenberg Lie algebra>.

= Complexification of a Lie algebra
{parent=Lie algebra}

For a real Lie algebra $\mathfrak g$, its complexification is $\mathfrak g_{\mathbb C}=\mathfrak g\otimes_{\mathbb R}\mathbb C$ with the bracket extended complex-bilinearly.

= Complexification preserves semisimplicity
{parent=Complexification of a Lie algebra}
{title2=$\operatorname{rad}(\mathfrak g_{\mathbb C})=(\operatorname{rad}\mathfrak g)_{\mathbb C}$}

For a finite-dimensional real <Lie algebra> $\mathfrak g$, the <solvable radical> of its <complexification of a Lie algebra> is invariant under complex conjugation. Its real fixed subspace is a solvable ideal of $\mathfrak g$, and complexifying that fixed subspace recovers the whole radical. Conversely the complexification of any real solvable ideal is solvable. These inclusions prove the displayed identity; in particular $\mathfrak g$ is semisimple if and only if $\mathfrak g_{\mathbb C}$ is semisimple.

= Universal enveloping algebra
{title2=$U(\mathfrak g)$}
{parent=Lie algebra}
{wiki}

The universal enveloping algebra $U(\mathfrak g)$ is the associative algebra generated by $\mathfrak g$ subject to $xy-yx=[x,y]$. Its modules are the same as <Lie algebra representations>.

= Poincare-Birkhoff-Witt theorem
{c}
{parent=Universal enveloping algebra}
{wiki=Poincaré–Birkhoff–Witt_theorem}

For an ordered basis $(x_i)$ of a <Lie algebra> $\mathfrak g$, the ordered monomials $x_1^{a_1}\cdots x_n^{a_n}$ form a basis of $U(\mathfrak g)$. In particular, a triangular decomposition $\mathfrak g=\mathfrak n^-\oplus\mathfrak t\oplus\mathfrak n^+$ gives $U(\mathfrak g)\cong U(\mathfrak n^-)\otimes U(\mathfrak t)\otimes U(\mathfrak n^+)$ as vector spaces.

= Semisimple Lie algebra
{parent=Lie algebra}
{wiki}

A finite-dimensional <Lie algebra> is semisimple when it has no nonzero solvable ideals.

= Jordan decomposition in an abstract semisimple Lie algebra
{parent=Semisimple Lie algebra}
{c}
{title2=$x=x_s+x_n$}

= Absolute Jordan decomposition
{synonym}

A complex <semisimple Lie algebra> has an intrinsic <Jordan–Chevalley decomposition> $x=x_s+x_n$ characterized by $[x_s,x_n]=0$, a diagonalizable <adjoint representation> operator $\operatorname{ad}x_s$, and a nilpotent <adjoint representation> operator $\operatorname{ad}x_n$. The <adjoint representation> is faithful because the centre is zero. Closure of <semisimple matrix Lie algebras are closed under additive Jordan decomposition> applied to this faithful representation supplies the parts and their uniqueness. For every finite-dimensional <Lie algebra representation> $\rho$, the operators $\rho(x_s)$ and $\rho(x_n)$ are the semisimple and nilpotent parts of $\rho(x)$. Thus the decomposition is independent of a matrix embedding; for a nonfaithful representation it passes to the semisimple quotient by its kernel.

= Split semisimple Lie algebra
{parent=Semisimple Lie algebra}
{title2=$\mathfrak g=\mathfrak h\oplus\bigoplus_{\alpha}\mathfrak g_\alpha\quad\text{over the ground field}$}

A <semisimple Lie algebra> over a field of <characteristic zero> is split if it has a <Cartan subalgebra> whose adjoint action is simultaneously diagonalizable over that field. Its <root-space decomposition> then needs no extension of scalars. The <sl2R Lie algebra> is split, with the real diagonal <Cartan subalgebra>. The real <special unitary Lie algebra> $\mathfrak{su}(2)$ is not: its adjoint operators are skew-adjoint for the positive definite <inner product> obtained by negating its <Killing form>. Thus a split adjoint operator would have to be zero.

= Compact real form of a complex semisimple Lie algebra
{title2=$\mathfrak u\otimes_{\mathbb R}\mathbb C\cong\mathfrak g$}
{parent=Semisimple Lie algebra}

= Compact real form
{synonym}

A compact real form of a complex <semisimple Lie algebra> $\mathfrak g$ is a real <Lie subalgebra> $\mathfrak u$ whose <complexification of a Lie algebra> is $\mathfrak g$ and whose <Killing form> is negative definite. Its simply connected <Lie group> is compact. The positive internal <inner product> used in unitary gauge theory is therefore $-\kappa$. For $\mathfrak{sl}_2(\mathbb C)$ the <SU(2) Lie algebra> is a compact real form, whereas $\mathfrak{sl}_2(\mathbb R)$ is a different real form with indefinite Killing form.

= Perfect Lie algebra
{parent=Semisimple Lie algebra}
{wiki=Perfect_Lie_algebra}

A <Lie algebra> $L$ is perfect when $[L,L]=L$. Every semisimple Lie algebra over a field of characteristic zero is perfect.

= Weyl complete reducibility theorem
{c}
{parent=Semisimple Lie algebra}
{wiki}

Every finite-dimensional representation of a complex <semisimple Lie algebra> is a direct sum of irreducible representations. Equivalently, every invariant subspace has an invariant complement.

= Haar averaging produces invariant complements
{c}
{parent=Weyl complete reducibility theorem}
{title2=$V=W\oplus W^\perp$}

For a finite-dimensional continuous complex representation $R$ of a <compact group>, normalize <Haar measure> to mass one and average any positive-definite <Hermitian inner product>:
$$
\langle v,w\rangle_K=\int_K\langle R(g)v,R(g)w\rangle_0\,d\mu(g).
$$
The resulting inner product is positive-definite and invariant. If $W$ is an <invariant subspace>, then $W^\perp$ is invariant as well, since $\langle R(g)v,w\rangle_K=\langle v,R(g^{-1})w\rangle_K$. Thus $V=W\oplus W^\perp$. Restriction to a <compact real form>, followed by <integration of a Lie-algebra representation>, proves the <Weyl complete reducibility theorem>.

= Invariant complement from an equivariant projection
{parent=Weyl complete reducibility theorem}
{title2=$V=U\oplus\ker P$}

If a submodule $U$ of a <Lie algebra representation> $V$ admits an intertwining map $P:V\to U$ with $P|_U=I_U$, then $P^2=P$ and its <kernel> is an invariant complement. For a complex <semisimple Lie algebra>, the <Hom representation> on maps $V\to U$ whose restriction is a scalar identity reduces finding $P$ to <splitting of a trivial quotient for a semisimple Lie algebra>.

= Casimir splitting of a trivial quotient
{c}
{parent=Weyl complete reducibility theorem}

= Splitting of a trivial quotient for a semisimple Lie algebra
{synonym}

In a finite-dimensional <short exact sequence> $0\to N\to E\to\mathbb C\to0$ of representations of a complex <semisimple Lie algebra>, the generalized zero <eigenspace> of the <Casimir operator> maps onto the trivial quotient. All its irreducible <composition factors> have zero <Casimir eigenvalue>, so they are trivial. The action is therefore strictly upper triangular and has solvable image; because a <semisimple Lie algebra> is a <perfect Lie algebra>, that image is zero. Every lift in this generalized zero <eigenspace> is invariant, giving a <split short exact sequence>.

= Borel subalgebra
{c}
{parent=Semisimple Lie algebra}
{wiki}

For a choice of positive roots, the corresponding Borel subalgebra is $\mathfrak b=\mathfrak t\oplus\mathfrak n^+$, the sum of a <Cartan subalgebra> and all positive root spaces.

= Simple Lie algebra
{parent=Semisimple Lie algebra}
{wiki}

A nonabelian <Lie algebra> is simple when its only ideals are zero and the entire algebra.

= Special linear Lie algebra
{title2=$\mathfrak{sl}_n$}
{parent=Simple Lie algebra}
{wiki=Special_linear_Lie_algebra}

The special linear Lie algebra $\mathfrak{sl}_n$ consists of the trace-zero $n\times n$ matrices with the <commutator> bracket.

= sl2 triple
{parent=Special linear Lie algebra}
{title2=$[h,e]=2e,\quad[h,f]=-2f,\quad[e,f]=h$}

An sl2 triple consists of elements of a <Lie algebra> satisfying the displayed brackets. It defines a homomorphism from $\mathfrak{sl}_2$ by sending its standard generators to $h,e,f$. A nonzero homomorphism is injective because $\mathfrak{sl}_2$ is a <simple Lie algebra>. Root vectors and their coroot give such triples in a <semisimple Lie algebra>, and their representations supply finite <weight strings>.

= Symmetric powers of the defining sln representation
{parent=Special linear Lie algebra}
{title2=$\operatorname{Sym}^m\mathbb C^n=V(m\omega_1)$}

For $n\geq2$ and $m\geq0$, the representation $\operatorname{Sym}^m\mathbb C^n$ of $\mathfrak{sl}_n(\mathbb C)$ is irreducible with <highest weight> $m\omega_1$. Its monomial basis supplies weights by adding $m$ defining-representation weights, with repeated weights counted separately.

= sl3 decomposition of the symmetric-square dual tensor product
{parent=Symmetric powers of the defining sln representation}
{title2=$(S^2V)\otimes V^*\cong\Gamma_{2,1}\oplus\Gamma_{1,0}$}

For the defining three-dimensional <special linear Lie algebra> representation $V$, contraction $(uv)\otimes f\mapsto f(u)v+f(v)u$ maps $(S^2V)\otimes V^*$ onto $V$. The kernel contains the <highest-weight vector> $e_1^2\otimes e_3^*$ of <Dynkin labels> $(2,1)$. The <Weyl dimension formula> gives its irreducible module dimension $15$, the entire kernel dimension; the <Weyl complete reducibility theorem> supplies the displayed splitting.

= Weight basis of the trace-free symmetric-square dual tensor module
{parent=sl3 decomposition of the symmetric-square dual tensor product}
{title2=$\ker(S^2W\otimes W^*\to W)$}

Let $t_{ij}=(e_i\otimes e_j+e_j\otimes e_i)/2$, and let $f_i$ be the dual <basis>. The equivariant contraction sends $t_{ij}\otimes f_k$ to $\delta_{ik}e_j+\delta_{jk}e_i$. Its kernel has a <weight basis> consisting of $t_{ii}\otimes f_k$ for $i\ne k$, $t_{ij}\otimes f_k$ for three distinct indices with $i<j$, and $t_{ij}\otimes f_j-\tfrac12t_{ii}\otimes f_i$ for $i\ne j$. The three families have sizes six, three and six. This is the irreducible fifteen-dimensional <highest-weight representation> with <highest weight> $2L_1-L_3$.

= Exterior square of the sl3 representation of highest weight (2,1)
{parent=sl3 decomposition of the symmetric-square dual tensor product}
{title2=$\bigwedge^2\Gamma_{2,1}\cong\Gamma_{5,0}\oplus\Gamma_{2,3}\oplus\Gamma_{3,1}\oplus\Gamma_{1,2}\oplus\Gamma_{0,1}$}

The <formal character> identity $\chi_{\wedge^2U}(x)=\tfrac12(\chi_U(x)^2-\chi_U(x^2))$ counts unordered pairs of distinct basis vectors. Substituting $\chi_{2,1}=h_2(x)h_1(x^{-1})-h_1(x)$ and the <Weyl character formula> gives the displayed multiplicity-free decomposition. Its dimensions sum to $21+42+24+15+3=105=\binom{15}{2}$. The <sl3 interlacing character formula> provides an exact weight-enumeration check.

= Symmetric square of the sl3 representation of highest weight (2,1)
{parent=Special linear Lie algebra}
{title2=$S^2\Gamma_{2,1}$}

For the <special linear Lie algebra> $\mathfrak{sl}(3)$, with <Dynkin labels> indexing irreducible <highest-weight representations>,
$$
S^2\Gamma_{2,1}\cong\Gamma_{4,2}\oplus\Gamma_{3,1}\oplus\Gamma_{0,4}\oplus\Gamma_{1,2}\oplus\Gamma_{2,0}.
$$
The summands have dimensions $60,24,15,15,6$, summing to $120=15\cdot16/2$. The identity follows from the <formal character of a weight module> formula $\chi_{S^2W}(z)=(\chi_W(z)^2+\chi_W(z^2))/2$ and the <Weyl character formula>.

= Matrix-unit extraction lemma for special linear ideals
{parent=Special linear Lie algebra}

Over a <field> of <characteristic> different from two, any <Lie algebra ideal> in $\mathfrak{sl}_n$ containing a nonscalar <matrix> is the whole <special linear Lie algebra>. Indeed, if $A_{ji}\ne0$ for $i\ne j$, then $[E_{ij},[E_{ij},A]]=-2A_{ji}E_{ij}$ extracts a <matrix unit>. For a nonscalar diagonal <matrix>, $[E_{ij},A]=(A_{jj}-A_{ii})E_{ij}$ extracts one instead. Its bracket with $E_{ji}$ produces $H=E_{ii}-E_{jj}$, and $[H,E_{ji}]=-2E_{ji}$ extracts the reverse unit. Brackets $[E_{ij},E_{jk}]=E_{ik}$ and $[E_{ki},E_{ij}]=E_{kj}$ spread these units to every row and column; opposite pairs span all trace-zero diagonal <matrices>. Thus only ideals consisting of scalar <matrices> can be proper. This proof works even when restrictions of distinct <roots of a root system> to a <Cartan subalgebra> coincide in positive <characteristic>.

= Fundamental representations of sl3
{title2=$V(\omega_1)=\mathbb C^3,\quad V(\omega_2)=\Lambda^2\mathbb C^3$}
{c}
{parent=Special linear Lie algebra}

The two fundamental representations of $\mathfrak{sl}_3$ are the defining representation $V(\omega_1)=\mathbb C^3$ and its dual
$$
V(\omega_2)=\Lambda^2\mathbb C^3\cong(\mathbb C^3)^*.
$$

= Tensor cube of the defining SU(3) representation
{parent=Fundamental representations of sl3}
{title2=$\mathbf3^{\otimes3}=\mathbf{10}\oplus\mathbf8\oplus\mathbf8\oplus\mathbf1$}

The <tensor product of Lie algebra representations> $\mathbf3\otimes\mathbf3$ splits into the symmetric $\mathbf6$ and antisymmetric $\overline{\mathbf3}$. Then $\mathbf6\otimes\mathbf3=\mathbf{10}\oplus\mathbf8$ and $\overline{\mathbf3}\otimes\mathbf3=\mathbf8\oplus\mathbf1$, as <highest-weight character subtraction> verifies. The decuplet is fully symmetric under quark permutations, the singlet fully antisymmetric, and the two octets constitute the two-dimensional mixed permutation multiplicity space. Applying the <Pauli constraint on three-quark flavour multiplets> gives one ground-state spin-one-half octet and one spin-three-halves decuplet.

// Target: physics.bigb

= Fundamental weights of sl3
{parent=Fundamental representations of sl3}
{title2=$\omega_1=\varepsilon_1,\quad\omega_2=-\varepsilon_3$}

For the <special linear Lie algebra> $\mathfrak{sl}_3(\mathbb C)$ with upper triangular <positive roots>, the <simple coroots> are $\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,0)$ and $\operatorname{diag}(0,1,-1)$. Their dual <fundamental weights> are $\omega_1(\operatorname{diag}(t_1,t_2,t_3))=t_1$ and $\omega_2(\operatorname{diag}(t_1,t_2,t_3))=t_1+t_2=-t_3$. In simple-root coordinates, $\omega_1=(2\alpha_1+\alpha_2)/3$ and $\omega_2=(\alpha_1+2\alpha_2)/3$. The primitive vectors $e_1\in\mathbb C^3$ and $e_1\wedge e_2\in\Lambda^2\mathbb C^3$ realize these <fundamental representations of sl3>.

= Triple tensor decomposition for the defining sl3 representation
{title2=$V\otimes V\otimes V^*$}
{c}
{parent=Fundamental representations of sl3}

For the defining representation $V=V(\omega_1)$ of $\mathfrak{sl}_3$,
$$
V\otimes V\otimes V^*
\cong
V(2\omega_1+\omega_2)\oplus
V(2\omega_2)\oplus
V(\omega_1)^{\oplus2}.
$$

= sl2 Lie algebra
{title2=$\mathfrak{sl}_2$}
{c}
{parent=Special linear Lie algebra}
{wiki=Representation_theory_of_sl2}

The Lie algebra $\mathfrak{sl}_2$ has generators $e,h,f$ with $[h,e]=2e$, $[h,f]=-2f$, and $[e,f]=h$.

= sl2R Lie algebra
{parent=sl2 Lie algebra}
{title2=$\mathfrak{sl}(2,\mathbb R)$}

The real traceless two-by-two matrices form a three-dimensional <Lie algebra>. A basis adapted to <conformal mechanics> obeys $\{D,E\}=-E$, $\{D,K\}=K$, $\{E,K\}=2D$.

= SU(2) Lie algebra
{title2=$\mathfrak{su}(2)$}
{c}
{parent=sl2 Lie algebra}

The SU(2) Lie algebra is the compact real form of $\mathfrak{sl}_2(\mathbb C)$. In the Hermitian physics convention its generators satisfy $[J_i,J_j]=i\epsilon_{ijk}J_k$.

= Killing form of the SU(2) Lie algebra
{parent=SU(2) Lie algebra}
{c}
{title2=$\kappa(T^a,T^b)=-2\delta^{ab}$}

In the anti-Hermitian basis $T^a=-i\sigma^a/2$, the <SU(2) Lie algebra> has $[T^a,T^b]=\varepsilon^{abc}T^c$ and <Killing form> $\kappa(T^a,T^b)=-2\delta^{ab}$. This is negative definite; the positive internal form for compact <Yang-Mills theory> is its negative.

= Classification of finite-dimensional sl2 representations
{parent=sl2 Lie algebra}

For every $n\geq0$ there is one irreducible $\mathfrak{sl}_2(\mathbb C)$-module $V(n)$ of dimension $n+1$, with weights $n,n-2,\ldots,-n$, each of multiplicity one. Every finite-dimensional representation is a direct sum of these modules.

= Parity unimodality of an sl2 character
{parent=Classification of finite-dimensional sl2 representations}
{title2=$c_j\ge c_{j+2}\quad(j\ge0)$}

For a finite-dimensional <sl2 Lie algebra> representation, write $\chi(q)=\sum_jc_jq^j$. Each irreducible summand has character $q^m+q^{m-2}+\cdots+q^{-m}$, so $c_j=c_{-j}$ and $c_j\ge c_{j+2}$ for nonnegative $j$. Thus each parity subsequence is symmetric and unimodal. If all weights have one parity, removing the unused exponents gives an ordinarily unimodal coefficient sequence. The full sequence including every integer exponent need not be unimodal: $q+q^{-1}$ has coefficients $1,0,1$.

= sl2 highest-weight lowering formula
{parent=Classification of finite-dimensional sl2 representations}
{title2=$XY^{k+1}v=(k+1)(m-k)Y^kv$}

In the <sl2 Lie algebra>, if $Xv=0$ and $Hv=mv$, then the <commutator> relations give $HY^jv=(m-2j)Y^jv$ and $XY^{k+1}v=(k+1)(m-k)Y^kv$. Induct using $XY=YX+H$. This controls the <raising operator> on a string formed by the <lowering operator> and yields the <classification of finite-dimensional sl2 representations>.

= Trace of a raising-lowering product in an irreducible sl2 module
{parent=sl2 highest-weight lowering formula}
{title2=$\operatorname{tr}(XY)=k(k+1)(k+2)/6$}

For a highest-weight vector $v$ of weight $k$ in an irreducible finite-dimensional <sl2 Lie algebra> module, the <basis> $v,Yv,\ldots,Y^kv$ diagonalizes $XY$, with eigenvalue $(j+1)(k-j)$ on $Y^jv$. Summing those eigenvalues gives $\operatorname{tr}(XY)=k(k+1)(k+2)/6$. The formula $XY^nv=n(k-n+1)Y^{n-1}v$ requires $v$ to be a <highest-weight vector>, not an arbitrary vector of the module.

= Invariant form on an irreducible sl2 module
{parent=Classification of finite-dimensional sl2 representations}

In the irreducible <sl2 Lie algebra> module $V(m)$, use $v_j=f^jv_0$, with $0\le j\le m$. The <Lie-invariant bilinear form> $B(v_j,v_k)=(-1)^j\mathbf1_{\{j+k=m\}}$ is <nondegenerate>: its anti-diagonal is nonzero. Invariance follows from $hv_j=(m-2j)v_j$, $fv_j=v_{j+1}$ and $ev_j=j(m-j+1)v_{j-1}$. Its transpose equals $(-1)^mB$, so it is a <symmetric bilinear form> when $m$ is an <even number>, and an <alternating bilinear form> when $m$ is an <odd integer>. The <Weyl complete reducibility theorem> gives a nondegenerate invariant form on any finite-dimensional module by taking the orthogonal <direct sum> of these forms.

= Injectivity of sl2 lowering above weight zero
{c}
{parent=Classification of finite-dimensional sl2 representations}

On any finite-dimensional $\mathfrak{sl}_2$-module, the lowering operator $f$ is injective from the $m$-weight space to the $(m-2)$-weight space whenever $m>0$. This follows on each irreducible summand from its standard weight string.

= Reducibility of an sl2 Verma module
{title2=$M_\lambda$ reducible iff $\lambda\in\mathbb Z_{\geq0}$}
{c}
{parent=sl2 Lie algebra}

The Verma module $M_\lambda$ has basis $(f^rv_\lambda)_{r\geq0}$ and
$$
e f^rv_\lambda=r(\lambda-r+1)f^{r-1}v_\lambda.
$$
It is reducible exactly when $\lambda=m\in\mathbb Z_{\geq0}$; then its unique proper nonzero submodule is generated by $f^{m+1}v_\lambda$ and is isomorphic to $M_{-m-2}$.

= Intermediate-series sl2 module
{c}
{parent=sl2 Lie algebra}

An intermediate-series $\mathfrak{sl}_2$-module has one-dimensional weight spaces indexed by all integers. In the basis $(w_i)_{i\in\mathbb Z}$, one useful normalization is
$$
hw_i=(a+2i)w_i,\qquad fw_i=w_{i-1},\qquad
ew_i=(b-ia-i(i+1))w_{i+1}.
$$
It is irreducible exactly when every raising coefficient is nonzero.

= Symplectic Lie algebra
{title2=$\mathfrak{sp}_{2n}$}
{parent=Simple Lie algebra}

For a nondegenerate alternating matrix $J$, the symplectic Lie algebra is $\mathfrak{sp}_{2n}=\{A:AJ+JA^T=0\}$.

= Tensor-square decomposition of the defining symplectic representation
{parent=Symplectic Lie algebra}
{title2=$V\otimes V=L(2\omega_1)\oplus L(\omega_2)\oplus L(0)$}

For $n\ge2$, the defining <Lie algebra representation> $V$ of $\mathfrak{sp}_{2n}$ has the displayed <tensor product> decomposition. The <crystal of the defining symplectic representation> and the <crystal tensor-product rule> give exactly these three highest vertices, each occurring once. The <symmetric square> is $L(2\omega_1)$ of <dimension> $n(2n+1)$. The <exterior square> splits into its invariant inverse-form line and the <primitive exterior square> $L(\omega_2)$ of <dimension> $n(2n-1)-1$. Their dimensions sum to $4n^2$. At $n=1$, the <exterior square> is only the invariant line, giving $L(2\omega_1)\oplus L(0)$.

= Compact symplectic Lie algebra
{parent=Symplectic Lie algebra}
{title2=$\mathfrak{usp}(2n)\cong\mathfrak{sp}(n)$}

= Lie algebra of the compact symplectic group
{synonym}
{c}

The <Lie algebra> of the <compact symplectic group> consists of $X^\dagger=-X$ and $XJ+JX^T=0$. Equivalently $X=\begin{pmatrix}A&B\\-\overline B&\overline A\end{pmatrix}$ with $A^\dagger=-A$ and $B^T=B$. Its real dimension is $n(2n+1)$, and its complexification is the <symplectic Lie algebra>.

= Matrix-unit basis of the compact symplectic Lie algebra
{parent=Compact symplectic Lie algebra}

In its block form, a <matrix-unit basis of the unitary Lie algebra> spans the anti-Hermitian block $A$, with the corresponding conjugate block below. Independent real and imaginary symmetric <matrix units> span $B$, with lower block $-\overline B$. These give $n^2+n(n+1)$ independent real generators, including diagonal symmetric entries.

= Symplectic root sl2 triple
{parent=Symplectic Lie algebra}
{title2=$(X_\alpha,H_\alpha,Y_\alpha)$}

For the <symplectic Lie algebra> $\mathfrak{sp}(4)$ preserving $J=\begin{pmatrix}0&I_2\\-I_2&0\end{pmatrix}$, write $h_1=E_{11}-E_{33}$ and $h_2=E_{22}-E_{44}$. Its positive <roots of a root system> have <sl2 subalgebra associated with a root> triples
$$
\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}
\alpha&X_\alpha&H_\alpha&Y_\alpha\\\hline
\varepsilon_1-\varepsilon_2&E_{12}-E_{43}&h_1-h_2&E_{21}-E_{34}\\
\varepsilon_1+\varepsilon_2&E_{14}+E_{23}&h_1+h_2&E_{41}+E_{32}\\
2\varepsilon_1&E_{13}&h_1&E_{31}\\
2\varepsilon_2&E_{24}&h_2&E_{42}
\end{array}
$$
The <matrix unit> commutator identity verifies $[X_\alpha,Y_\alpha]=H_\alpha$ and the two eigenvalue relations $[H_\alpha,X_\alpha]=2X_\alpha$, $[H_\alpha,Y_\alpha]=-2Y_\alpha$.

= Exceptional isomorphism between sp4 and so5
{parent=Symplectic Lie algebra}
{title2=$\mathfrak{sp}_4\cong\mathfrak{so}_5$}

Over $\mathbb C$, the <symplectic Lie algebra> on a four-dimensional <symplectic vector space> acts faithfully on its five-dimensional <primitive exterior square>, preserving its <symmetric bilinear form>. Faithfulness follows because an operator killing all wedges $e_i\wedge e_j$ has all off-diagonal coefficients zero and satisfies $A_{ii}+A_{jj}=0$ for every pair, hence is zero. The source and target <Lie algebras> both have <dimension> ten, so this faithful map is an <isomorphism> to the <special orthogonal Lie algebra> $\mathfrak{so}_5$.

= Tensor-square decomposition of the defining sp4 representation
{parent=Symplectic Lie algebra}

For the defining four-dimensional representation $V$ of the <symplectic Lie algebra> $\mathfrak{sp}_4$, with the short <simple root> numbered first, its <highest weight> is $\omega_1$. Its <tensor square> decomposes as
$$
V\otimes V\cong V(2\omega_1)\oplus V(\omega_2)\oplus V(0),
$$
with dimensions $10,5,1$. The first summand is the <symmetric square>; the other two form the <exterior square>, split by <symplectic contraction of an exterior square>.

= Cn root system
{title2=$C_n$}
{c}
{parent=Symplectic Lie algebra}

In an orthonormal basis $(\varepsilon_i)$, the $C_n$ roots are $\pm\varepsilon_i\pm\varepsilon_j$ for $i<j$ and $\pm2\varepsilon_i$.

= Cn Dynkin diagrams
{c}
{parent=Cn root system}
{title2=$C_n,\quad C_n^{(1)}$}

For $n\ge2$, the finite <Dynkin diagram> has a chain of <short roots> $\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_{n-1}$ with single bonds, followed by a double bond to the <long root> $\alpha_n$, pointing toward $\alpha_{n-1}$. The <extended Dynkin diagram> adds the long root $\alpha_0=-2\varepsilon_1$, doubly bonded to $\alpha_1$ with arrow toward $\alpha_1$. All other bonds remain. For $n=1$, the finite diagram is a single vertex; the affine diagram has two equal-length vertices joined by a double bond without an arrow, with <Cartan matrix> $\begin{pmatrix}2&-2\\-2&2\end{pmatrix}$.

= Positive-root data for Cn
{c}
{parent=Cn root system}
{title2=$\alpha_i=\varepsilon_i-\varepsilon_{i+1},\quad\alpha_n=2\varepsilon_n$}

Choose positive <roots of a root system> $\varepsilon_i-\varepsilon_j$, $\varepsilon_i+\varepsilon_j$ for $i<j$, and $2\varepsilon_i$. The <simple roots> are $\alpha_i=\varepsilon_i-\varepsilon_{i+1}$ for $i<n$ and $\alpha_n=2\varepsilon_n$. Their <coroots> are $\alpha_i^\vee=\varepsilon_i-\varepsilon_{i+1}$ and $\alpha_n^\vee=\varepsilon_n$. Therefore the <fundamental weights> and <Weyl vector> are
$$
\omega_i=\sum_{j=1}^i\varepsilon_j,\qquad \rho=\sum_{j=1}^n(n-j+1)\varepsilon_j.
$$
The <highest root> is $2\varepsilon_1=2\alpha_1+\cdots+2\alpha_{n-1}+\alpha_n$. These formulas include rank one.

= Cn Weyl group
{c}
{parent=Cn root system}
{title2=$W(C_n)\cong(\mathbb Z/2\mathbb Z)^n\rtimes S_n$}

The <Weyl group> of the <Cn root system> is the <signed symmetric group>. A <Weyl reflection> in $2\varepsilon_i$ changes the sign of coordinate $i$; one in $\varepsilon_i-\varepsilon_j$ interchanges coordinates $i,j$; one in $\varepsilon_i+\varepsilon_j$ interchanges and negates both coordinates. These generate all independent sign changes and all <permutations>, giving order $2^nn!$.

= C2 root system
{c}
{parent=Cn root system}
{title2=$C_2$}

In an orthonormal basis $(\varepsilon_1,\varepsilon_2)$, the roots are $\pm(\varepsilon_1-\varepsilon_2)$, $\pm(\varepsilon_1+\varepsilon_2)$, $\pm2\varepsilon_1$, and $\pm2\varepsilon_2$. Choose <simple roots> $\alpha_1=\varepsilon_1-\varepsilon_2$ and $\alpha_2=2\varepsilon_2$, with $\alpha_1$ a <short root>. The <fundamental weights> are $\omega_1=\varepsilon_1$ and $\omega_2=\varepsilon_1+\varepsilon_2$.

= C3 root system
{title2=$C_3$}
{c}
{parent=Cn root system}

The $C_3$ roots are $\pm\varepsilon_i\pm\varepsilon_j$ for $1\leq i<j\leq3$ and $\pm2\varepsilon_i$ for $1\leq i\leq3$. One root basis is $\alpha_1=\varepsilon_1-\varepsilon_2$, $\alpha_2=\varepsilon_2-\varepsilon_3$, $\alpha_3=2\varepsilon_3$. With $A_{ij}=\langle\alpha_i,\alpha_j^\vee\rangle$, its <Cartan matrix> is
$$
\begin{pmatrix}2&-1&0\\-1&2&-1\\0&-2&2\end{pmatrix}.
$$
The fundamental representation of highest weight $[1,0,0]$ is six-dimensional, with weights $\pm\varepsilon_1,\pm\varepsilon_2,\pm\varepsilon_3$.

= Weyl chamber geometry of C3
{c}
{parent=C3 root system}
{title2=$x_1>x_2>x_3>0$}

The <C3 root system> consists of $\pm2e_i$ and $\pm e_i\pm e_j$ in $\mathbb R^3$. Its <Weyl group> is the <signed symmetric group> on three coordinates, of order $48$. The reflecting planes are $x_i=0$ and $x_i=\pm x_j$. The open <Weyl chamber> $x_1>x_2>x_3>0$ has closure generated by $(1,0,0)$, $(1,1,0)$ and $(1,1,1)$. Sign choices and absolute-coordinate orderings give its $48$ translates. On the unit sphere the chamber is a spherical triangle with angles $\pi/2$, $\pi/3$, $\pi/4$, and hence area $\pi/12$; the areas of all $48$ triangles sum to $4\pi$.

= Cartan matrix convention for C3
{c}
{parent=C3 root system}
{title2=$A_{ij}=\langle\alpha_i,\alpha_j^\vee\rangle$}

For the <C3 root system>, take $\alpha_1=e_1-e_2$, $\alpha_2=e_2-e_3$, $\alpha_3=2e_3$. With columns indexing <coroots>, the <Cartan matrix> is
$$
A_{ij}=\frac{2(\alpha_i,\alpha_j)}{(\alpha_j,\alpha_j)},\qquad A=\begin{pmatrix}2&-1&0\\-1&2&-1\\0&-2&2\end{pmatrix}.
$$
The convention with rows indexing coroots gives $A^T$. Both describe a double bond between the short root $\alpha_2$ and long root $\alpha_3$, with arrow toward $\alpha_2$.

= Special orthogonal Lie algebra
{title2=$\mathfrak{so}_n$}
{c}
{parent=Simple Lie algebra}

The special orthogonal Lie algebra consists of the infinitesimal transformations preserving a nondegenerate symmetric bilinear form.

= Dn root system
{c}
{parent=Special orthogonal Lie algebra}
{title2=$D_n=\{\pm e_i\pm e_j:i<j\}$}

For the complexified <special orthogonal Lie algebra> $\mathfrak{so}_{2n}$, $n\geq2$, choose positive <roots of a root system> $e_i-e_j$ and $e_i+e_j$ for $i<j$. Its <Weyl vector> is $\rho=(n-1,n-2,\ldots,0)$. Dominant coordinates satisfy $\lambda_1\geq\cdots\geq\lambda_{n-1}\geq|\lambda_n|$. The <Weyl dimension formula> becomes $\prod_{i<j}((\lambda_i+\rho_i)^2-(\lambda_j+\rho_j)^2)/(\rho_i^2-\rho_j^2)$. The case $D_2$ is two independent <A1 root systems>, while $D_3$ is isomorphic to the <A3 root system>.

= Real four-dimensional rotation algebra splitting
{parent=Special orthogonal Lie algebra}
{title2=$\mathfrak{so}(4)\cong\mathfrak{so}(3)\oplus\mathfrak{so}(3)$}

For antisymmetric Maurer–Cartan forms with $d\lambda_{ij}=\lambda_{ik}\wedge\lambda_{kj}$, let $\alpha^\pm=(\lambda_{12}\pm\lambda_{43},\lambda_{23}\pm\lambda_{41},\lambda_{31}\pm\lambda_{42})$. Direct expansion gives $d\alpha_1^\pm=-\alpha_2^\pm\wedge\alpha_3^\pm$ and cyclic analogues, with no mixed terms. The dual triples form two commuting ideals, each with the <SO(3) Lie algebra> brackets. This is a real compact splitting, distinct from the splitting of the complexified algebra into two copies of $\mathfrak{sl}_2(\mathbb C)$.

= SO(3) Lie algebra
{parent=Special orthogonal Lie algebra}
{c}
{title2=$\mathfrak{so}(3)$}

Differentiating $R^TR=I$ gives $\mathfrak{so}(3)=\{A\in M_3(\mathbb R):A^T=-A\}$. The generators $L_a v=e_a\times v$ satisfy $[L_a,L_b]=\epsilon_{abc}L_c$ by the <cross product> identity. This identifies it with the <SU(2) Lie algebra> by matching a skew-Hermitian Pauli basis.

= Bn root system
{title2=$B_n$}
{c}
{parent=Special orthogonal Lie algebra}

In an orthonormal basis $(\varepsilon_i)$, the $B_n$ roots are $\pm\varepsilon_i\pm\varepsilon_j$ for $i<j$ and $\pm\varepsilon_i$.

= Bn Dynkin diagram and affine extension
{c}
{parent=Bn root system}

For $n\ge2$, the <Bn root system> has <simple roots> $\alpha_i=\varepsilon_i-\varepsilon_{i+1}$ for $i<n$ and $\alpha_n=\varepsilon_n$. Its <Dynkin diagram> is a chain with a double terminal bond pointing toward short $\alpha_n$. The <highest root> is $\theta=\varepsilon_1+\varepsilon_2$. In the <extended Dynkin diagram>, $\alpha_0=-\theta$ attaches by a single bond to $\alpha_2$ for $n\ge3$. For $n=2$, both long nodes $0,1$ attach by double bonds to short node $2$. Rank one is the <rank-one root system>; its affine diagram has two equal-length nodes joined by a double bond without a short-root arrow.

= B3 root system
{title2=$B_3$}
{c}
{parent=Bn root system}

The $B_3$ roots are $\pm\varepsilon_i\pm\varepsilon_j$ for $1\leq i<j\leq3$ and $\pm\varepsilon_i$ for $1\leq i\leq3$. Its root basis $\alpha_1=\varepsilon_1-\varepsilon_2$, $\alpha_2=\varepsilon_2-\varepsilon_3$, $\alpha_3=\varepsilon_3$ exhibits it as the <dual root system> of the <C3 root system>. With $A_{ij}=\langle\alpha_i,\alpha_j^\vee\rangle$, its <Cartan matrix> is
$$
\begin{pmatrix}2&-1&0\\-1&2&-2\\0&-1&2\end{pmatrix}.
$$
The fundamental representation of highest weight $[1,0,0]$ is the seven-dimensional vector representation, with weights $\pm\varepsilon_1,\pm\varepsilon_2,\pm\varepsilon_3,0$.

= so4 Lie algebra
{title2=$\mathfrak{so}_4$}
{c}
{parent=Special orthogonal Lie algebra}

The complexified so4 Lie algebra consists of four-by-four complex skew-symmetric matrices and has dimension six. Its root system is $D_2=A_1\times A_1$, reflecting its decomposition into two simple rank-one ideals.

= Chiral decomposition of the complexified so4 Lie algebra
{title2=$\mathfrak{so}_4(\mathbb C)\cong\mathfrak{sl}_2(\mathbb C)\oplus\mathfrak{sl}_2(\mathbb C)$}
{c}
{parent=so4 Lie algebra}

The self-dual and anti-self-dual combinations of the skew generators form two commuting copies of $\mathfrak{sl}_2(\mathbb C)$. Consequently
$$
\mathfrak{so}_4(\mathbb C)\cong\mathfrak{sl}_2(\mathbb C)\oplus\mathfrak{sl}_2(\mathbb C),
$$
and its <adjoint representation> decomposes as $(\mathbf3,\mathbf1)\oplus(\mathbf1,\mathbf3)$.

= Spin group
{c}
{parent=Special orthogonal Lie algebra}
{wiki}

The spin group $\operatorname{Spin}(n)$ is the connected double cover of $SO(n)$ constructed inside the units of a <Clifford algebra>.

= Exterior-square double cover of SO(3,3)
{parent=Spin group}
{title2=$SL(4,\mathbb R)/\{\pm I\}\cong SO_0(3,3)$}

A fixed volume form makes wedge pairing on $\Lambda^2\mathbb R^4$ a symmetric form of signature $(3,3)$. The exterior-square action of <special linear group> <matrices> preserves it. Its kernel is $\{\pm I\}$: a transformation acting trivially on every bivector preserves every two-plane and hence every line, so is scalar, with scalar square one. The derivative is injective and both groups have dimension fifteen, so the connected image is the full <identity component>. This is a concrete real spin-group cover.

= Spin(4) double cover
{c}
{parent=Spin group}
{title2=$\operatorname{Spin}(4)\cong SU(2)\times SU(2)$}

Two <unit quaternions> act on Euclidean four-space by $q\mapsto aqb^{-1}$. This norm-preserving action maps onto the <SO(4) group>. Its kernel is precisely $(1,1)$ and $(-1,-1)$: a kernel pair must have equal entries commuting with every <quaternion>. The two simply connected <SU(2)> factors therefore form the universal spin cover.

= Spinor representation
{parent=Spin group}
{wiki=Spin_representation}

A spinor representation is a representation of a <spin group>, or infinitesimally of a <special orthogonal Lie algebra>, constructed from a <Clifford algebra>. In even dimensions it can split into two chiral spinor representations.

= Spinor dimension in arbitrary spacetime dimension
{parent=Spinor representation}
{title2=$\dim_{\mathbb C}S_D=2^{\lfloor D/2\rfloor}$}

An irreducible complex <Clifford algebra> module in even spacetime dimension $2k$ has dimension $2^k$ and splits under the connected <Spin group> into two <Weyl spinor> modules of dimension $2^{k-1}$. In odd dimension $2k+1$, an irreducible spinor still has dimension $2^k$ but has no Weyl splitting. Reality conditions depend on signature and are additional to these complex counts.

= Lorentz algebra
{title2=$\mathfrak{so}(1,3)$}
{c}
{parent=Special orthogonal Lie algebra}

The Lorentz algebra has rotation generators $J_i$ and boost generators $K_i$ satisfying
$$
[J_i,J_j]=\epsilon_{ijk}J_k,\qquad
[J_i,K_j]=\epsilon_{ijk}K_k,\qquad
[K_i,K_j]=-\epsilon_{ijk}J_k.
$$

= Lorentz generator
{c}
{parent=Lorentz algebra}
{title2=$M_{\mu\nu}=-M_{\nu\mu}$}

Lorentz generators produce infinitesimal <Lorentz transformations>. In four dimensions they split into three rotation and three boost generators. Their action on <supercharges> uses the appropriate spinor representation, while their action on translations is the mixed <Poincare algebra> commutator. Signs in component formulas must agree with whether the generators act on states, operator columns or dual rows.

= Rotation and boost commutators with a fixed metric signature
{parent=Lorentz algebra}
{title2=$[K_i,K_j]=-si\epsilon_{ijk}J_k,\quad[J_i,K_j]=si\epsilon_{ijk}K_k$}

For the <Lorentz algebra> convention $[M_{\mu\nu},M_{\rho\sigma}]=i(M_{\mu\sigma}\eta_{\nu\rho}+M_{\nu\rho}\eta_{\mu\sigma}-M_{\mu\rho}\eta_{\nu\sigma}-M_{\nu\sigma}\eta_{\mu\rho})$, choose $\eta=\operatorname{diag}(s,-s,-s,-s)$, $s=\pm1$, $\epsilon_{123}=1$, $J_i=\epsilon_{ijk}M_{jk}/2$, and $K_i=M_{0i}$. The resulting rotation bracket is $[J_i,J_j]=si\epsilon_{ijk}J_k$ as well as the two displayed brackets. Changing the <Minkowski metric> signature while holding this generator convention fixed reverses all coefficients. A simultaneous redefinition of the generators can instead preserve the familiar form. Conventions must be specified before comparing algebra signs.

= Chiral decomposition of the complex Lorentz algebra
{parent=Lorentz algebra}

Over the complex numbers, $A_i=(J_i+iK_i)/2$ and $B_i=(J_i-iK_i)/2$ generate commuting copies of $\mathfrak{sl}_2(\mathbb C)$, giving
$$
\mathfrak{so}(1,3)_\mathbb C\cong
\mathfrak{sl}_2(\mathbb C)\oplus\mathfrak{sl}_2(\mathbb C).
$$
Finite-dimensional irreducible representations are labelled $(j_L,j_R)$.

= Lorentz reality condition on chiral generators
{c}
{parent=Chiral decomposition of the complex Lorentz algebra}
{title2=$A_i^\dagger=B_i$}

For Hermitian rotations $J_i$ and boosts $K_i$, the complex combinations $A_i=(J_i+iK_i)/2$ and $B_i=(J_i-iK_i)/2$ are adjoints of one another. Although their complex brackets split into two commuting $\mathfrak{sl}_2$ factors, they are not two independent Hermitian compact rotation algebras. The real Lorentz <spin> group is $SL(2,\mathbb C)$; $SU(2)\times SU(2)$ is a different compact real form.

= Finite-dimensional complex Lorentz representations
{parent=Chiral decomposition of the complex Lorentz algebra}
{title2=$D^{(j_L,j_R)}(S)=\operatorname{Sym}^{2j_L}S\otimes\operatorname{Sym}^{2j_R}\overline S$}

The complexified <Lorentz algebra> splits into two commuting $\mathfrak{sl}_2$ factors. Extending two <homogeneous polynomial representations of SU2> therefore gives every finite-dimensional irreducible complex <group representation> of the <Lorentz spinor double cover>. The kernel sign permits descent precisely when $j_L+j_R$ is an integer. These field <group representations> are generally nonunitary for a positive-definite inner product; infinite-dimensional unitary Lorentz <group representations> belong to a different construction.

= Parity action on a Lorentz representation
{parent=Chiral decomposition of the complex Lorentz algebra}

Parity fixes $J_i$, negates $K_i$, and therefore exchanges the two chiral factors. It sends $(j_L,j_R)$ to $(j_R,j_L)$.

= Lorentz Casimir invariants
{parent=Chiral decomposition of the complex Lorentz algebra}

The quadratic contraction $M_{\mu\nu}M^{\mu\nu}$ is parity even, while $M_{\mu\nu}\widetilde M^{\mu\nu}$ is parity odd. Their linear combinations give the quadratic Casimirs of the two chiral $\mathfrak{sl}_2$ factors.

= so5 Lie algebra
{title2=$\mathfrak{so}_5$}
{c}
{parent=Special orthogonal Lie algebra}

The Lie algebra $\mathfrak{so}_5$ has dimension ten and root system $B_2$.

= SO5 to SO4 branching
{title2=$SO(5)\downarrow SO(4)$}
{c}
{parent=so5 Lie algebra}

Under the standard block-diagonal subgroup, the vector and adjoint representations branch as
$$
\mathbf5\to\mathbf4\oplus\mathbf1,
\qquad
\mathbf{10}\to\mathbf6\oplus\mathbf4.
$$
Using $\mathfrak{so}_4\cong\mathfrak{su}_2\oplus\mathfrak{su}_2$, these are $(\tfrac12,\tfrac12)\oplus(0,0)$ and $(1,0)\oplus(0,1)\oplus(\tfrac12,\tfrac12)$.

= Left SU(2) subgroup of SO(4)
{parent=SO5 to SO4 branching}
{title2=$SU(2)_L\subset SO(4)$}

Identify $\mathbb R^4$ with the <quaternions>. Unit <quaternions> act by left multiplication, embedding the <SU(2) group> in $SO(4)$. Under this subgroup, the complexified vector representation branches as $\mathbf4\to2\mathbf2$, and the <adjoint representation> as $\mathbf6\to\mathbf3\oplus3\mathbf1$. Right multiplication supplies the commuting second factor.

= Diagonal-root SU(2) embedding in SO(5)
{parent=Left SU(2) subgroup of SO(4)}
{title2=$\langle(x,y),(1,1)^\vee\rangle=x+y$}

The long <roots of a root system> $\pm(1,1)$ and $\pm(1,-1)$ give the commuting $SU(2)$ factors of the standard $SO(4)$ subgroup. Choosing $(1,1)$ for the left factor pairs a <weight> $(x,y)$ with its <coroot> as $x+y$. The five-dimensional vector then gives two doublets and a singlet, whereas a short-axis root would give a triplet and two singlets.

= B2 root system
{title2=$B_2$}
{c}
{parent=Special orthogonal Lie algebra}

The $B_2$ root system consists of $\pm\varepsilon_1$, $\pm\varepsilon_2$, and $\pm\varepsilon_1\pm\varepsilon_2$. Its vector representation has weights $\varepsilon_1,\varepsilon_2,0,-\varepsilon_2,-\varepsilon_1$.

= B2 weight lattice
{parent=B2 root system}
{c}
{title2=$P(B_2)=\mathbb Z^2\cup(\mathbb Z+\frac12)^2$}

For simple roots $(1,0)$ and $(-1,1)$, <coroot> integrality gives the <weight lattice> $P=\mathbb Z^2\cup(\mathbb Z+1/2)^2$. This is the lattice for the <Spin group> $\operatorname{Spin}(5)$ and its <Lie algebra>. Representations descending to $SO(5)$ use the integer coset, because a $2\pi$ rotation in either coordinate plane must act trivially.

= Isomorphism between so5 and sp4
{title2=$\mathfrak{so}_5(\mathbb C)\cong\mathfrak{sp}_4(\mathbb C)$}
{c}
{parent=B2 root system}

The root systems $B_2$ and $C_2$ are isomorphic after interchanging long and short simple-root labels. The classification of complex simple Lie algebras therefore gives $\mathfrak{so}_5(\mathbb C)\cong\mathfrak{sp}_4(\mathbb C)$.

= Fundamental representations of B2
{title2=$[1,0],[0,1]$}
{c}
{parent=B2 root system}

For simple roots $(1,0)$ and $(-1,1)$, the fundamental weights are $\omega_1=(1/2,1/2)$ and $\omega_2=(0,1)$. The representation $[1,0]$ is the four-dimensional spin representation with weights $(\pm1/2,\pm1/2)$; $[0,1]$ is the five-dimensional vector representation with weights $(\pm1,0),(0,\pm1),(0,0)$.

= Weyl dimension formula for B2
{title2=$\dim V(a\omega_1+b\omega_2)$}
{c}
{parent=Fundamental representations of B2}

When $\alpha_1$ is the short simple root, $\omega_1$ is the spin fundamental weight and $\omega_2$ is the vector fundamental weight. The <Weyl dimension formula> gives
$$
\dim V(a\omega_1+b\omega_2)
=\frac{(a+1)(b+1)(a+b+2)(a+2b+3)}6.
$$

= Traceless symmetric square of the defining so5 representation
{title2=$V(2\omega_2)$}
{c}
{parent=Weyl dimension formula for B2}

For the five-dimensional defining representation $V=V(\omega_2)$ of $\mathfrak{so}_5$, the invariant quadratic form splits
$$
S^2V\cong\mathbb C\oplus V(2\omega_2).
$$
Thus the traceless symmetric square is irreducible of dimension $15-1=14$.

= Tensor-square decomposition of the defining so5 representation
{title2=$V(\omega_2)^{\otimes2}=V(0)\oplus V(2\omega_1)\oplus V(2\omega_2)$}
{c}
{parent=Traceless symmetric square of the defining so5 representation}

For the five-dimensional defining representation of $\mathfrak{so}_5$, the <symmetric square> is the sum of its invariant trace and the traceless representation $V(2\omega_2)$, while the <exterior square> is the <adjoint representation> $V(2\omega_1)$. Hence
$$
V(\omega_2)\otimes V(\omega_2)
\cong V(0)\oplus V(2\omega_1)\oplus V(2\omega_2).
$$

= Cartan subalgebra
{c}
{parent=Semisimple Lie algebra}
{wiki}

= Maximal torus of a semisimple Lie algebra
{c}
{synonym}

For a complex semisimple <Lie algebra>, a Cartan subalgebra is a maximal abelian subalgebra consisting of semisimple elements. It is also called a maximal torus in this setting.

= Self-centralizing property of a Cartan subalgebra
{parent=Cartan subalgebra}
{title2=$C_L(H)=H$}

For a <Cartan subalgebra> $H$ of a finite-dimensional complex <semisimple Lie algebra> $L$, $H$ is abelian and $C_L(H)=H$, so it is maximal among abelian subalgebras. Starting from the nilpotent self-normalizing definition, the <generalized-weight decomposition for a nilpotent Lie algebra> and the <Engel theorem> show that the zero generalized <weight space> of its adjoint action is exactly $H$. Invariance of the <Killing form> makes $H$ orthogonal to every nonzero generalized <weight space>. The <Lie theorem> gives $B([H,H],H)=0$, and nondegeneracy then forces $[H,H]=0$. An element commuting with $H$ normalizes it, hence belongs to $H$.

= Nondegeneracy of the Killing form on a Cartan subalgebra
{parent=Cartan subalgebra}

For a complex <semisimple Lie algebra>, the <Cartan subalgebra> is orthogonal under the <Killing form> to every nonzero <root space>. A vector in the Cartan subalgebra orthogonal to that whole subalgebra is therefore orthogonal to the entire Lie algebra and must vanish. On the Cartan subalgebra the form is $\kappa(h,h')=\sum_{\alpha\in\Phi}\alpha(h)\alpha(h')$.

= Euclidean subspace of a Cartan subalgebra
{c}
{title2=$\mathfrak h_{\mathbb R}=\operatorname{span}_{\mathbb R}\{h_{\alpha_i}\}$}
{parent=Nondegeneracy of the Killing form on a Cartan subalgebra}

The real span of the simple <coroots> inside a complex <Cartan subalgebra> carries a positive-definite restriction of the <Killing form>. Equivalently it consists of the $h$ for which all roots have real values. The formula $\kappa(h,h)=\sum_{\alpha\in\Phi}\alpha(h)^2$ is positive for $h\ne0$, since the roots span the dual. The induced dual <inner product> makes the <root system> Euclidean and makes <root reflections> orthogonal.

= Rank of a semisimple Lie algebra
{parent=Cartan subalgebra}
{title2=$\ell$}

The rank is the dimension of a <Cartan subalgebra>, equivalently the dimension of the real span of its <root system>.

= Root-space decomposition
{parent=Cartan subalgebra}

For a Cartan subalgebra $\mathfrak t$, a semisimple <Lie algebra> decomposes as
$$
\mathfrak g=\mathfrak t\oplus\bigoplus_{\alpha\in R}\mathfrak g_\alpha,
\qquad
\mathfrak g_\alpha=\{x:[h,x]=\alpha(h)x\text{ for every }h\in\mathfrak t\}.
$$
The nonzero functionals $\alpha$ are the roots.

= Triangular decomposition of a Lie algebra
{parent=Root-space decomposition}
{title2=$\mathfrak g=\mathfrak n^-\oplus\mathfrak h\oplus\mathfrak n^+$}

Choosing a <positive system of a root system> for a complex <semisimple Lie algebra> divides its <root spaces> into negative and positive ones. Their respective sums $\mathfrak n^-$ and $\mathfrak n^+$, together with the <Cartan subalgebra> $\mathfrak h$, give this <direct sum> of <vector spaces>. Both $\mathfrak n^\pm$ are <nilpotent Lie algebras>, and $\mathfrak h\oplus\mathfrak n^+$ is a <Borel subalgebra>. The <Poincare-Birkhoff-Witt theorem> turns this into an ordered vector-space decomposition of the <universal enveloping algebra>, useful for building <Verma modules>.

= Root-space reducedness lemma
{parent=Root-space decomposition}
{title2=$\dim\mathfrak g_\alpha=1,\quad R\cap\mathbb R\alpha=\{\alpha,-\alpha\}$}

For a complex <semisimple Lie algebra>, restrict the module $\mathfrak h\oplus\bigoplus_{j\ne0}\mathfrak g_{j\alpha}$ to the <sl2 subalgebra associated with a root>. All its weights are even. Its raising operator maps the weight-zero space $\mathfrak h$ onto the one-dimensional line of the chosen <root vector>, so there is exactly one nontrivial irreducible summand: the adjoint module of highest weight $2$. Thus the root spaces at $\pm\alpha$ are one-dimensional and $2\alpha$ is not a root. For any parallel root $c\alpha$, the integers $2c$ and $2/c$ have product $4$; excluding double and half roots leaves only $c=\pm1$.

= Root space
{title2=$\mathfrak g_\alpha$}
{parent=Root-space decomposition}

For a root $\alpha$, its root space is the simultaneous eigenspace
$$
\mathfrak g_\alpha=\{x:[h,x]=\alpha(h)x\text{ for every }h\in\mathfrak t\}
$$
of the <adjoint representation> of the <Cartan subalgebra>.

= Root vector
{parent=Root space}
{title2=$e_\alpha$}

A root vector is a nonzero element of a <root space> $\mathfrak g_\alpha$ in the <root-space decomposition> of a <semisimple Lie algebra>.

= Regular element of a semisimple Lie algebra
{parent=Root-space decomposition}
{c}

An element $x$ of a semisimple <Lie algebra> of rank $\ell$ is regular when its <centralizer> has the minimum possible dimension $\ell$.

= Regular element criterion in a Cartan subalgebra
{parent=Regular element of a semisimple Lie algebra}
{c}

For $t$ in a <Cartan subalgebra> $\mathfrak t$, the <root-space decomposition> gives
$$
\mathfrak z_{\mathfrak g}(t)=\mathfrak t\oplus\bigoplus_{\alpha(t)=0}\mathfrak g_\alpha.
$$
Thus $t$ is regular exactly when $\alpha(t)\ne0$ for every root $\alpha$.

= Two-root decomposition with a nondegenerate trace form
{parent=Root-space decomposition}

Let a maximal torus $\mathfrak t$ give a decomposition $\mathfrak g=\mathfrak g_\alpha\oplus\mathfrak t\oplus\mathfrak g_\beta$. If $\mathfrak g$ has a nondegenerate trace form, then $\beta=-\alpha$, the opposite root spaces generate $\mathfrak{sl}_2$, and
$$
\mathfrak g\cong\mathfrak{sl}_2\oplus\ker\alpha,
$$
where $\ker\alpha\subset\mathfrak t$ is central and abelian.

= sl2 subalgebra associated with a root
{title2=$\mathfrak m_\alpha\cong\mathfrak{sl}_2$}
{c}
{parent=Root-space decomposition}

For every root $\alpha$ of a complex semisimple Lie algebra, there are root vectors $e_\alpha\in\mathfrak g_\alpha$ and $f_\alpha\in\mathfrak g_{-\alpha}$ and an element $h_\alpha\in\mathfrak t$ satisfying
$$
[h_\alpha,e_\alpha]=2e_\alpha,\qquad
[h_\alpha,f_\alpha]=-2f_\alpha,\qquad
[e_\alpha,f_\alpha]=h_\alpha.
$$
Their span is an $\mathfrak{sl}_2$ subalgebra.

= Nonisotropic root lemma
{parent=sl2 subalgebra associated with a root}
{title2=$\alpha(t_\alpha)\ne0$}

For a <root of a root system> of a complex <semisimple Lie algebra>, define $t_\alpha$ by $B(t_\alpha,h)=\alpha(h)$ using the <Killing form> on its <Cartan subalgebra>. Then $\alpha(t_\alpha)\ne0$. Otherwise vectors $x\in\mathfrak g_\alpha$, $y\in\mathfrak g_{-\alpha}$ with $B(x,y)=1$ would generate a <solvable Lie algebra> with $[x,y]=t_\alpha$ central. The <Lie theorem> would make $\operatorname{ad}t_\alpha$ nilpotent, while the <root-space decomposition> makes it diagonalizable, forcing $t_\alpha$ into the zero <center of a Lie algebra>.

= Root system
{parent=Root-space decomposition}
{wiki}

A root system is a finite set of nonzero vectors closed under the reflections they define and satisfying the crystallographic integrality condition when it arises from a semisimple <Lie algebra>.

= Obtuse nonopposite roots have a root sum
{parent=Root system}
{title2=$(\alpha,\beta)<0,\ \beta\ne-\alpha\Rightarrow\alpha+\beta\in R$}

For nonproportional roots in a <reduced root system> which is a <crystallographic root system>, the negative <Cartan integers> $m=2(\alpha,\beta)/(\alpha,\alpha)$ and $n=2(\alpha,\beta)/(\beta,\beta)$ satisfy $mn=4\cos^2\theta\in\{1,2,3\}$. At least one of $m,n$ equals $-1$, so reflecting one root in the other produces their sum. The exclusion of opposite roots is essential: their inner product is negative but their sum is zero, which is not a <root>.

= Irreducible root system
{parent=Root system}

A <root system> spanning a real <inner product space> is irreducible when it cannot be partitioned into two nonempty mutually <orthogonal> <root systems>. Its <Dynkin diagram> is connected. This condition concerns the roots themselves, rather than an arbitrary ambient space larger than their span.

= Crystallographic root system
{parent=Root system}
{title2=$2(\beta,\alpha)/(\alpha,\alpha)\in\mathbb Z$}

A finite <root system> in a real <inner product space> is crystallographic when every <Cartan integer> $2(\beta,\alpha)/(\alpha,\alpha)$ is an integer. The roots of a complex <semisimple Lie algebra> satisfy this because each number is a weight of its <sl2 subalgebra associated with a root>. Reducedness is a separate axiom; the <root-space reducedness lemma> establishes it for these Lie-algebra roots.

= Root of a root system
{parent=Root system}

= Root
{synonym}

= Roots of a root system
{synonym}

A root is an individual nonzero vector in a <root system>. For a complex <semisimple Lie algebra>, it is a nonzero <weight> of the <adjoint representation> restricted to a <Cartan subalgebra>; its <root space> consists of vectors $x$ satisfying $[h,x]=\alpha(h)x$ for all $h$ in that subalgebra.

= Rank-one root system
{parent=Root system}
{title2=$A_1$}

= A1 root system
{c}
{synonym}

A reduced <root system> of rank one is $Φ=\{\alpha,-\alpha\}$. Its <fundamental weight> is $\alpha/2$, and its <Weyl group> has two elements. Its ordinary <Dynkin diagram> has one node; adjoining $\alpha_0=-\alpha$ gives affine <Cartan matrix> $\begin{pmatrix}2&-2\\-2&2\end{pmatrix}$, drawn as a double bond between equal-length roots.

= A2 root system
{c}
{title2=$A_2$}
{parent=Root system}

The $A_2$ <root system> has equal-length <simple roots> $\alpha_1,\alpha_2$ with angle $2\pi/3$, and roots $\pm\alpha_1$, $\pm\alpha_2$, $\pm(\alpha_1+\alpha_2)$. Its <Cartan matrix> is $\begin{pmatrix}2&-1\\-1&2\end{pmatrix}$. The corresponding complex <simple Lie algebra> is $\mathfrak{sl}_3(\mathbb C)$, of dimension eight.

= A2 fundamental weights and weight lattice
{parent=A2 root system}
{c}
{title2=$\omega_1=(2\alpha_1+\alpha_2)/3,\quad\omega_2=(\alpha_1+2\alpha_2)/3$}

For the <A2 root system>, $\omega_1=(2\alpha_1+\alpha_2)/3$ and $\omega_2=(\alpha_1+2\alpha_2)/3$. The <weight lattice> $P=\mathbb Z\omega_1+\mathbb Z\omega_2$ contains the <root lattice> $Q$ with index three. In fundamental coordinates $(a,b)$, root-lattice membership is $a-b\equiv0\pmod3$.

= Automorphism of a root system
{parent=Root system}

An automorphism of a <root system> $\Phi\subset E$ is an <orthogonal transformation> of $E$ mapping $\Phi$ onto itself. The <Weyl group> is a subgroup of this automorphism group; additional automorphisms can act nontrivially on a <Dynkin diagram> while preserving a <root basis>.

= Long root
{parent=Root system}

In an irreducible <root system> with two root lengths, a long root has the larger length with respect to a <Weyl group> invariant <inner product>.

= Short root
{parent=Root system}

In an irreducible <root system> with two root lengths, a short root has the smaller length with respect to a <Weyl group> invariant <inner product>.

= Root reflection
{title2=$s_\alpha$}
{parent=Root system}

For a nonzero vector $\alpha$ in a <Euclidean normed vector space>, the root reflection is
$$
s_\alpha(v)=v-\frac{2(v,\alpha)}{(\alpha,\alpha)}\alpha.
$$
It fixes the hyperplane perpendicular to $\alpha$ and sends $\alpha$ to $-\alpha$.

= Cartan integer
{title2=$\langle\beta,\alpha^\vee\rangle$}
{c}
{parent=Root system}

The Cartan integer associated with two roots is
$$
\langle\beta,\alpha^\vee\rangle
=\frac{2(\beta,\alpha)}{(\alpha,\alpha)}.
$$
A crystallographic root system requires every Cartan integer to be an <integer>.

= Reduced root system
{parent=Root system}

A root system is reduced when the only scalar multiples of any root $\alpha$ that are also roots are $\alpha$ and $-\alpha$.

= A3 root system
{title2=$A_3$}
{c}
{parent=Root system}

The $A_3$ root system is $\{e_i-e_j:1\leq i\ne j\leq4\}$ in the hyperplane of $\mathbb R^4$ whose coordinates sum to zero. It has $12$ roots and simple roots $e_1-e_2,e_2-e_3,e_3-e_4$.

= Cartan matrix of type A3
{title2=$A_3$ Cartan matrix}
{c}
{parent=A3 root system}

The Cartan matrix of type A3 is
$$
\begin{pmatrix}2&-1&0\\-1&2&-1\\0&-1&2\end{pmatrix}.
$$

= Root string
{parent=Root system}
{title2=$S_{\alpha,\beta}=\Phi\cap(\beta+\mathbb Z\alpha)$}

A root string is the set $S_{\alpha,\beta}=\Phi\cap(\beta+\mathbb Z\alpha)$ for roots $\alpha,\beta$ of a <root system> $\Phi$. For nonproportional roots $\alpha$ and $\beta$ of a reduced crystallographic <root system>, the roots of the form $\beta+n\alpha$ form an unbroken <root string> $\beta-p\alpha,\ldots,\beta+q\alpha$, and $p-q=\langle\beta,\alpha^\vee\rangle$. The <root-string theorem> explains the endpoint relation. If $\beta=\pm\alpha$, zero is missing and this consecutive-string statement does not apply.

= Root-string theorem
{parent=Root string}

For nonproportional roots $\gamma,\delta$ of a reduced crystallographic <root system>, $S_{\gamma,\delta}=\{\delta-p\gamma,\ldots,\delta+q\gamma\}$ is consecutive, and $p-q=\langle\delta,\gamma^\vee\rangle$. Realize the crystallographic <root system> as the roots of a complex <semisimple Lie algebra>. The sum of the one-dimensional <root spaces> in this string is a module for the <sl2 subalgebra associated with a root>. Its weights are $\langle\delta,\gamma^\vee\rangle+2n$; they have one fixed parity. Complete reducibility gives a sum of <irreducible representations> of the <sl2 Lie algebra>. Two summands of this parity would overlap at weight zero when the parity is even, or at weights $\pm1$ when it is odd, contradicting the one-dimensional <root spaces>. Thus there is one irreducible string, whose opposite endpoint weights sum to zero. This proves the relation. For distinct <simple roots>, $p=0$ and the cardinality is $1-\langle\delta,\gamma^\vee\rangle$. The nonproportional hypothesis excludes $\delta=\pm\gamma$, where zero is not a root and the consecutive-string statement fails.

= Cartan matrix
{title2=$A_{ij}$}
{c}
{parent=Root system}
{wiki}

For ordered simple roots, the Cartan matrix has entries $A_{ij}=\langle\alpha_i,\alpha_j^\vee\rangle$. It determines their angles and relative lengths and therefore determines the corresponding crystallographic root system.

= Positive root
{parent=Root system}

A choice of positive roots selects exactly one of $\alpha$ and $-\alpha$ and is closed under addition whenever the sum is a root.

= Simple root
{title2=$\alpha_i$}
{parent=Positive root}
{wiki}

The simple roots are the positive roots that cannot be expressed as sums of two positive roots. Every root is an integer combination of simple roots with coefficients of one sign.

= Simple-root subtraction lemma
{parent=Simple root}

If a positive root $\alpha$ is not simple, then $\alpha-\beta$ is a positive root for some simple root $\beta$. Iteration writes every positive root as a sum of simple roots whose successive partial sums are roots.

= Action of a simple reflection on positive roots
{parent=Simple root}

For a simple root $\alpha$, the reflection $s_\alpha$ sends $\alpha$ to $-\alpha$ and permutes all other positive roots.

= Highest root
{title2=$\theta$}
{parent=Positive root}

The highest root is the maximal positive root in the root order determined by the <simple root>[simple roots].

= Half-sum of positive roots
{title2=$\rho$}
{parent=Positive root}

= Weyl vector
{c}
{synonym}

The Weyl vector is $\rho=\frac12\sum_{\alpha\in R^+}\alpha$. It satisfies $\langle\rho,\alpha_i^\vee\rangle=1$ for every simple root.

= Coroot
{title2=$\alpha^\vee$}
{parent=Root system}
{wiki}

The coroot associated with a nonzero root is $\alpha^\vee=2\alpha/(\alpha,\alpha)$.

= Simple coroot
{parent=Coroot}
{title2=$\alpha_i^\vee$}

For chosen <simple roots>, the corresponding <coroots> are the simple coroots. They form the simple system of the <dual root system>. The <fundamental weights> are defined by $\langle\omega_j,\alpha_i^\vee\rangle=\delta_{ij}$, so pairings with simple coroots record coordinates in the fundamental-weight <basis>.

= Dual root system
{title2=$\Phi^\vee$}
{parent=Coroot}
{wiki}

The dual root system of $\Phi$ is $\Phi^\vee=\{\alpha^\vee:\alpha\in\Phi\}$. It has the transposed <Cartan matrix>, so its <Dynkin diagram> reverses every multiple-edge arrow; in particular, $B_n$ and $C_n$ are dual.

= Root lattice
{title2=$Q$}
{parent=Root system}
{wiki}

The root lattice is the integer span of the roots, equivalently of the <simple root>[simple roots].

= Kostant partition function
{title2=$P(\nu)$}
{c}
{parent=Root lattice}
{wiki}

The Kostant partition function $P(\nu)$ counts the ways to express a weight $\nu$ as a nonnegative integer combination of positive roots. By the <Poincare-Birkhoff-Witt theorem>, $P(\lambda-\mu)$ is the multiplicity of the weight $\mu$ in the <Verma module> $M(\lambda)$.

= Generating series of the Kostant partition function
{parent=Kostant partition function}
{title2=$\sum_\nu p(\nu)e^{-\nu}=\prod_{\alpha>0}(1-e^{-\alpha})^{-1}$}

The <Kostant partition function> counts <nonnegative integer> combinations of the positive <roots of a root system>, without ordering the summands. Multiply the formal <geometric series> $\sum_{k\geq0}e^{-k\alpha}$ for every <positive root>. The coefficient of $e^{-\nu}$ counts precisely these combinations. Every coefficient is finite because a <linear functional> strictly positive on the <positive roots> bounds every participating integer coefficient. The sign of each exponent in the cancelling product must agree with that of this series.

= Dominance order
{title2=$\mu\preceq\lambda$}
{parent=Root lattice}
{wiki}

For weights $\mu,\lambda$, one writes $\mu\preceq\lambda$ when $\lambda-\mu$ is a nonnegative integer combination of the simple roots.

= Weight lattice
{title2=$P$}
{parent=Root system}
{wiki}

The weight lattice is the set of vectors $\lambda$ for which $\langle\lambda,\alpha^\vee\rangle$ is an integer for every root $\alpha$.

= Dynkin label
{title2=$[\lambda^1,\ldots,\lambda^r]$}
{c}
{parent=Weight lattice}

The Dynkin labels of a weight $\lambda$ are its simple-coroot coordinates $\lambda^i=\langle\lambda,\alpha_i^\vee\rangle$. Thus $\lambda=\sum_i\lambda^i\omega_i$ in the basis of <fundamental weight>[fundamental weights].

= Dominant weight
{parent=Weight lattice}
{wiki=Weight_(representation_theory)#Dominant_integral_weights}

A weight is dominant when all its <Dynkin label>[Dynkin labels] are nonnegative.

= Dominant integral weight
{parent=Weight lattice}
{wiki=Weight_(representation_theory)#Dominant_integral_weights}

An integral weight $\lambda$ is dominant when $\langle\lambda,\alpha_i^\vee\rangle\geq0$ for every simple coroot. Equivalently, it is a nonnegative integer combination of the fundamental weights.

= Fundamental weight
{title2=$\omega_i$}
{parent=Weight lattice}
{wiki}

The fundamental weights are dual to the simple coroots: $\langle\omega_i,\alpha_j^\vee\rangle=\delta_{ij}$.

= Fundamental representation
{parent=Fundamental weight}
{wiki}

The fundamental representation associated with a fundamental weight $\omega_i$ is the irreducible highest-weight representation $V(\omega_i)$.

= Dynkin index
{c}
{title2=$I(R)$}
{parent=Fundamental representation}
{wiki}

The Dynkin index of a representation $R$ fixes the trace normalization $\operatorname{tr}_R(T^aT^b)=I(R)\delta^{ab}$ up to the convention for the generators. It controls matter contributions to gauge beta functions and mixed anomalies.

= Cubic anomaly coefficient
{title2=$A(R)$}
{parent=Dynkin index}

The cubic anomaly coefficient is defined by the symmetrized trace of three representation matrices. A conjugate representation has the opposite coefficient, while a real or pseudoreal representation has coefficient zero.

= Two-index symmetric representation
{title2=$\operatorname{Sym}^2V$}
{parent=Fundamental representation}

The two-index symmetric representation of $SU(N)$ is the symmetric square of its fundamental representation. It has dimension $N(N+1)/2$, Dynkin index $N+2$, and cubic anomaly coefficient $N+4$ when the fundamental index and anomaly are normalized to one.

= Two-index antisymmetric representation
{title2=$\Lambda^2V$}
{parent=Fundamental representation}

The two-index antisymmetric representation of $SU(N)$ is the exterior square of its fundamental representation. It has dimension $N(N-1)/2$, Dynkin index $N-2$, and cubic anomaly coefficient $N-4$ in the same normalization.

= Real structure of the SU4 exterior square
{parent=Two-index antisymmetric representation}
{title2=$(\mathcal JT)^{ab}=\tfrac12\epsilon^{abcd}\overline{T^{cd}},\quad\mathcal J^2=1$}

On $\bigwedge^2\mathbb C^4$, the invariant Hermitian form and volume <tensor> define the displayed <antilinear map> with square one, commuting with <SU(4)>. Its fixed space satisfies $T^{12}=\overline{T^{34}}$, $T^{13}=-\overline{T^{24}}$, and $T^{14}=\overline{T^{23}}$. Three complex choices give six real coordinates, and this fixed space has the original exterior square as its complexification. It is therefore a <real representation> of dimension six, rather than merely the twelve-dimensional realification of an arbitrary six-dimensional complex representation.

= Weyl reflection
{title2=$s_\alpha$}
{c}
{parent=Root system}

The reflection associated with a root is $s_\alpha(x)=x-\langle x,\alpha^\vee\rangle\alpha$.

= Reflection group of a root system
{title2=$W(\Phi)$}
{parent=Weyl reflection}

The reflection group of a root system $\Phi$ is the subgroup of the orthogonal group generated by the reflections $s_\alpha$ for $\alpha\in\Phi$. It permutes the roots and acts freely and transitively on the fundamental systems.

= Weyl group
{title2=$W$}
{c}
{parent=Reflection group of a root system}
{wiki}

The Weyl group is generated by the <Weyl reflection>[reflections] in the roots.

= Reflection representation of a Weyl group
{parent=Weyl group}
{title2=$W\longrightarrow O(\operatorname{span}_{\mathbb R}R)$}

The reflection representation is the action of the <Weyl group> on the real span of its <root system>, generated by its <Weyl reflections>. For an <irreducible root system>, it is an <irreducible representation>: if $U$ is an <invariant subspace>, each <root> either lies in $U^\perp$ or, by subtracting a reflected vector of $U$, lies in $U$. These alternatives partition the roots into <orthogonal> sets. Irreducibility and spanning force $U=0$ or the whole space.

= Longest Weyl-group element
{parent=Weyl group}
{title2=$w_0$}

The longest element of a finite <Weyl group> is the element sending all positive <roots of a root system> to negative roots. It is the <longest element of a finite Coxeter group>. For the <G2 root system> it is $-I$: its twelve roots form two hexagons, and the dihedral <reflection group of a root system> contains the rotation through $\pi$. It determines the <highest weight of a dual representation>.

= Coxeter length
{title2=$\ell(w)$}
{c}
{parent=Weyl group}
{wiki=Length_function}

The Coxeter length $\ell(w)$ is the smallest number of simple reflections whose product is $w$. Its parity gives the sign of a Weyl-group element.

= Weyl stabilizer of a dominant point
{c}
{parent=Coxeter length}

If $\lambda$ and $w\lambda$ both lie in the closed dominant chamber, then $w$ is a product of simple reflections whose walls contain $\lambda$. In particular, $w\lambda=\lambda$.

= Inversion set of a Weyl-group element
{title2=$N(w)$}
{c}
{parent=Coxeter length}
{wiki=Inversion_set}

For a chosen <positive system of a root system> $\Pi$, the inversion set is
$$
N(w)=\{\beta\in\Pi:w(\beta)\in-\Pi\}.
$$
For a finite <Weyl group>, $|N(w)|=\ell(w)$.

= Positive-root criterion for Coxeter length
{parent=Coxeter length}

In the geometric representation of a Coxeter system,
$$
\ell(ws_i)>\ell(w)\iff w(e_i)\text{ is a positive root},
$$
and
$$
\ell(s_iw)>\ell(w)\iff w^{-1}(e_i)\text{ is a positive root}.
$$
Replacing “positive” by “negative” reverses either length inequality.

= Dynkin diagram
{c}
{parent=Root system}
{wiki}

A Dynkin diagram records the angles and relative lengths of the simple roots. A multiple-edge arrow points toward the shorter root.

= Classification of finite crystallographic Dynkin diagrams
{parent=Dynkin diagram}

The connected <Dynkin diagrams> of finite reduced <crystallographic root systems> are $A_r$, $B_r$, $C_r$, $D_r$, $E_6$, $E_7$, $E_8$, $F_4$ and $G_2$. Bonds encode products of off-diagonal <Cartan integers>; multiple-bond arrows point toward shorter roots. The simply laced branching diagrams have arm lengths $(1,1,r-3)$ for $D_r$ and $(1,2,2)$, $(1,2,3)$, $(1,2,4)$ for the three exceptional $E$ types. The $B$ and $C$ chains have an end double bond, $F_4$ a middle double bond, and $G_2$ a triple bond. This edge weighting is distinct from the vertex labels used to classify nilpotent orbits.

= Acyclicity of a finite Dynkin diagram
{parent=Dynkin diagram}

The underlying graph of a finite <Dynkin diagram> is a forest. Normalize every <simple root> to length one. Each bonded pair has inner product at most $-1/2$, and every other distinct pair has nonpositive inner product. A cycle with $m$ vertices would make the squared norm of their sum at most $m-m=0$, contradicting their <linear independence> in an <inner product space>. Multiple bonds denote root-length information, not graph-theoretic parallel-edge cycles.

= G2 Dynkin diagram
{c}
{parent=Dynkin diagram}
{title2=$G_2$}

The finite <Dynkin diagram> $G_2$ has two vertices joined by a triple bond, with the arrow toward the <short root>. The squared root-length ratio is three. It represents the <G2 root system>.

= F4 Dynkin diagram
{c}
{parent=Dynkin diagram}
{title2=$F_4$}

The finite <Dynkin diagram> $F_4$ is a four-vertex chain whose central bond is double. The two consecutive long roots lie on one side, the two consecutive short roots on the other, and the arrow points toward the short pair. The remaining two bonds are single.

= En Dynkin diagram
{c}
{parent=Dynkin diagram}
{title2=$E_6,\ E_7,\ E_8$}

The finite <Dynkin diagrams> $E_6,E_7,E_8$ are simply laced trees with one trivalent vertex. Their three arm lengths, counting edges, are respectively $(1,2,2)$, $(1,2,3)$ and $(1,2,4)$.

= Dn Dynkin diagram
{c}
{parent=Dynkin diagram}
{title2=$D_n$}

For $n\ge4$, the finite <Dynkin diagram> $D_n$ is a simply laced tree with one trivalent vertex and three arms containing $1,1,n-3$ edges. All its <roots> have the same length.

= Cn Dynkin diagram
{c}
{parent=Dynkin diagram}
{title2=$C_n$}

For $n\ge2$, the finite <Dynkin diagram> $C_n$ is a chain with a terminal double bond. Its arrow points from the terminal long root toward the adjacent short root. It represents the <Cn root system>; $C_2$ is isomorphic to $B_2$ after relabelling.

= An Dynkin diagram
{c}
{parent=Dynkin diagram}
{title2=$A_n$}

For $n\ge1$, the finite <Dynkin diagram> $A_n$ is a chain of $n$ vertices joined by single bonds. It represents the <root system> of $\mathfrak{sl}_{n+1}$.

= Simply laced root system
{parent=Dynkin diagram}
{wiki}

An irreducible root system is simply laced when all roots have the same length, equivalently when its <Dynkin diagram> has only single edges. This is also equivalent to
$$
\langle\alpha,\beta^\vee\rangle\in\{0,\pm1\}
$$
whenever $\alpha\ne\pm\beta$.

= ADE classification
{c}
{parent=Simply laced root system}

The connected positive-definite simply-laced Dynkin diagrams are $A_n$ for $n\geq1$, $D_n$ for $n\geq4$, and the exceptional diagrams $E_6,E_7,E_8$.

= D3 root system
{title2=$D_3$}
{c}
{parent=Dynkin diagram}

The $D_3$ roots are $\pm\varepsilon_i\pm\varepsilon_j$ for $1\leq i<j\leq3$. The simple roots
$$
\alpha_1=\varepsilon_1-\varepsilon_2,\quad
\alpha_2=\varepsilon_2-\varepsilon_3,\quad
\alpha_3=\varepsilon_2+\varepsilon_3
$$
form the three-node chain $\alpha_2-\alpha_1-\alpha_3$, so $D_3\cong A_3$.

= Isomorphism between so6 and sl4
{title2=$\mathfrak{so}_6(\mathbb C)\cong\mathfrak{sl}_4(\mathbb C)$}
{c}
{parent=D3 root system}

The root-system isomorphism $D_3\cong A_3$ and the classification of complex simple Lie algebras give $\mathfrak{so}_6(\mathbb C)\cong\mathfrak{sl}_4(\mathbb C)$.

= Extended Dynkin diagram
{c}
{parent=Dynkin diagram}

An extended, or affine, Dynkin diagram adjoins the root $\alpha_0=-\theta$, where $\theta$ is the <highest root>.

= Long-root subsystem
{parent=Root system}

The roots of maximal length in a <root system> form a root subsystem. A chamber for the original system lies in a unique chamber for this long-root subsystem, because its interior avoids every long-root reflecting hyperplane.

= G2 root system
{title2=$G_2$}
{c}
{parent=Root system}
{wiki=G2_(mathematics)}

The $G_2$ root system has six short and six long roots. For a short simple root $\alpha_1$ and a long simple root $\alpha_2$, its positive roots are
$$
\alpha_1,\ \alpha_2,\ \alpha_1+\alpha_2,\ 2\alpha_1+\alpha_2,\ 3\alpha_1+\alpha_2,\ 3\alpha_1+2\alpha_2.
$$

= Triple bond isolates a G2 component
{parent=G2 root system}

If two <simple roots> have angle $5\pi/6$, normalize them to unit vectors $u,v$ with $(u,v)=-\sqrt3/2$. For any other unit <simple root> $w$, set $r=(u,w)\le0$, $s=(v,w)\le0$. Its three-root <Gram matrix> has determinant $1/4-r^2-s^2-\sqrt3rs$. Any nonzero $r$ or $s$ has absolute value at least $1/2$, contradicting positive definiteness. Thus all other <simple roots> are orthogonal to $u,v$. An irreducible <root system> containing this triple bond must be the rank-two <G2 root system>.

= G2 adjoint branching to a short-root sl2 subalgebra
{c}
{parent=G2 root system}

For a <short root> $\alpha$ in the <G2 root system>, restriction of the <adjoint representation> to the <sl2 subalgebra associated with a root> gives
$$
\mathfrak g\cong V(3)^{\oplus2}\oplus V(2)\oplus V(0)^{\oplus3}.
$$
Here $V(n)$ is the irreducible <sl2 Lie algebra> representation of <highest weight> $n$ and dimension $n+1$. Every nonzero <root vector> $x\in\mathfrak g_\alpha$ has $\dim Z_{\mathfrak g}(x)=6$: the <raising operator> has a one-dimensional <kernel> in each of these six irreducible summands.

= Long-root A2 subsystem of G2
{title2=$A_2\subset G_2$}
{c}
{parent=G2 root system}

For short and long simple roots $\alpha,\beta$ of $G_2$, the long roots form an $A_2$ root system with simple roots $\delta_1=\beta$ and $\delta_2=3\alpha+\beta$. If $\omega_1,\omega_2$ are the corresponding $A_2$ <fundamental weights>, then $\alpha=\omega_2-\omega_1$ and $\alpha^\vee=\delta_2^\vee-\delta_1^\vee$.

= Restriction of the seven-dimensional G2 representation to long-root A2
{title2=$\mathbf7\downarrow_{A_2}=\mathbf3\oplus\overline{\mathbf3}\oplus\mathbf1$}
{c}
{parent=Long-root A2 subsystem of G2}

The seven-dimensional fundamental $G_2$ representation has weights zero and the six short roots. Under its long-root $A_2$ subalgebra, these are the weights of the defining three-dimensional representation, its dual, and the trivial representation:
$$
V_7\downarrow_{A_2}\cong V(\omega_1)\oplus V(\omega_2)\oplus V(0).
$$

= Root-system finiteness lemma
{parent=Root system}

For roots $\alpha\ne\pm\beta$,
$$
\langle\alpha,\beta^\vee\rangle
\langle\beta,\alpha^\vee\rangle
=4\cos^2\theta\in\{0,1,2,3\},
$$
where $\theta$ is their angle. Integrality gives an integer, and nonparallelism makes it strictly smaller than four.

= Classification of rank-two root systems
{parent=Root-system finiteness lemma}

Every reduced crystallographic rank-two root system is isomorphic to $A_1\times A_1$, $A_2$, $B_2$, or $G_2$. Their simple-root angles are respectively $\pi/2$, $2\pi/3$, $3\pi/4$, and $5\pi/6$.

= Weyl group of a rank-two root system
{parent=Classification of rank-two root systems}

The two simple reflections generate a <dihedral group>. For $A_1\times A_1$, $A_2$, $B_2$, and $G_2$, the rotation has order $2,3,4,6$, so the Weyl groups have orders $4,6,8,12$ respectively.

= Fundamental system of a root system
{title2=$\Delta$}
{parent=Root system}

= Root basis
{synonym}

= Base of a root system
{synonym}

A fundamental system $\Delta$ is a basis made of roots such that every root is a linear combination of $\Delta$ whose nonzero coefficients all have the same sign. Its members are the <simple root>[simple roots].

= Positive system of a root system
{title2=$\Pi$}
{parent=Fundamental system of a root system}

The positive system associated with a <fundamental system of a root system> $\Delta$ consists of the roots whose coordinates in the basis $\Delta$ are nonnegative.

= Fundamental chamber of a root system
{parent=Fundamental system of a root system}

= Weyl chamber
{c}
{synonym}

The fundamental chamber is the connected component
$$
C=\{v:\langle v,\alpha\rangle>0\text{ for every }\alpha\in\Delta\}
$$
of the complement of the reflecting hyperplanes. The Weyl group acts freely and transitively on its chambers.

= Closed dominant Weyl chamber
{c}
{parent=Fundamental chamber of a root system}

For a root basis $\Delta$, the closed dominant chamber is
$$
\overline{C(\Delta)}=\{\lambda:\langle\lambda,\alpha^\vee\rangle\geq0
\text{ for every }\alpha\in\Delta\}.
$$
Every Weyl-group orbit meets it in exactly one point.

= Highest-weight representation
{parent=Semisimple Lie algebra}
{wiki=Highest-weight_representation}

A highest-weight representation is generated by a <weight vector> killed by every positive-root subspace.

= A2 representation of highest weight (2,0)
{parent=Highest-weight representation}
{c}
{title2=$R(2,0)=\operatorname{Sym}^2\mathbb C^3$}

The <A2 root system> representation $R(2,0)=\operatorname{Sym}^2\mathbb C^3$ has dimension six and weights $(2,0),(0,1),(1,-1),(-2,2),(-1,0),(0,-2)$ in <Dynkin label> coordinates, each with multiplicity one.

= Tensor square of the A2 representation of highest weight (2,0)
{parent=A2 representation of highest weight (2,0)}
{title2=$R(2,0)^{\otimes2}=R(4,0)\oplus R(2,1)\oplus R(0,2)$}

The <tensor product of Lie algebra representations> decomposes as $R(2,0)\otimes R(2,0)=R(4,0)\oplus R(2,1)\oplus R(0,2)$, of dimensions $15,15,6$. The exterior square is $R(2,1)$ and the symmetric square is $R(4,0)\oplus R(0,2)$. Highest-weight vectors and the dimension formula determine the decomposition.

= Weight multiplicities in the A2 tensor square of highest weight (2,0)
{parent=Tensor square of the A2 representation of highest weight (2,0)}

The tensor square has fifteen distinct weights: three outer corners of multiplicity one, six near-corner edge weights of multiplicity two, three edge midpoints of multiplicity three, and three interior weights of multiplicity four. In the irreducible components only $R(2,1)$ is weight-degenerate: $(1,0),(-1,1),(0,-1)$ each have multiplicity two. All weights of $R(4,0)$ and $R(0,2)$ have multiplicity one.

= Dominant root-lattice highest weights have zero weight
{parent=Highest-weight representation}

If a <dominant integral weight> $\lambda$ lies in the <root lattice>, its finite-dimensional irreducible <highest-weight representation> contains zero as a weight. First $\lambda$ is a nonnegative integral combination of <simple roots>: a negative part would have negative <inner product> with $\lambda$, contradicting dominance. For any nonzero weight $\mu=\sum m_i\alpha_i$ with $m_i\geq0$, the identity $(\mu,\mu)=\sum m_i(\mu,\alpha_i)>0$ supplies an index with $m_i>0$ and $\langle\mu,\alpha_i^\vee\rangle>0$. By <injectivity of sl2 lowering above weight zero>, applying the corresponding <lowering operator> produces a nonzero vector of weight $\mu-\alpha_i$. Repetition reaches zero.

= Minuscule representation
{parent=Highest-weight representation}
{wiki}

A finite-dimensional irreducible representation of a complex <semisimple Lie algebra> is minuscule if its weights form one <Weyl group> orbit. Every <weight multiplicity> is then one, because multiplicities are invariant under the <Weyl group> and the <highest weight> has multiplicity one.

= Formal character of a minuscule representation
{parent=Minuscule representation}

The <formal character of a weight module> for a <minuscule representation> of <highest weight> $\lambda$ is $\operatorname{ch}V(\lambda)=\sum_{\mu\in W\lambda}e^\mu$. The sum is over distinct points of the <Weyl group> orbit, so $\dim V(\lambda)=|W\lambda|$.

= Minuscule weight
{parent=Minuscule representation}
{title2=$\lambda$}

A <dominant integral weight> $\lambda$ is minuscule when its irreducible <highest-weight representation> is a <minuscule representation>. Equivalently, $\langle\lambda,\alpha^\vee\rangle\in\{0,1\}$ for every <positive root> $\alpha$. This convention includes the zero weight and its <trivial Lie algebra representation>.

= Minuscule weights in the root lattice are zero
{parent=Minuscule weight}

By <dominant root-lattice highest weights have zero weight>, a <minuscule weight> in the <root lattice> has zero in its <Weyl group> orbit. Since every element of the <Weyl group> is invertible, the highest weight must itself be zero. Thus equality of the <weight lattice> and <root lattice> rules out every nontrivial <minuscule representation> without requiring a classification of <root systems>.

= Highest weight of a representation
{title2=$\Lambda$}
{parent=Highest-weight representation}

= Highest weight
{synonym}

The highest weight of a highest-weight representation is the weight of a generating vector annihilated by every positive-root operator. Finite-dimensional irreducible representations of a complex semisimple Lie algebra are classified by dominant integral highest weights.

= Highest-weight coordinates
{parent=Highest weight of a representation}
{title2=$(\lambda(H_1),\ldots,\lambda(H_r))$}

The coordinates of a <highest weight> record its eigenvalues on a chosen basis of a <Cartan subalgebra>. They depend on that basis. <Dynkin labels> instead use the simple <coroots>; orthonormal Cartesian coordinates and Dynkin labels must be converted before applying a dimension or <Casimir eigenvalue> formula.

= SU(3) highest-weight coordinates
{c}
{parent=Highest-weight coordinates}
{title2=$(p,q)=(a/2,(a+2b)/(2\sqrt3))$}

With $H_1=\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,0)/2$ and $H_2=\operatorname{diag}(1,1,-2)/(2\sqrt3)$, the simple roots are $(1,0)$ and $(-1/2,\sqrt3/2)$. Their coroots give <Dynkin labels> $a=2p$ and $b=\sqrt3q-p$ for Cartesian <highest-weight coordinates> $(p,q)$. Finite-dimensional irreducible representations require $a,b\in\mathbb Z_{\geq0}$. The vertical coordinate is $\sqrt3/2$ times <flavor hypercharge>.

= Ladder operator
{parent=Highest-weight representation}
{wiki}

A ladder operator maps weight vectors to weight vectors whose weights differ by one root. Positive-root vectors raise weights and negative-root vectors lower them.

= Raising operator
{parent=Ladder operator}

A raising operator is a positive-root vector. Acting on a weight vector of weight $\mu$ either gives zero or a weight vector of weight $\mu+\alpha$.

= Lowering operator
{parent=Ladder operator}

A lowering operator is a negative-root vector. Acting on a weight vector of weight $\mu$ either gives zero or a weight vector of weight $\mu-\alpha$.

= Cartan-Weyl basis
{c}
{parent=Highest-weight representation}
{wiki=Cartan–Weyl_basis}

A Cartan-Weyl basis consists of a basis of a <Cartan subalgebra> together with root vectors $E_\alpha$. It makes weight raising and lowering explicit through $[H,E_\alpha]=\alpha(H)E_\alpha$.

= Disentangling identity
{parent=Cartan-Weyl basis}

A disentangling identity factors the exponential of a sum of noncommuting Lie-algebra generators into an ordered product of exponentials. For $\mathfrak{sl}_2$, a common order is $e^{aE_-}e^{bH}e^{cE_+}$.

= Complete reducibility of semisimple Lie algebra representations
{c}
{parent=Highest-weight representation}
{wiki=Weyl's_theorem_on_complete_reducibility}

Every finite-dimensional representation of a complex semisimple Lie algebra is a direct sum of irreducible representations.

= Generation by fundamental representations
{parent=Highest-weight representation}

If $\lambda=\sum_im_i\omega_i$ is dominant integral, the tensor product $\bigotimes_iV(\omega_i)^{\otimes m_i}$ contains $V(\lambda)$ as the irreducible summand generated by the tensor product of highest-weight vectors.

= Weight multiplicity decreases away from a dominant weight
{parent=Highest-weight representation}

If $\mu$ and $\lambda$ are weights of a finite-dimensional representation, $\mu$ is dominant, and $\mu\preceq\lambda$, then
$$
\operatorname{mult}(\mu)\geq\operatorname{mult}(\lambda).
$$
Successively subtract a simple root $\alpha_i$ for which $\langle\lambda,\alpha_i^\vee\rangle>0$ and use injectivity of the corresponding $\mathfrak{sl}_2$ lowering operator.

= Weight vector
{parent=Highest-weight representation}
{wiki=Weight_(representation_theory)}

A vector $v$ has weight $\mu\in\mathfrak t^*$ when $h v=\mu(h)v$ for every $h$ in the Cartan subalgebra.

= Highest-weight vector
{parent=Weight vector}

= Primitive element of a Lie algebra representation
{synonym}

A highest-weight vector is a nonzero <weight vector> annihilated by every positive-root <raising operator>. The module it generates is a <highest-weight representation>.

= Weight of a representation
{parent=Weight vector}

= Weights of a representation
{synonym}

= Weight
{synonym}

= Representation weight
{synonym}

A weight of a representation is a functional $\mu$ whose <weight space> is nonzero.

= Weight space
{title2=$V_\mu$}
{parent=Weight vector}
{wiki=Weight_(representation_theory)#Weight_spaces}

The weight space $V_\mu$ consists of all <weight vector>[weight vectors] of weight $\mu$, together with zero.

= Generalized weight space of a Lie algebra representation
{parent=Weight space}
{title2=$V^\lambda=\{v:(\rho(h)-\lambda(h)I)^{\dim V}v=0\ \forall h\}$}

= Generalized weight space
{synonym}

For a finite-dimensional complex <Lie algebra representation> $\rho$ of a <nilpotent Lie algebra> $H$, a generalized weight space is the simultaneous generalized <eigenspace> displayed above, where $\lambda:H\to\mathbb C$ is a <linear functional>. Every occurring $\lambda$ vanishes on the <derived algebra> of $H$. Ordinary <weight vectors> satisfy the same equations without the powers.

= Generalized-weight decomposition for a nilpotent Lie algebra
{parent=Generalized weight space of a Lie algebra representation}
{title2=$V=\bigoplus_\lambda V^\lambda$}

Every finite-dimensional complex <Lie algebra representation> of a <nilpotent Lie algebra> decomposes into invariant <generalized weight spaces>. The nilpotence assumption is essential: a general solvable action can have extensions between distinct weights.

Here is a proof. For $h,k\in H$, nilpotence gives $(\operatorname{ad}h)^N k=0$, hence $(\operatorname{ad}\rho(h))^N\rho(k)=0$. By the <nilpotent commutator preserves generalized eigenspaces> lemma, every <generalized eigenspace> of $\rho(h)$ is invariant under every $\rho(k)$. Split successively by the acting operators of a <basis> of $H$. Each resulting block is invariant under $H$, and each basis operator has only one <eigenvalue> on it. The <Lie theorem> triangularizes the action on each block; its diagonal entries therefore give one and the same <linear functional> $\lambda$ on every basis vector of that block. Every $\rho(h)-\lambda(h)I$ is strictly upper triangular there, proving that the block is $V^\lambda$. Distinct blocks have distinct weights, so this is a <direct sum>. On the zero generalized <weight space>, every acting operator is a <nilpotent endomorphism>, so the <Engel theorem> supplies a nonzero common annihilated vector whenever that space is nonzero.

= Zero generalized weight space of a Cartan subalgebra
{parent=Generalized-weight decomposition for a nilpotent Lie algebra}
{title2=$L^0(H)=H$}

Let $H$ be a <Cartan subalgebra> of a finite-dimensional complex <Lie algebra> $L$. In the adjoint <generalized-weight decomposition for a nilpotent Lie algebra>, its zero summand satisfies $L^0=H$. Nilpotence gives $H\subseteq L^0$. If $L^0/H$ were nonzero, all adjoint operators of $H$ on this quotient would be nilpotent, so the <Engel theorem> would supply a nonzero coset $x+H$ with $[H,x]\subseteq H$. That contradicts self-normalization. The derivation rule ensures that $L^0$ is a subalgebra, so the quotient action is well-defined.

= Weight string
{parent=Weight space}
{title2=$q-p=\langle\mu,\alpha^\vee\rangle$}

For a finite-dimensional representation of a complex <semisimple Lie algebra>, the weights on a line $\mu+\mathbb Z\alpha$ form a contiguous string from $\mu+p\alpha$ to $\mu-q\alpha$, with $q-p=\langle\mu,\alpha^\vee\rangle$. Restriction to the associated sl2 subalgebra gives nested strings of the same parity, so the string endpoints determine the weight set but not its multiplicities.

= Weight-string enumeration algorithm
{parent=Weight string}

Start with the <highest weight> and process weights by increasing height below it. For each simple root, compute the upper endpoint from the already known higher weights, use the <weight string> endpoint relation, and append all downward steps. Repeat until the finite set stabilizes. This recovers the weights of a finite-dimensional <irreducible Lie algebra representation> without determining <weight multiplicities>.

= Weight-space decomposition
{parent=Weight space}
{title2=$V=\bigoplus_\mu V_\mu$}

A weight-space decomposition expresses a <Lie algebra representation> as a <direct sum> of simultaneous <eigenspaces> for a <Cartan subalgebra>. Each summand is the <weight space> for its <weight>. In complex finite-dimensional representations of a <semisimple Lie algebra>, the simple <coroots> act as commuting diagonalizable operators, giving this decomposition.

= Weight basis
{parent=Weight-space decomposition}

A weight basis of a <Lie algebra representation> is a <basis> consisting of <weight vectors> for a chosen <Cartan subalgebra>. When the Cartan action is diagonalizable, choose a basis separately in every <weight space> and combine them. This makes <weight multiplicities> directly visible and gives diagonal matrices for Cartan elements.

= Weight diagram
{parent=Weight-space decomposition}

A diagram placing the <weights> of a representation in the real span of its <weight lattice>, recording each <weight multiplicity>. Coincident weights must retain their multiplicity: for an <adjoint representation> the zero-weight multiplicity is the dimension of its <Cartan subalgebra>.

= Shell multiplicities in an SU(3) weight diagram
{parent=Weight diagram}
{title2=$m_{p,q}(\mu)=\sum_{k=0}^{\min(p,q)}\mathbf1_{P_{p-k,q-k}}(\mu)$}

Let $P_{a,b}$ be the convex hull of the <Weyl group> orbit of the <highest weight> $a\omega_1+b\omega_2$ for the <SU(3) Lie algebra>. Restrict the displayed formula to the <weight lattice> coset $p\omega_1+q\omega_2+$ root lattice. It describes successive inward shells: the outer boundary has <weight multiplicity> one, each inward shell increases it by one, and the final triangular region has the constant value $\min(p,q)+1$. When $p=q$ that region is a single point. A triangular diagram with $p=0$ or $q=0$ has multiplicity one throughout. This is a geometric form of the <dominant weight multiplicity formula for sl3>, transported by Weyl reflections.

// Target: lie-theory.bigb

= Weight diagram of the defining SU(3) representation
{parent=Weight diagram}
{title2=$\mu_u=(1/2,1/3),\ \mu_d=(-1/2,1/3),\ \mu_s=(0,-2/3)$}

Using $I_3=\operatorname{diag}(1/2,-1/2,0)$ and flavour $Y=\operatorname{diag}(1/3,1/3,-2/3)$, the three coordinate vectors of the <SU(3) group> defining <group representation> have the displayed <weights of a representation>. The complex-conjugate <group representation> has their negatives. These coordinates use <flavor hypercharge>; another Cartan normalization rescales the vertical axis.

= Tensor-product weight diagram
{parent=Weight diagram}
{title2=$m_{V\otimes W}(\mu)=\sum_\nu m_V(\mu-\nu)m_W(\nu)$}

For a <tensor product of Lie algebra representations> with <weight-space decompositions>, the <weights> add and their <weight multiplicities> convolve. A diagram must sum contributions landing at the same weight, rather than treating coincident points as distinct positions.

= Extremal weight space
{parent=Weight space}

For a finite-dimensional irreducible <highest-weight representation> with highest weight $\lambda$, every weight in the <Weyl group> orbit $W\lambda$ is extremal and has <weight multiplicity> one.

= Weight multiplicity
{parent=Weight space}
{wiki=Weight_(representation_theory)#Weight_multiplicity}

The multiplicity of a weight $\mu$ is the dimension of its <weight space>.

= Kostant multiplicity formula
{c}
{parent=Weight multiplicity}
{title2=$m_\lambda(\mu)=\sum_{w\in W}\varepsilon(w)p(w(\lambda+\rho)-(\mu+\rho))$}

Multiply the <Weyl character formula> by the <generating series of the Kostant partition function>. In each numerator summand, the coefficient of $e^\mu$ occurs when $\nu=w(\lambda+\rho)-(\mu+\rho)$. Summing those coefficients with the <Weyl group> signs proves the displayed <weight multiplicity> formula. Although $\rho$ need not itself be an integral <group> <weight>, $w\rho-\rho$ is in the <root lattice>, so each partition-function argument is an integral <weight>.

= Cyclic trace identity between adjacent weight spaces
{parent=Weight multiplicity}
{title2=$\operatorname{tr}_{V_\mu}(F_\alpha E_\alpha)=\operatorname{tr}_{V_{\mu+\alpha}}(E_\alpha F_\alpha)$}

For linear maps $E:U\to W$ and $F:W\to U$ between finite-dimensional <vector spaces>, $\operatorname{tr}_U(FE)=\operatorname{tr}_W(EF)$ even when their dimensions differ; expanding rectangular matrices proves it. For <root vectors> normalized by $[E_\alpha,F_\alpha]=t_\alpha$, with $B(t_\alpha,h)=\alpha(h)$, this gives $T_\alpha(\mu)=T_\alpha(\mu+\alpha)+(\mu+\alpha,\alpha)\dim V_{\mu+\alpha}$. Iteration along a finite <weight string> is the trace step in <Freudenthal multiplicity formula>.

= Freudenthal multiplicity formula
{parent=Weight multiplicity}
{c}
{title2=$\bigl(\|\lambda+\rho\|^2-\|\mu+\rho\|^2\bigr)m_\lambda(\mu)=2\sum_{\alpha>0}\sum_{j\geq1}(\mu+j\alpha,\alpha)m_\lambda(\mu+j\alpha)$}

For a finite-dimensional <irreducible Lie algebra representation> of a complex <semisimple Lie algebra> with <highest weight> $\lambda$, this recursion computes its <weight multiplicities> from $m_\lambda(\lambda)=1$. Here $\rho$ is the <half-sum of positive roots> and the <inner product> is induced by the <Killing form>. Write the <Casimir operator> on the weight-$\mu$ space as $(\mu,\mu+2\rho)I+2\sum_{\alpha>0}F_\alpha E_\alpha$. The <cyclic trace identity between adjacent weight spaces> gives $\operatorname{tr}(F_\alpha E_\alpha|_{V_\mu})=\sum_{j\geq1}(\mu+j\alpha,\alpha)m_\lambda(\mu+j\alpha)$. Taking the trace and using the <Casimir eigenvalue> proves the formula. Only finitely many terms are nonzero.

= Singular vector
{parent=Weight vector}

A singular vector is a nonzero <weight vector> annihilated by the positive nilpotent subalgebra $\mathfrak n^+$.

= Verma module
{title2=$M_\lambda$}
{c}
{parent=Highest-weight representation}
{wiki}

For a Borel subalgebra $\mathfrak b=\mathfrak t\oplus\mathfrak n^+$ and a weight $\lambda$, the Verma module is
$$
M_\lambda=U(\mathfrak g)\otimes_{U(\mathfrak b)}\mathbb C_\lambda,
$$
where $\mathfrak n^+$ kills $\mathbb C_\lambda$ and $h\in\mathfrak t$ acts by $\lambda(h)$.

= Universal property of a Verma module
{parent=Verma module}

If a $\mathfrak g$-module contains a vector $v$ of weight $\lambda$ annihilated by $\mathfrak n^+$, there is a unique homomorphism $M_\lambda\to V$ sending the canonical highest-weight vector to $v$. Every highest-weight module of weight $\lambda$ is therefore a quotient of $M_\lambda$.

= Irreducible quotient of a Verma module
{parent=Verma module}

The sum of all proper submodules of $M_\lambda$ is its unique maximal proper submodule. The quotient is the unique irreducible highest-weight module $V(\lambda)$.

= Maximal proper submodule of a dominant integral Verma module
{parent=Irreducible quotient of a Verma module}
{c}

If $\lambda$ is a <dominant integral weight>, the maximal proper submodule of $M(\lambda)$ is generated by the simple-root <singular vectors>
$$
f_i^{\langle\lambda,\alpha_i^\vee\rangle+1}v_\lambda.
$$
The generator associated with $\alpha_i$ has weight $s_i(\lambda+\rho)-\rho$.

= Formal character of a weight module
{title2=$\operatorname{ch}V$}
{parent=Highest-weight representation}

= Formal character
{synonym}

For a module with finite-dimensional <weight space>[weight spaces], its formal character is $\operatorname{ch}V=\sum_\mu(\dim V_\mu)e^\mu$ in the completed group algebra of the <weight lattice>.

= Highest-weight character subtraction
{parent=Formal character of a weight module}

For a finite-dimensional representation of a complex <semisimple Lie algebra>, select a maximal remaining <dominant weight>, subtract that multiplicity of the corresponding irreducible <formal character>, and repeat. The <Weyl complete reducibility theorem> guarantees nonnegative residual multiplicities and termination. Checking the full character or the exhausted total dimension confirms that no summands remain.

= sl3 interlacing character formula
{parent=Formal character of a weight module}
{title2=$\chi_{a,b}=\sum_{b\leq p\leq a+b}\sum_{0\leq q\leq b}\sum_{q\leq r\leq p}x_1^r x_2^{p+q-r}x_3^{a+2b-p-q}$}

For the <special linear Lie algebra> $\mathfrak{sl}_3$, the irreducible <highest-weight representation> with <Dynkin labels> $(a,b)$ has the displayed <formal character> on diagonal matrices with $x_1x_2x_3=1$. The interlacing rows $(a+b,b,0)$, $(p,q)$ and $(r)$ enumerate a <Gelfand–Tsetlin basis>; each triple contributes the exponent differences $(r-(p+q-r),(p+q-r)-(a+2b-p-q))$ as its weight. This gives exact integer <weight multiplicities>, including repeated weights, without floating-point character evaluation.

= Dominant weight multiplicity formula for sl3
{parent=sl3 interlacing character formula}
{title2=$m_{a,b}(p,q)=\max\{0,1+\min(a,b,r,s)\}$}

For the irreducible <highest-weight representation> of the <special linear Lie algebra> $\mathfrak{sl}_3$ with <Dynkin labels> $(a,b)$, let $(p,q)$ be a <dominant integral weight>. Put $r=(2a+b-2p-q)/3$ and $s=(a+2b-p-2q)/3$. If these are not <integers>, the <weight multiplicity> is zero; otherwise it is $\max\{0,1+\min(a,b,r,s)\}$.

To derive the formula, use the <sl3 interlacing character formula> with top row $(a+b,b,0)$. The target diagonal exponents are $(s+p+q,s+q,s)$, and an interlacing middle row $(P,Q)$ has $P+Q=2s+p+2q$, with bottom entry $s+p+q$. Thus
$$
\max\{b,2s+p+2q-b,s+p+q\}\leq P\leq\min\{a+b,2s+p+2q\}.
$$
Every integral $P$ in this interval gives exactly one <Gelfand–Tsetlin basis> vector. The upper bound minus the lower bound is the minimum of six differences: $a,s,r,r+p+q,b,s+q$. Since $p,q\geq0$, this minimum reduces to $\min(a,b,r,s)$. Counting the interval proves the formula, including absent <weights of a representation>. This converts a two-dimensional <weight diagram> calculation into four elementary boundary distances.

= Weyl character formula
{c}
{parent=Highest-weight representation}
{wiki}

For a dominant integral weight $\lambda$, the irreducible character is
$$
\operatorname{ch}L_\lambda=
\frac{\sum_{w\in W}(-1)^{\ell(w)}e^{w(\lambda+\rho)}}
{\sum_{w\in W}(-1)^{\ell(w)}e^{w\rho}}.
$$

= Alternant character formula for the general linear group
{parent=Weyl character formula}
{title2=$\phi_\lambda(x)=A_{\lambda+\delta}(x)/A_\delta(x)$}

For a dominant integer tuple $\lambda$ and $\delta=(m-1,\ldots,0)$, the rational Schur <character> is $\det(x_i^{\lambda_j+m-j})/\det(x_i^{m-j})$. For partitions it is a <symmetric polynomial>; for general tuples it is Laurent on the diagonal torus. The formula follows by comparing the <trace of a permuted tensor power> with the <Frobenius alternant character formula> and using independence of symmetric-group <characters>. Apparent poles at coincident nonzero <eigenvalues> are removable.

= Character of a Weyl-vector multiple
{parent=Weyl character formula}
{title2=$\operatorname{ch}L(k\rho)$}

For a complex <semisimple Lie algebra> and an <integer> $k\ge0$, the <Weyl character formula> and <Weyl denominator formula> give
$$
\operatorname{ch}L(k\rho)=e^{k\rho}\prod_{\alpha\in R^+}(1+e^{-\alpha}+\cdots+e^{-k\alpha}),\qquad
\dim L(k\rho)=(k+1)^{|R^+|}.
$$
Apply the dilation $e^\mu\mapsto e^{(k+1)\mu}$ to the denominator identity to obtain the numerator. Cancellation leaves a finite <geometric series> for each positive <root of a root system>. Evaluating the resulting polynomial at all formal exponentials equal to one gives the <dimension>, without taking an undefined quotient at the identity.

= Weyl denominator formula
{c}
{parent=Weyl character formula}
{wiki}

For a positive root system $\Phi^+$,
$$
\sum_{w\in W}(-1)^{\ell(w)}e^{w\rho}
=e^\rho\prod_{\alpha\in\Phi^+}(1-e^{-\alpha}).
$$

= Weyl dimension formula
{c}
{parent=Weyl character formula}
{wiki}

The dimension obtained by evaluating the <Weyl character formula> at the identity is
$$
\dim L_\lambda=\prod_{\alpha\in R^+}
\frac{\langle\lambda+\rho,\alpha^\vee\rangle}
{\langle\rho,\alpha^\vee\rangle}.
$$

= G2 dimension polynomial
{c}
{parent=Weyl dimension formula}
{title2=$\dim L(a\omega_1+b\omega_2)$}

For the <G2 root system> with $\alpha_1$ short and $\alpha_2$ long, the <fundamental weights> are $\omega_1=2\alpha_1+\alpha_2$ and $\omega_2=3\alpha_1+2\alpha_2$. The positive <coroots>, relative to the <simple coroots>, have coordinates $(1,0),(0,1),(1,3),(2,3),(1,1),(1,2)$. The <Weyl dimension formula> therefore gives, for nonnegative <integers> $a,b$,
$$
\dim L(a\omega_1+b\omega_2)=\frac{(a+1)(b+1)(a+b+2)(a+2b+3)(a+3b+4)(2a+3b+5)}{120}.
$$
The denominator is the product of the pairings of the <Weyl vector> with those coroots. The <dimensions> at $\omega_1,\omega_2,2\omega_1$ are seven, fourteen and twenty-seven.

= Weyl dimension formula for C2
{c}
{parent=Weyl dimension formula}

For the <C2 root system> with its first <simple root> short, the irreducible <highest-weight representation> of <highest weight> $a\omega_1+b\omega_2$, where $a,b\in\mathbb Z_{\geq0}$, has dimension
$$
\frac{(a+1)(b+1)(a+b+2)(a+2b+3)}6.
$$

= q-character of a highest-weight representation
{title2=$\operatorname{ch}_qL_\lambda$}
{parent=Weyl character formula}

For the principal specialization, let $h_{\mathrm{pr}}=2\rho^\vee$, so every simple root takes value two on $h_{\mathrm{pr}}$. The q-character is
$$
\operatorname{ch}_qL_\lambda=
\sum_\mu(\dim L_\lambda[\mu])q^{\mu(h_{\mathrm{pr}})}.
$$
It is obtained from the <Weyl character formula> by substituting $e^\mu=q^{\mu(h_{\mathrm{pr}})}$.

= Principal q-character of the G2 adjoint representation
{parent=q-character of a highest-weight representation}
{c}
{title2=$\operatorname{ch}_q\mathfrak g=\operatorname{ch}_qL_{10}+\operatorname{ch}_qL_2$}

Use $\operatorname{ch}_q V=\operatorname{tr}_V q^{2\rho^\vee}$. The positive <roots of a root system> of the <G2 root system> have simple-root heights $1,1,2,3,4,5$. Each root space has <dimension> one, and the zero <weight space> has dimension two. Consequently
$$
\operatorname{ch}_q\mathfrak g=q^{10}+q^8+q^6+q^4+2q^2+2+2q^{-2}+q^{-4}+q^{-6}+q^{-8}+q^{-10}.
$$
By the <classification of finite-dimensional sl2 representations>, restriction to the <principal sl2 subalgebra> is $L_{10}\oplus L_2$, of dimensions eleven and three. If the grading uses $\rho^\vee$ instead, divide every exponent by two.

= Crystal basis
{parent=Highest-weight representation}
{wiki}

A crystal basis is the combinatorial limit of a basis of a quantum-group representation. Its colored directed graph records the actions of the <Kashiwara operator>[Kashiwara lowering operators].

= Crystal of the defining symplectic representation
{parent=Crystal basis}
{title2=$B(\omega_1)$}

For the <symplectic Lie algebra> $\mathfrak{sp}_{2n}$, the defining <crystal basis> is the chain $1,2,\ldots,n,\bar n,\ldots,\bar1$, with <weights> $\varepsilon_1,\ldots,\varepsilon_n,-\varepsilon_n,\ldots,-\varepsilon_1$. Its successive lowering-edge colors are $1,2,\ldots,n-1,n,n-1,\ldots,1$. Every step subtracts its color's <simple root>. Applying the <crystal tensor-product rule> to two copies gives a complete grid description of the tensor-square crystal. For $n\ge2$, its only highest vertices are $1\otimes1$, $1\otimes2$, and $1\otimes\bar1$, of <highest weights> $2\omega_1$, $\omega_2$, and zero. For $n=1$, the middle vertex is absent and only the other two highest vertices remain.

= Crystal of the seven-dimensional G2 representation
{parent=Crystal basis}

The seven-dimensional irreducible representation of the <G2 root system> has <highest weight> $\omega_1=2\alpha_1+\alpha_2$. Its <crystal basis> is the chain of weights $2\alpha_1+\alpha_2,\alpha_1+\alpha_2,\alpha_1,0,-\alpha_1,-\alpha_1-\alpha_2,-2\alpha_1-\alpha_2$, with successive edge colors $1,2,1,1,2,1$. Each step subtracts its color's <simple root>. The nonzero weights are the six short roots, each of <multiplicity> one; the zero weight also has multiplicity one.

= Exterior and symmetric squares of the seven-dimensional G2 representation
{parent=Crystal of the seven-dimensional G2 representation}

For $V=L(\omega_1)$ with the <G2 root system> numbered short-first,
$$
\Lambda^2V=L(\omega_2)\oplus L(\omega_1),\qquad S^2V=L(2\omega_1)\oplus L(0).
$$
In the chain basis of the <crystal of the seven-dimensional G2 representation>, normalize $v_1=f_1v_0$, $v_2=f_2v_1$, $v_3=f_1v_2$. Then $v_0\wedge v_1$ and $v_0\wedge v_3-2v_1\wedge v_2$ are nonzero <highest-weight vectors> of weights $\omega_2$ and $\omega_1$; the identities $e_1v_1=v_0$, $e_1v_3=2v_2$, $e_2v_2=v_1$ verify both raising conditions. The <Weyl complete reducibility theorem> and <G2 dimension polynomial> exhaust the twenty-one dimensions of the <exterior square>. In the <symmetric square>, $v_0^2$ generates the twenty-seven-dimensional summand. Self-duality supplies a <Lie-invariant bilinear form>, which is symmetric because a <nondegenerate> <alternating bilinear form> cannot have odd <dimension>; its inverse gives the remaining invariant line.

= Crystal of the defining odd-orthogonal representation
{parent=Crystal basis}

The defining representation of $\mathfrak{so}_{2n+1}$ has <weights> $\varepsilon_1,\ldots,\varepsilon_n,0,-\varepsilon_n,\ldots,-\varepsilon_1$. Its <crystal basis> is a chain on these vertices, with lowering-edge colors $1,\ldots,n-1,n,n,n-1,\ldots,1$. The short-root string has length two through zero. With the <crystal tensor-product rule>, for $n\ge2$ the tensor square has highest vertices $1\otimes1$, $1\otimes2$ and $1\otimes\bar1$, corresponding to the traceless <symmetric square>, the <exterior square> and the <trivial Lie algebra representation>. Their <dimensions> are $(2n+1)(n+1)-1$, $n(2n+1)$ and one.

= Root-string property of a crystal
{parent=Crystal basis}

For each simple root $\alpha_i$, the $i$-colored edges of a crystal form strings. Along such a string, $\widetilde e_i$ raises the weight by $\alpha_i$ and $\widetilde f_i$ lowers it by $\alpha_i$.

= Kashiwara operator
{title2=$\widetilde e_i,\widetilde f_i$}
{c}
{parent=Crystal basis}
{wiki=Crystal_base#Definition}

On a crystal, $\widetilde f_i$ follows an edge of color $i$ and $\widetilde e_i$ follows that edge backwards.

= Tensor product of crystals
{parent=Crystal basis}
{wiki=Crystal_base#Tensor_products_of_crystals}

The tensor product crystal uses the $i$-string data $\varepsilon_i$ and $\varphi_i$ to decide on which tensor factor a <Kashiwara operator> acts. Its connected components are highest-weight crystals and encode the decomposition of the tensor product representation.

= Crystal tensor-product rule
{parent=Tensor product of crystals}

Write $\varepsilon_i(b)$ and $\varphi_i(b)$ for the numbers of raising and lowering steps of color $i$ available from a vertex of a <crystal basis>. One convention for the <tensor product of crystals> is
$$
\widetilde f_i(a\otimes b)=\begin{cases}\widetilde f_i(a)\otimes b,&\varphi_i(a)>\varepsilon_i(b),\\a\otimes\widetilde f_i(b),&\varphi_i(a)\le\varepsilon_i(b).\end{cases}
$$
The raising operator uses the first factor for $\varphi_i(a)\ge\varepsilon_i(b)$ and the second otherwise. The different strictness makes raising and lowering inverse along every edge. An edge is absent if the chosen <Kashiwara operator> vanishes. Stating the convention is essential when identifying the <highest-weight vectors> as ordered tensor vertices.

= Casimir element
{c}
{parent=Semisimple Lie algebra}
{wiki}

= Casimir operator
{c}
{synonym}

The quadratic Casimir element is a central element of $U(\mathfrak g)$. On a highest-weight module of highest weight $\lambda$, one standard normalization makes it act by $|\lambda+\rho|^2-|\rho|^2$.

= Quadratic Casimir operator
{parent=Casimir element}
{title2=$\sum_a t_at_a=C_2(R)I$}

For a <Lie algebra representation> with generators referred to an orthonormal invariant form, the sum $\sum_at_at_a$ commutes with every generator. Indeed, the <commutator> is proportional to a contraction of antisymmetric <structure constants of a Lie algebra> with the symmetric product $t_at_b+t_bt_a$. The <Schur lemma> makes it scalar in an irreducible complex representation; that scalar is its quadratic <Casimir eigenvalue>. With <trace> normalization $\operatorname{tr}(t_at_b)=C(R)\delta_{ab}$, taking the <trace> gives $C(R)\dim\mathfrak g=C_2(R)\dim R$.

= SU(3) quadratic Casimir eigenvalue
{c}
{parent=Quadratic Casimir operator}
{title2=$C_2(a,b)=(a^2+ab+b^2+3a+3b)/3$}

For generators normalized by $\operatorname{tr}_{\mathbf3}(t_at_b)=\delta_{ab}/2$, the <quadratic Casimir operator> on the irreducible <SU(3)> representation of <Dynkin labels> $(a,b)$ has the displayed scalar value. In <SU(3) highest-weight coordinates> $(p,q)$ it is $p^2+q^2+p+\sqrt3q$.

To derive this, write the operator as $H_1^2+H_2^2+\sum_{i=1}^3(E_+^iE_-^i+E_-^iE_+^i)$. On a <highest-weight vector>, the raising operators vanish, and the three root commutators sum to $H_1+\sqrt3H_2$. Centrality and the <Schur lemma> extend this value to the representation. The singlet, fundamental, adjoint and decuplet values are $0,4/3,3,6$, respectively. Tensor labels and multiplet dimensions are summarized in https://pdg.lbl.gov/2024/reviews/rpp2024-rev-young-diagrams.pdf[the Particle Data Group's SU(n) multiplet guide].

= Tensor-product Casimir trace identity
{parent=Quadratic Casimir operator}
{title2=$\sum_i d(R_i)C_2(R_i)=d(R_1)d(R_2)[C_2(R_1)+C_2(R_2)]$}

For traceless generators, the <tensor product of Lie algebra representations> has generators $t_a\otimes I+I\otimes s_a$. The <trace> of their summed squares is the sum of the two factor Casimir <traces>; the cross term vanishes because $\operatorname{tr}t_a=\operatorname{tr}s_a=0$. Decomposition into <irreducible representations>, with multiplicities counted, gives the displayed identity. Without tracelessness an extra term $2\sum_a\operatorname{tr}t_a\operatorname{tr}s_a$ is present. A one-dimensional abelian representation of charge one tensored with itself has Casimir four, not the sum two; semisimple <Lie algebras> avoid this problem because every generator is a sum of <commutators>.

= Casimir eigenvalue
{c}
{parent=Casimir element}
{title2=$c_\lambda=(\lambda,\lambda+2\rho)$}

The <Casimir operator> formed from the <Killing form> acts on an <irreducible Lie algebra representation> with <highest weight> $\lambda$ by the scalar $(\lambda,\lambda+2\rho)$, where $\rho$ is the <half-sum of positive roots>. It is zero on the <trivial Lie algebra representation> and nonzero on every nontrivial finite-dimensional irreducible complex representation of a <semisimple Lie algebra>.

= Casimir eigenvalue for sl2
{title2=$\frac12m(m+2)$}
{c}
{parent=Casimir element}

For $\mathfrak{sl}_2$ with $[h,e]=2e$, $[h,f]=-2f$, and $[e,f]=h$, the Casimir
$$
\Omega=ef+fe+\frac12h^2
$$
acts on the irreducible highest-weight module $V(m)$ by the scalar $\frac12m(m+2)$.

= Principal sl2 subalgebra
{title2=$\mathfrak{sl}_2^{\mathrm{pr}}$}
{c}
{parent=Semisimple Lie algebra}
{wiki=Principal_three-dimensional_subgroup}

A principal sl2 subalgebra of a semisimple <Lie algebra> has semisimple generator $h_{\mathrm{pr}}=2\rho^\vee$. Restriction to it packages a representation's weights into ordinary $\mathfrak{sl}_2$ weight strings.

= Construction of a principal sl2 triple
{parent=Principal sl2 subalgebra}
{title2=$e=\sum_i e_i,\quad h=2\rho^\vee,\quad f=\sum_i c_i f_i$}

Choose <simple-root> vectors with $[e_i,f_i]=h_i=\alpha_i^\vee$, and let $\rho^\vee$ be the half-sum of positive <coroots>. It satisfies $\alpha_i(\rho^\vee)=1$. Expand $h=2\rho^\vee=\sum_i c_i h_i$. For $i\ne j$, $[e_i,f_j]=0$, since the difference of distinct simple roots is not a <root of a root system>. Thus $[e,f]=h$, while $[h,e]=2e$ and $[h,f]=-2f$. These identities construct an embedded <sl2 Lie algebra> in any nonzero complex <semisimple Lie algebra>. Its raising vector is the <principal nilpotent element> with a nonzero component in each simple-root space.

= Principal nilpotent element
{parent=Principal sl2 subalgebra}
{wiki}

A principal nilpotent element of a semisimple <Lie algebra> is a nilpotent element whose <centralizer> has dimension equal to the rank. For nonzero simple-root vectors $e_i$, the sum $e=\sum_i e_i$ is principal nilpotent and forms the raising element of a <principal sl2 subalgebra>.

= Coxeter group
{c}
{parent=Lie theory}
{wiki}

A Coxeter group is a <group> with a presentation
$$
W=\langle x_i\ (i\in I): (x_ix_j)^{m_{ij}}=1\rangle,
$$
where $m_{ii}=1$ and $m_{ij}=m_{ji}\in\{2,3,\ldots,\infty\}$ for $i\ne j$. The pair consisting of $W$ and its distinguished generating reflections is a <Coxeter system>.

= Coxeter system
{c}
{parent=Coxeter group}

A Coxeter system $(W,I,M)$ records a <Coxeter group> $W$, its set $I$ of simple generators, and its <Coxeter matrix> $M=(m_{ij})$.

= Coxeter matrix
{c}
{parent=Coxeter system}
{wiki}

The Coxeter matrix of a <Coxeter system> is the symmetric matrix whose entry $m_{ij}$ is the order of $x_ix_j$.

= Coxeter graph
{c}
{parent=Coxeter matrix}

= Coxeter diagram
{c}
{synonym}

The Coxeter graph has vertex set $I$, joins $i$ and $j$ when $m_{ij}\geq3$, and labels the edge by $m_{ij}$ when $m_{ij}>3$. A <Coxeter system> is <irreducible Coxeter system>[irreducible] exactly when this graph is connected.

= Admissible crystallographic Coxeter graph
{parent=Coxeter graph}
{title2=$G_{ii}=1,\quad G_{ij}=-\sqrt{n_{ij}}/2$}

= Admissible Coxeter graph
{synonym}

In the crystallographic convention, a <Coxeter graph> is admissible when it is realized by linearly independent unit <vectors> $v_i$ in a <Euclidean space>, with nonpositive mutual <inner products> and $4(v_i,v_j)^2=n_{ij}\in\{0,1,2,3\}$ for distinct vertices. The number $n_{ij}$ records a single, double or triple connection; equivalently, the displayed <Gram matrix> is <positive definite>. In edge-label notation the nonzero connections have labels $3,4,6$. Forgetting multiplicity, the underlying <graph> is a <forest>: for any cycle of $k\geq3$ distinct vertices, the sum of the corresponding unit <vectors> would have squared <norm> at most $k-k=0$, contradicting their linear independence. Multiple lines on one connection are labels and are not length-two cycles. This is the convention of \a[https://www.mathematik.uni-muenchen.de/~wehler/LieAlgebrasScript.pdf][Wehler's Lie algebra notes, section 6.3].

= Irreducible Coxeter system
{c}
{parent=Coxeter graph}

An irreducible Coxeter system is one whose <Coxeter graph> is connected. The connected components of a Coxeter graph give the direct-product decomposition of its Coxeter group.

= Type E(p,q,k) Coxeter graph
{title2=$E(p,q,k)$}
{c}
{parent=Coxeter graph}

The type $E(p,q,k)$ graph is a simply-laced tree with one trivalent vertex and arms containing $p$, $q$, and $k$ vertices beyond that vertex.

= Reduced expression in a Coxeter group
{parent=Coxeter system}
{wiki=Reduced_word}

= Reduced word in a Coxeter group
{synonym}
{c}

A reduced expression for $w\in W$ is a product of the fewest possible simple generators representing $w$. The number of factors is its <Coxeter length> $\ell(w)$.

= Deletion condition for involutory generators
{parent=Reduced expression in a Coxeter group}

For a group generated by involutions, the deletion condition says that every nonreduced word can be shortened without changing its value by deleting two of its letters.

= Folding-grid proof of the deletion condition
{parent=Deletion condition for involutory generators}

Arrange the lengths of all consecutive subwords of a word in a triangular grid. Under the <folding condition>, the boundary between length ascents and descents must contain a folding square. Its equal opposite vertices identify two letters that can be deleted. Induction proves the <deletion condition for involutory generators>.

= Exchange condition for a Coxeter group
{parent=Reduced expression in a Coxeter group}

If $w=s_1\cdots s_n$ is a reduced expression and $s$ is simple with $\ell(ws)<\ell(w)$, then
$$
ws=s_1\cdots\widehat{s_j}\cdots s_n
$$
for some index $j$. The analogous statement holds for multiplication on the left.

= Matsumoto theorem
{c}
{parent=Reduced expression in a Coxeter group}

Matsumoto's theorem says that any two reduced expressions for one element of a Coxeter group are connected by a finite sequence of braid moves.

= Tits word reduction theorem
{c}
{parent=Matsumoto theorem}

Every word in simple Coxeter generators can be reduced by braid moves and cancellations $ss\mapsto1$. Consequently, a word is reduced exactly when no sequence of braid moves can make a cancellation possible.

= Geometric representation of a Coxeter group
{parent=Coxeter system}
{c}

For a finite-rank <Coxeter system>, let $V$ have basis $(e_i)_{i\in I}$ and symmetric <bilinear form>
$$
\langle e_i,e_j\rangle=-2\cos(\pi/m_{ij}).
$$
Its geometric representation sends the generator $x_i$ to the reflection
$$
\sigma(x_i)v=v-\langle v,e_i\rangle e_i.
$$

= Coxeter Gram matrix
{c}
{parent=Geometric representation of a Coxeter group}

The Coxeter Gram matrix is the <Gram matrix> $G=(-2\cos(\pi/m_{ij}))_{i,j\in I}$ of the distinguished basis in the <geometric representation of a Coxeter group>.

= Dual geometric representation of a Coxeter group
{title2=$\sigma^*$}
{c}
{parent=Geometric representation of a Coxeter group}

The dual geometric representation acts on $V^*$ by
$$
(\sigma^*(w)f)(v)=f(\sigma(w^{-1})v).
$$
The open fundamental chamber consists of the functionals positive on every simple basis vector.

= Tits cone
{title2=$U$}
{c}
{parent=Dual geometric representation of a Coxeter group}
{wiki}

For the closed fundamental chamber $D\subseteq V^*$, the Tits cone is
$$
U=\bigcup_{w\in W}wD.
$$
It equals all of $V^*$ exactly when the finite-rank Coxeter group $W$ is finite.

= Coxeter complex
{c}
{parent=Dual geometric representation of a Coxeter group}
{wiki}

The Coxeter complex has faces represented by cosets $wW_J$ of <standard parabolic subgroup>[standard parabolic subgroups], ordered by reverse inclusion. Geometrically, its maximal simplices are the chambers of the Coxeter arrangement intersected with a sphere.

= Reflection of a Coxeter group
{parent=Coxeter system}

A reflection of a Coxeter system $(W,S)$ is a conjugate $wsw^{-1}$ of a simple generator $s\in S$.

= Wall of a Coxeter group
{title2=$H_r$}
{parent=Reflection of a Coxeter group}

The wall associated with a reflection $r$ consists of the Cayley-graph edges fixed setwise and reversed by left multiplication by $r$. Its complement has two connected components.

= Half-space of a Coxeter group
{parent=Wall of a Coxeter group}

The two connected components of the complement of a <wall of a Coxeter group> are its half-spaces. A wall separates two group elements when they lie in opposite half-spaces.

= Standard parabolic subgroup
{title2=$W_J$}
{parent=Coxeter system}

For $J\subseteq I$, the standard parabolic subgroup is $W_J=\langle s_j:j\in J\rangle$. Its cosets index faces of a fixed type in the <Coxeter complex>.

= Spherical subset of a Coxeter system
{parent=Standard parabolic subgroup}

A subset $T\subseteq S$ is spherical when its <standard parabolic subgroup> $W_T$ is finite.

= Davis complex
{title2=$\Sigma(W,S)$}
{c}
{parent=Standard parabolic subgroup}
{wiki}

The Davis complex is the geometric realization of the poset of cosets $wW_T$ for spherical subsets $T\subseteq S$. Equivalently, it is the <basic construction of a Coxeter group> obtained by gluing copies of the Davis chamber along mirrors. It is a contractible proper $W$-CW complex.

= Basic construction of a Coxeter group
{title2=$U(W,K)$}
{parent=Davis complex}

For a mirrored chamber $K$, the basic construction is
$$
U(W,K)=(W\times K)/\sim,
$$
where $(w,x)\sim(w',x)$ when $w^{-1}w'$ belongs to the subgroup generated by the mirrors containing $x$.

= Folding condition
{parent=Coxeter system}

For involutory generators, the folding condition requires that simple multiplication never preserve length and that simultaneous left and right ascents either combine to a two-step ascent or fold: if both $s_iw$ and $ws_j$ have length $\ell(w)+1$, then either $\ell(s_iws_j)=\ell(w)+2$ or $w=s_iws_j$.

= Braid relation in a Coxeter group
{parent=Folding condition}

For generators $s_i,s_j$ with $m_{ij}<\infty$, the braid relation equates the two alternating words of length $m_{ij}$ beginning with $s_i$ and $s_j$, respectively. When $m_{ij}=2$, it says that the generators commute.

= Coxeter element
{c}
{parent=Coxeter system}
{wiki}

A Coxeter element is a product of all simple generators in some order. For a finite irreducible <Coxeter group>, all Coxeter elements are conjugate.

= Coxeter number
{title2=$h$}
{c}
{parent=Coxeter element}
{wiki}

The Coxeter number is the <order of a group element>[order] of a <Coxeter element> in a finite irreducible <Coxeter group>.

= Finite Coxeter group
{c}
{parent=Coxeter group}

A finite Coxeter group is a <Coxeter group> with finitely many elements. Its <Coxeter Gram matrix> is positive definite.

= Longest element of a finite Coxeter group
{title2=$w_0$}
{parent=Finite Coxeter group}
{wiki=Longest_element_of_a_Coxeter_group}

The longest element $w_0$ is the unique element of maximal <Coxeter length> in a finite <Coxeter group>. It is an involution and satisfies $\ell(w_0w)=\ell(w_0)-\ell(w)$.

= E7 Coxeter group
{title2=$E_7$}
{c}
{parent=Finite Coxeter group}

The $E_7$ Coxeter graph is the simply-laced seven-vertex tree whose three arms have lengths $1$, $2$, and $3$. Its Coxeter Gram matrix is positive definite and has determinant $2$.

= Signed symmetric group
{title2=$SS_n$}
{parent=Finite Coxeter group}
{wiki=Hyperoctahedral_group}

The signed symmetric group consists of permutations of $\{\pm1,\ldots,\pm n\}$ commuting with sign change. It is the Coxeter group of type $B_n$.

= Even signed symmetric group
{title2=$ESS_n$}
{parent=Signed symmetric group}

The even signed symmetric group is the index-two subgroup of signed permutations with an even number of sign changes. It is the Coxeter group of type $D_n$.

= Affine Coxeter group
{c}
{parent=Coxeter group}
{wiki=Affine_Coxeter_group}

An irreducible affine Coxeter group has a positive-semidefinite <Coxeter Gram matrix> with a one-dimensional <radical of a bilinear form>[radical].

= Hyperbolic Coxeter group
{c}
{parent=Coxeter group}
{wiki}

A hyperbolic Coxeter group has a nondegenerate <Coxeter Gram matrix> of Lorentzian signature in its standard geometric realization.

= BN-pair
{c}
{parent=Coxeter group}
{wiki}

A BN-pair in a group $G$ consists of subgroups $B,N$ such that $G=\langle B,N\rangle$, $H=B\cap N$ is normal in $N$, and $W=N/H$ has distinguished involutory generators satisfying the Bruhat multiplication axioms. The quotient $W$ is its <Weyl group>.

= Bruhat decomposition of a BN-pair
{c}
{parent=BN-pair}
{wiki=Bruhat_decomposition}

The Bruhat decomposition is the disjoint union
$$
G=\bigsqcup_{w\in W}B\dot wB
$$
associated with a <BN-pair>.

= Hecke algebra
{c}
{parent=Coxeter group}
{wiki}

A Hecke algebra is a deformation of the <group algebra> of a <Coxeter group>, obtained by deforming the quadratic relations for its simple generators while retaining the braid relations.

= Generic Hecke algebra of a Coxeter system
{title2=$H_{\mathrm{gen}}(W,\{a_i\})$}
{c}
{parent=Hecke algebra}
{wiki=Iwahori–Hecke_algebra}

Over the polynomial parameter ring, the generic Hecke algebra has basis $(T_w)_{w\in W}$ and multiplication
$$
T_iT_w=
\begin{cases}
T_{x_iw},&\ell(x_iw)>\ell(w),\\
a_iT_{x_iw}+(a_i-1)T_w,&\ell(x_iw)<\ell(w).
\end{cases}
$$
The parameters must agree for conjugate simple generators; equivalently, $(T_i-a_i)(T_i+1)=0$ together with the braid relations presents the algebra.

= 0-Hecke algebra
{c}
{parent=Generic Hecke algebra of a Coxeter system}

The 0-Hecke algebra is the <specialization of an algebra>[specialization] $a_i=0$ of a <generic Hecke algebra of a Coxeter system>. Its generators obey $T_i^2=-T_i$.

= Iwahori-Hecke algebra of a BN-pair
{title2=$H_k(G,B)$}
{c}
{parent=Hecke algebra}
{wiki=Iwahori–Hecke_algebra}

For a finite group $G$ with a <BN-pair>, the Iwahori-Hecke algebra over $k$ is the endomorphism algebra of the permutation module $k[G/B]$, up to the conventional opposite algebra. Its standard basis is indexed by the <Bruhat decomposition of a BN-pair>[Bruhat double cosets].

= Hecke parameter of a BN-pair
{title2=$q_i$}
{c}
{parent=Iwahori-Hecke algebra of a BN-pair}

For a representative $\dot x_i\in N$, the Hecke parameter is
$$
q_i=[B:B\cap\dot x_iB\dot x_i^{-1}].
$$
The <Iwahori-Hecke algebra of a BN-pair> is obtained from the generic algebra by $a_i\mapsto q_i\cdot1_k$.
